elements (page 239) Demand to address the shortcomings in Tista \nHydel Project in Sikkim (page Demand to set up a Central University in Nagpur \n(page 24) Concern over taking off turbans at t he airports in America for \nidentification \nvisitors (page 241) Concern over restriction on mining of sand along the \nriverbeds of coas \nKarnataka (page 241) Demand for regularization of services of Anganwadi \nworkers (page 241 — 42) Demand for taking concrete steps for speedy disposal of \ncases in courts (page 242) Demand for shifting barbed-wire fencing on Indo-\nPak border to extreme \nborder (pages 242—43) Demand for development of cities in Bihar \nunder Jawaharlal Nehru Urban \nRenewal Mission (page243)  \nDemand for approval of Amendment to Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, \n1954 (pages 243—44) Request for improvement of Railway Station at \nGursahaiganj, District \nKannauj (UP) (page 244) Need to take effective steps for construction of \nairport in Pakyang, near \nGangtok in Sikkim (pages 244 —25) Concern over extinction of local breeds \nof bovine animals in the country (page 245) Urgent need to take measures to \nreduce atrocities on children (page 245 — 46) Demand to declare Goa as an \nindependent Telecom and Postal Circle (page 246) Demand for re-opening the \nKurkunta Unit of Cement Corporation of India in \nGulbarga, Karnataka (pages 246 —47) Demand for improvement in services \nof local trains in Mumbai (pages 247— 49) Statutory Resolution—  \nAmendment of First Schedule to Customs Tariff Act, 1975— Adopted'(page 249) \nGovernment Bill—  \nThe Apprentices (Amendment) Bill, 2006— Passed (pages 249— 277) Statements \nby Ministers — Productivity-Linked-Reward  for  Port  and  Dock  \nWorkers/Employee, \n(pages 293—94) Strike by resident Doctors' in AIIMS , New Delhi (pages \n294— 301) Short Duration Discussion — Heavy floods in various parts of the \ncountry and relief measures undertaken by \nthe Government (pages 301—315) \n\nRAJYA SABHA \nThursday, the 30th August, 2007/8 Bhadrapada, 1929 (Saka) \nThe House met at eleven of the clock, \n[MR. CHAIRMAN in the Chair.] \nORAL ANSWERS TO QUESTIONS \nEco Mark certification \n \n   *241. SHRI HARISH RAWAT:†† \n  SHRI SANTOSH BAGRODIA: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of products that have been identified for Eco Mark since 1991; \n(b) the basis on which such Eco Mark certification is given; and \n(c) the steps taken to popularize the Eco Mark among producers and  \ncustomers? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a) to (c) A Statement is laid on the \nTable of the House. \nStatement \n(a) The Government of India has notified sixteen product categories under the Eco \nMark Scheme. \n(b) Environmental criteria for each product category are notified by the Central \nGovernment and later on transla ted into Indian Standards by the Bureau of Indian \nStandards (BIS). Products are examined in terms of the following main environmental \nimpacts:- \n(i)     that they have substantially less potential for pollution than other \ncomparable products in production, usage and disposal. \n(ii)    that they are recycled, recyclable, made from recycled products or bio-\ndegradable, where comparable products are not; \n†† The question was actually asked on the floor of the House by Shri Harish Rawat.  \n\n 2       Oral Answers [RAJYA SABHA] to Questions \n(iii) that they make significant contribution to saving non -recyclable resources \nincluding non-renewable energy sources and natural resources compared \nwith comparable products; \n(iv) that the product must contribute to a reduction of the adverse primary \ncriteria which has the highest environmental impact associated with the \nuse of the product, and which will be specifically set for each of the \nproduct categories. \nIn determining the product specific requirements for setting eco -criteria, the \nfollowing are taken into account: \n(i) production process including source of raw materials; \n(ii) use of natural resources; \n(iii) likely impact on the environment; \n(iv) energy conservation in the production of the product; \n(v) effect and extent of waste arising from the production process; \n(vi) disposal of the product and its container; \n(vii) utilization of \"Waste\" and recycled materials; \n(viii) suitability for recycling or packaging; and \n(ix) biodegradability \n(c) The steps taken to popularize the Eco Mark among manufacturers and consumers \ninclude the following:- \n(i) Conduct of consumer awareness programme and industry awareness \nprogramme including workshops and studies on Eco Mark. \n(ii) Financial support to consumer organizations for comparative testin g of \nproducts and dissemination of its findings. \n \nश्री हर�श रावत : सर, म� माननीय मंत्री जी से जानना चाहता हूं �क अभी तक ऎसे \n�कतने प्रोडक्टस ह�, जो Eco Mark स्क�म के अंतगर्त सिम्म�लत �कए गए ह� तथा इस Eco \nMark स्क�म का present status क्या  है? क्या इसको determine करने के �लए \nexperts क� कोई body है?  \n \nश्री नमो नारायण मीणा : सर, यह स्क�म भारत सरकार ने 1991 म� प्रारं भ क� थी \nऔर इसम� अब तक 16 categories को नो�टफाई �कया गया है । माननीय सदस्य ने यह \nपूछा है �क �कतने आइटम्स को इसम� कवर �कया गया है? इस स्क�म म� अब तक 20 Eco \nMark Licenses पंद्रह क ं प�नय� को �दए जा चु\nके है, जो तीन product categories म� \nआते ह� – paper, wood substitute and finished leather. सर , इस \n�रज़ॉल्यूशन के तहत steering committees और दूसर� technical committees ह� । \nये steering committees policy formulation का  काम करती है और \ntechnical committees इन प्रोडक्टस को approve करती ह� ।  \n\nOral Answers [30 AUG, 2007] to Questions       3 \n \nश्री हर�श रावत : सर, माननीय मंत्री जी के उत्तर से यह स्पष्ट है �क 1991 से \nजो स्क�म ऑपरे शन म� है, अभी तक के व ल 20 प्रोडक्टस ऎसे ह�, िजनको eco friendly \nmark �दया गया है । इसका अथर् यह है �क यह स्क�म लोक�प्रय नह� हो पाई है । तो इसको \nलोक�प्रय बनाने के �लए, इसको popularise करने के �लए इनका मंत्रालय क्य