r. ...(Interruptions)... \n   \n‘�तब्बत के ऊपर� इलाक� म� ब फर् का �पघलना’ \n \n *244.   श्री जनेश्वर �मश्र :†† \n   श्री बृज भूषण �तवार� : \n \n क्या प्रधान मंत्री यह बताने क� कृ पा कर� गे �क : \n \n ( क) क्या यह सच है �क �तब्बत के ऊपर� इ ल ा के म� तेजी से ब फर् के �पघलने, \n�हम न�दय� के �समटने, घास के पीला पड़ने एवं न�दय� के सूखते जाने से पयार्वरण को \nगंभीर खतरा उत्पन्न हो गया है;  \n ( ख) य�द हां, तो क्या सरकार ने दे श पर पड़ने वाले इ स के दुष्प्रभाव� के बारे म� \nकोई अध्ययन कराया है; और  \n ( ग) य�द हां, तो तत्संबंधी ब्यौरा क्या है?  \n \n पयार्वरण और वन मंत्रालय म� राज्य मंत्री ( श्री नमो नारायन मीणा) : ( क) से \n(ग)  एक �ववरण सदन के पटल पर रखा है ।  \n \n ( क) इन्टरगवनर्म�टल पैनल ऑन क्लाइमेट च�ज क� अप्रैल, 2007 म� प्रका�शत \nव � क� ग ग्रुप-II  �रपोटर्  के अनुसार, �हमालय के �हमनद �वश्व के अन्य �कसी भाग क� \nअपे�ा ज्यादा तेजी से �पघल रहे है । चाइनीज ए के ड म ी ऑफ साइंसेज के अग्रणी वै�ा�नक� के \nअनु\nसार, �तब्बत पठार के �हमनद तेजी से �पघल रहे है । �हमनद� के �पघलने से अन्तत: \nऔर अ�धक सूखा पड़ेगा, मरुस्थल�करण का �वस्तार और धूल भरे तू\nफान� म� वृिध्द होगी । \nब फर् के �पघलने से जल आपू�तर् म� भी बाधा पहुचन� का खतरा ह�, क्य��क कई न�दयां \n�हमालय से �नकलती है । \n \n \n ( ख) जी, नह� ।  \n \n ( ग) प्रश्न नह� उठता ।  \nMelting of ice in upper region of Tibet \n \n             *244. SHRI  JANES HWAR MISHRA: †† \n                 SHRI BRIJ BHUSHAN TIWARI:  \nWill the Prime Minister be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that rapid melting of ice in upper region of Tibet, shrinking of \nglaciers, grass turning yellow and drying of rivers have posed a se rious danger to the \nenvironment; \n(b) if so, whether Government have conducted any study with regard to its adverse \neffect on the country; and \n††सभा म� यह प्रश्न श्री जनेश्वर �मश्र द्वारा पूछा गया ।  \n††The question was actually asked on the floor of the House by [Shri Janeshwar Mishra.] \n \n \n \n \n\n \n24     Oral Answers [RAJYA SABHA] to Questions \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a) to (c) A statement is laid on the \nTable of the House.' \n(a) According to the Working Group- II Report of the Intergovernmental Panel on \nClimate Change (IPCC) released in April, 2007, the Himalaya n Glaciers are receding \nfaster than any part of the world. According to leading scientists from the Chinese \nAcademy of Sciences, there is rapid melting of the glaciers of the Tibetan Plateau. The \nmelting of glaciers will ultimate trigger more droughts, exp and desertification and \nincrease sand storms. Melting also threatens disruption of water supply, as many rivers \nemanate from the Himalayas. \n(b) No, Sir. \n(c) Does not arise. \nDR. MURLI MANOHAR JOSHI: Why is the Ganga so polluted? ...(Interruptions)... \nयह  क्या हो रहा है ...(व्यवधान)... \nMR. CHAIRMAN: May I request the hon. Member to sit down and allow the \nsupplementaries to proceed? ...(Interruptions)... Please let the supplementary proceed. \n \nश्री जनेश्वर �मश्र : सभाप�त जी, हमने जो सवाल पूछा है, जोशी जी गंगा के बारे म� \nबहुत �चं�तत ह� �क हमको खतरा है �क थोड़े �दन� के बाद गंगा बहेगी ह� नह� ।  \n \nश्री सभाप�त : आप अपना सप्ल�म�टर� पू\n�छए ।  \n \nश्री जनेश्वर �मश्र : म� उसी पर आ रहा हूं । हमको खतरा है �क थोड़े �दन� के बाद गंगा \nनह� बहेगी, क्य��क �तब्बत से िजस तरह ग्ले�शयसर् �पघल रहे ह�, गरम हो रहे है, गंगा का \nपानी आएगा या नह� ...(व्यवधान)... \n \nश्री सभाप�त : आप अपना सप्ल�म�टर� पू\n�छए ।  \n \nश्री जनेश्वर �मश्र : हां , म� पूछ रहा हूं । मंत्री जी ने यह जो जवाब �दया है, इसम� \nपहले तो उन्ह�ने कहा है �क Chinese Academy of Sciences  के  कु छ वै�ा�नक� ने \nयह कहा है �क �तब्बत के पठार के ग्ले�शयसर् गल रहे ह� और उससे बहने वाले पानी यानी \nगंगा-जमु\nना वगैरह को खतरा होगा, वहां पर रे �गस्तान आएगा, वहां सूखा पड़ेगा, वहां गरमी \nहोगी । हमने सवाल के (ख) भाग म� यह पू\nछा था �क आप भी इसक� कोई जांच करा रहे ह� \nया चीन के वै�ा�नक ह� कर रहे है, तो इन्ह�ने कह �दया �क हम जांच नह� करा रहे है । \nहमने कहा �क अगर कोई जांच कराई है, तो उसक� �रपोटर् क्या है, तो इन्ह�ने कह �दया �क \nquestion does not arise. हम समझते ह� �क एक तरह से दे श तबाह होने जा रहा है \n। िजस गंगा नद� के नाम पर जोशी जी ने इतना * हमको खतरा इस बात का है �क आने \nवाला �हंदुस्तान सू\nखाग्रस्त और तबाह �हंदुस्तान होगा और भारत क� सरकार इतनी लापरवाह� \nसे इस पर जवाब दे, आ�खर क्य� , क्या चीन  \n* Expunges as ordered by the Chair. \n\nOral Answers [30 AUG, 2007] to Questions    25 \n \nके डर से? क्या चीन के डर के मारे आपने जांच नह� कराई? क्या आपको मालूम नह� था \n�क एवरेस्ट क� चोट� तक चीन अपनी एक सड़क बना रहा है ।  \n \nश्री सभाप�त : आप अपना सवाल पू\nछ ल�िजए ।  \n \nश्री जनेश्वर �मश्र : म� सवाल पर ह� आ रहा हूँ । क्या आप यह जांच नह� कर सकते थे \n�क उससे ग्ले�शयसर् पर असर पड़ेगा, वहां क� जलवायु पर असर पड़ेगा? यह जवाहर लाल \nनेहरू के ज़माने का करार है, आ प के अमर�का के साथ एटम वाला करार नह� है \n...(व्यवधान)... \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Let the hon. Prime Minister answer. \nDR. MANMOHAN SINGH: Sir, the hon. Member has raised a very important issue. It is \nbeyond doubt that if the glacier system in Tibet or the Indus water system starts melting \nor shrinking, it will have catastrophic effects on all countries. And it is precisely because of \nthe linkages between the water systems in India and Tibet that I had myself raised this issue \nwith President Hu Jintao and it had been agreed between the two countries that technical \nstudies should take place to understand the dynamics of water flows between the two \ncountries. \n \nश्री जनेश्वर �मश्र : सभाप�त जी, �हमाचल प्रदे श म� इस समय वै�ा�नक� का एक \nसम्मेलन हो रहा है, वहां  से यह �रपोटर् आई है �तब्बत के ग्ले�शयसर् के �पघलाव के बारे म� \n�क इस नद� के अंत तक भारत का तापमान 7 से लेकर 10 �डग्री तक बढ़ जाएगा । उस \nगरमी क� जरा आप कल्पना कर ल�िजए, यानी मौसम �बल्कु ल बदलने जा रहा है और पता \nनह� हम लोग सो रहे ह� या क्या कर रहे ह� या �कस दबाव म� काम कर रहे ह� । यह सच है \n�क आ प के पु\nरख� ने �तब्बत को चीन के हाथ� �लख �दया, हमारे पुरख� ने �वरोध �कया, \nले�कन अब जो हो रहा है, उसम� कै स े adjust क र के चला जाए �क हमार� तबाह� न हो । \nवह भी हमारा �मत्र दे श है, उससे हम� बात करनी चा�हए । क्या आपने कभी प्रयास �कया \nChinese Government से बात करने के �लए �क तु\nम्हार� ...(व्यवधान)... \n \nश्री सभाप�त : आप सवाल पू�छए...(व्यवधान)... \n \nसश्री जनेश्वर �मश्र :उधर नेपाल क� न�दयां बहकर �हंदुस्तान म� आती ह� और आप बांध \nबनाना चाहते ह�, अभी तक नह� बना पाए, क्य��क नेपाल डरता है �क हम डू ब जाएंगे \n...(व्यवधान)... हम जानते ह� �क दो दे श� के बीच म� जो �रश्ते बना सकते ह�, उनम� \n�कतनी �दक्कत� आती है, सदन म� \n* हम लोग एक –दूसरे क� भावना को न दबाएं । तो इस \nबारे म� क्या मी�टंग हुई, उ स के क्या नतीजे �नकल रहे है, यह �हंदुस्तान क� बेचैनी का \nसवाल है, इस�लए हम इ स के बारे म� जानना चाहते ह� ।  \nSHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA: Sir, the hon. Prime Minister has already replied. \nMR. CHAIRMAN:  Question Hour is over. \n* Expunges as ordered by the Chair. \n\n26     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred Questions \nWRITTEN ANSWERS TO THE STARRED QUESTIONS \nUpgradation of services by DOP \n *245. SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \nSHRIMATI SHOBHANA BHARTIA: \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether in view of the burgeoning competition from private courier companies, \nthe Department of Posts has embarked upon a major strategy to rejig its operations \nacross the country and  has worked out a four -pronged strategy to consolidate its \nposition; \n(b) if so, the main strategies formulated; \n(c) to what extent it would be helpful; and \n(d) whether Government are also considering to upgrade traditional services through \ninduction of technology,  introduction of new products and services to meet the \nchallenges of faster, more reliable and more responsive services to customers?  \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI A. RAJA): (a) to (d) After assessing the unfolding market challenges and \nemerging opportunities for postal services, the Department of Posts has adopted the \nfollowing approach and strategic interventions to meet these challenges and to provide \nquality postal services to its customers. \n(i) Technology is being inducted in postal operations on a large scale -8263 post \noffices have been computerized and a National Data Centre is being set up. \nComplaints against service are being handled through a web based system \nin Customer Care Centres. The Department's Eleventh Five  Year Plan \nenvisages extensive technology induction for enhanced service capability. \n(ii) Expansion and upgradation of network are being taken up on a regular basis. \nThe network is being reinforced by administrative restructuring in selected \nareas and streamlining of operational procedures. Franchised outlets are \nalso being opened for enhancing access to postal services. The Department \nhas chartered a freighter air craft for transmission of mail parcels and \nlogistics between Kolkata and North East destinations to ensure faster and \nreliable service in this region thereby giving a boost to our Air Logistics \noperations also. \n(iii) The Department of Posts is providing market oriented services like Speed \nPost, Business Post and Express Parcel Post. In addition, t he Department \nhas introduced new business-oriented products like Bill \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Starred Questions     27 \nMail Service, Direct Post, Logistics Post and various e -enabled services \nlike e -Post, e -Payment and Instant Money Order Servic e. Customized \nsolutions and value additions are being provided in response to specific \nrequirements of customers. \n(iv) The Department has developed a market outlook to effectively respond to the \nbusiness challenges. This encompasses aspects like pricing of  products, \ncustomized solutions and value additions as part of business packages. The \nDepartment has taken initiatives to work out various business alliances and \npartnerships with public and private corporations for leveraging its rural \npostal network. The  rural post office can provide a platform for various \nservices necessary for our rural population. The Rural Postal Life Insurance \nhas registered more than 40% annual growth during last three years. \n(v) The Department has conducted extensive training -programmes on computer \nskills, departmental software, marketing and customer relations \nmanagement for its officers and staff. The human resource development \nwas an important area of activity and during Tenth Five Year Plan 3,93,000 \nemployees were imparted train ing at different levels. In the Eleventh Plan \nthe Department will have a larger outlay for Human Resource \nDevelopment. \nAccident database \n*246. SHRI B.K.HARIPRASAD: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Sundar Committee Report on Road Safety and Traffic \nManagement had commented that data on road accidents injuries and mortality was  \nboth inadequate and scattered; \n(b) whether the United Nation's Economic and Social Commission for Asia and the \nPacific (UNESCAP) has come forward to fund a project to build an accident database;  \nand \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF SHIPPING, ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI \nT.R BAALU): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) and (c) The United Nations Economic and Social Commission for A sia and \nPacific (UNESCAP) have sponsored a project titled \"Implementation of the Asia Pacific \nRoad Accident Database (APRAD) in Bay of Bengal Initiative for Multi Sectoral \nTechnical and Economic Cooperation (BIMSTEC) countries and Nepal and \nDevelopment of a Computerised National Road Accident Database as a Pilot Project in \nIndia\". The implementation of \"Indian Road Accident Data Base (IRAD)\" project has \nbeen taken up as a pilot project in India in the context of the above UNESCAP project. \nThe project aims at evolving a comprehensive Road Accident Data Base covering the \nentire country i.e. 28 States, 7 Union Territories and 23 select cities. \n\n28     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred Questions \nColachal Port \n*247. DR. K. MALAISAMY: Will the Minister of S HIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that a joint venture agreement was entered into for \ndevelopment of Colachal Port with Klana Port of Malaysia; \n(b) if so, the major terms of agreement and the time- frame before which  the project \nis to be completed; \n(c) the present stage and progress of the project; \n(d) whether there are any constraints in the completion of the project; \n(e) if so, the measures envisaged to get over such problems; and \n(f) the initiatives taken to develop the minor ports in Tamil Nadu? \nTHE MINISTER OF SHIPPING ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI \nT.R. BAALU): (a) to (f) Colachel Port is a Non -Major Port under the jurisdiction of the \nState Government of Tamil Nadu. According to the information received from the \nGovernment of Tamil Nadu, no joint venture agreement has been entered into by them \nwith Klang Port of Malaysia for the development of Colachel Port. The Government of \nTamil Nadu has further informed that their earlier Port Policy of 1996 -97 has now been \namended, with e ffect from August, 2007 to attract private investment and develop \ncaptive ports and commercial ports to handle various types of cargo and that the new \npolicy is expected to lead to development of Non-Major ports in Tamil Nadu. \nBoundary talks with China \n*248. SHRIMATI SHOBHANA BHARTIA: Will the Minister of EXTERNAL \nAFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether boundary talks between India and China had made significant progress \nand the border areas remained peaceful and tranquil and whether this issue was \ndiscussed with the Chinese President during his recent visit to India; \n(b) if so, the other issues that were discussed; \n(c) whether any final agreement on the boundary issue was signed; and \n(d) if so, the latest position thereon? \nTHE MINISTER OF EXTERNAL AFFAIRS (SHRI PRANAB MUK HERJEE): (a) \nto (b) India and China are engaged in discussions to arrive at a fair, reasonable and \nmutually acceptable solution to the boundary question. There have been ten meetings of \nthe Special Representatives so far. The first five meetings resulted i n the signing of the \n\"Agreement on the Political Parameters and Guiding Principles for the Settlement of the \nIndia-China Boundary Question\" on April 11, 2005 during the visit of the Chinese \nPremier to India. Presently, the Special Representatives are explo ring the framework for \na final package settlement covering all sectors of the boundary on the basis of this \nAgreement. The next (eleventh) round of talks between the Special Representatives will \nbe held at a mutually convenient time. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Starred Questions     29 \nPending the resolution of the boundary question, both sides have agreed to maintain \npeace and tranquillity in the border areas in accordance with the agreements of 1993, \n1996 and 2005. \nDuring the visit of Chinese Presi dent to India in November 2006, both sides stated \nthat they were committed to resolving outstanding differences through peaceful means \nand in a fair, reasonable, mutually acceptable and proactive manner, while ensuring that \nsuch differences are not allowed to affect the positive development of bilateral relations. \nFurther, both sides agreed that an early settlement of the boundary question will \nadvance the basic interests of the two countries and shall, therefore, be pursued as a \nstrategic objective. \nThe tw o sides issued a Joint Declaration in November 2006 that spelt out a ten -\npronged strategy to upgrade India -China relations to a qualitatively new level. The \nelements of this strategy include: ensuring comprehensive development of bilateral \nrelations; conso lidating commercial and economic exchanges; expanding all -round \nmutually beneficial cooperation; instilling mutual trust and confidence through defence \ncooperation; seeking early settlement of outstanding issues; promoting trans -border \nconnectivity and coo peration; boosting cooperation in Science and Technology; \nrevitalising cultural ties and nurturing people -to-people exchanges; and expanding \ncooperation on regional and international stage. \nNew CZM Notification \n*249. SHRI SYED AZEEZ PASHA: \nSHRI D. RAJA: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Government have made a new Coastal Zone \nManagement (CZM) Notification replacing the Coastal Regulation Zone (CRZ) \nNotification, 1991; \n(b) if so, the features of the new notification; \n(c) whether Government are aware that the new CZM Notification poses serious \nthreats to livelihood of coastal fishing workers and to coastal ecosystems; and  \n(d) if so, the details thereof and Government's reaction thereto? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONM ENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a) No Sir \n(b) to (d) Does not arise. \nMost polluted rivers \n†*250. SHRI RUDRA NARAYAN PANY: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the name of the Ganges has been included in the list of \nten most polluted rivers of the world; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n30     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred Questions \n(b) if so, the measures Government intend to take to prevent such unfortunate, \nsituations; and \n(c) whether it is a fact that just before the Magha fair, the waste polluted and black \nwater is released knowingly in the holy Ganges by the leather factories of Kanpur? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a ) In a report entitled \"World's top 10 \nrivers at risk\", the World Wide Fund for Nature (WWF), an international Non-\nGovernment Organization (NGO), has fisted the river Ganga as one of the world's ten \nrivers facing risk. The report profiles the biodiversity of the river Ganga, and perceives \nover-extraction of water for agriculture and excessive water diversions affecting natural \nflows as major threats to the river. However, the report has not categorized the river \nGanga under the category of polluted rivers. \n(b) Under the National River Conservation Plan, various works are being  \nundertaken for pollution abatement of major rivers in the country, including the  \nGanga, through interception and diversion of sewage, construction of sewage  \ntreatment plants, low cost sanitation, river front development, afforestation, public  \nparticipation, public awareness, etc. With the pollution abatement works completed  \nso far under the Plan, the water quality of the river Ganga has shown discernible  \nimprovement at major locations o ver its pre -Ganga Action Plan quality as per the  \nwater quality monitoring undertaken by independent reputed institutions in the  \ncountry. \n(c) No, Sir. As per the U.P. State Pollution Control Board, the polluted water  \nfrom the tanneries is not released into the river Ganga directly. The effluent  \ngenerated from the tanneries .receives primary treatment in the individual tannery  \nand is then sent to a Common Effluent Treatment Plant (CETP) for secondary  \ntreatment. The final treated effluent is used for irrigation. \nRoad-bridge on brahmaputra \n*251. SHRI KUMAR DEEPAK DAS: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether there is any proposal for construction of road- bridge downstream of \nexisting rail and road-bridge over Brahmaputra at Saraighat near Guwahati; \n(b) whether it is a fact that the Railway authorities have requested to review the \nplanning for construction of roadbridge or rail -cum-road bridge near Guwahati by \ninvolving the Ministry of Railways; and \n(c) if so, the steps taken by his Ministry so far? \nTHE MINISTER OF SHIPPING ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI \nT.R. BAALU): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) No, Sir. \n(c) Does not arise. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Starred Questions     31 \nIdentity verification by mobile operators \n*252. SHRI  DEVDAS APTE: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have announced that identity of mobile users would be \nverified by mobile operators; \n(b) whether the deadline of 31st March, 2007 was fixed in this matter; \n(c) whether this deadline has been extended; and \n(d) if not, the number of mobile operators who were detected as defaulters? \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI A. RAJA): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. \n(c) No, Sir. \n(d) A special driv e was conducted in May, 2007 to audit the status of  \nsubscriber verification being carried out by the mobile operators on sample basis.  \nAs per the reports of the said special drive, some discrepancies in subscriber  \nverification have been noticed in the sample data provided by 26 mobile operators. \nIllegal mining of coal \n*253. SHRI JANARDHANA POOJARY: Will the Minister of COAL be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that hundreds of people have lost their lives while extracting \ncoal from abandoned coal mines in Jharkhand and other coal mines in the last few years; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the measures which have been taken to prevent such illegal mining activities in \nthe country? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL (DR. DASARI \nNARAYANA RAO): (a) and (b) Since law and order is a State subject, coal companies \nhave no data with regard to persons who died in the process of illegal coal mining.  \n(c) Following measures have been taken to prevent illegal mining: \n(i)     Rat holes created for the purpose of illegal mining are being dozed off and \nfilled up with stone and debris wherever possible. \n(ii)    Trenches have been dug to isolate the illegal mining sites. \n(iii)   Concrete walls are being constructed at the mouth of the abandoned mines \nto prevent access and illegal activities in these areas. \n(iv)   Fencing  of illegal  mining  sites  and displaying  of sign  boards mentioning \n\"Dangerous and Prohibited Place\" \n\n32     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred Questions \n(v)    Dumping of the overburden is being done on the outcrop zones. \n(vi) Erection of barbed -wire/wall fencing around pithead depots, static security \nmanning including deployment of armed guards during the night hours. \n(vii) Sealing of illegal mining spots is resorted to. \n(viii) Stringent action is taken against transport vehicles caught in the act of theft \nor pilferage. \n(ix) Training of existing security personnel, refresher training of CISF personnel \nand basic training to new recruits in security discipline are arranged for \nstrengthening the security set up. \n(x) Engaging of lady security guard for preventing women and children indulging \nin theft/pilferage of coal, strengthening of the security discipline by \nreassessing the requirement of security personnel, horizontal movement of \nexecutives with aptitude for security work and inducting qualified security \npersonnel at junior, middle and senior levels. \n(xi) Ministry of Coal has been urging from time to time the Coal Producing \nStates to check illegal mining. The State Governments were also advised to \nconsider framing of suitable rules under the provisions of the Mines and \nMinerals (Development and Regulation Act, 1957) to strengthen the hands \nof District Authorities in curbing illegal activities. \n(xii) A Committee under the Chairmanship of Minister of State for Coal has been \nconstituted to address the problems of illegal mining of coal. \nPollution in Yamuna \n  †*254. SHRI DATTA MEGHE: \nSHRI SANJAY RAUT: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Yamuna Development Auth ority has been set up for \ncleaning the river Yamuna, wherein all those bodies and people have been included who \nare associated with its cleaning work in one way or the other; \n(b) if so, the reasons for not achieving success in the implementation of the schemes  \nmeant for cleaning the river Yamuna so far; \n \n(c) whether any action plan has been drawn after detailed study of the increasing \npollution in river Yamuna; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a) The Central Government has decided \nto constitute a High Powered Committee for Yamuna River Development. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Starred Questions     33 \nThe Committee will be chaired by the Lieutenant Governor, Delhi with Chief Minister \nof Delhi as Vice-chair and the following members; \nSecretary, Ministry of Urban Development \nSecretary, Ministry of Environment and Forests \nSecretary, Ministry of Water Resources \nChief Secretary, Government of NCT of Delhi \nPrincipal Secretary, Urban Development, Government of NCT of Delhi \nChief Executive Officer, Delhi Jal Board \nVice Chairman; Delhi Development Authority \n(b) The quality of water in Yamuna river has not shown the desired improve ment, \nparticularly in Delhi, due to enormous increase in pollution load and lack of fresh water \nin the river during lean period. Implementation of the river pollution abatement works is \nan ongoing and continuous activity based upon the estimated pollution loads. Due to \never-increasing population, leading to increased pollution load, and gap in the \navailability of financial resources, there is persistent divergence between the pollution \nload tackled and the actual pollution load. \n(c) and (d) To prevent increasin g pollution in the Yamuna river, two Action Plans \nhave been launched by the Government of India. The Yamuna Action Plan (YAP) \nPhase-I was started in April, 1993 in the three States of UP, Delhi and Haryana covering \n15 towns at an estimated cost of Rs. 715 crore. The project with funding from the Japan \nBank for International Cooperation (JBIC) was completed in February, 2003. A total of \n269 schemes of pollution abatement were completed including 38 sewage treatment \nplants having additional treatment capacity  of 753.25 million litres per day (mld) of \nwhich 401.25 mld in U.P., 322 mld in Haryana and 30 mld in Delhi. \nYAP Phase -II was started in December, 2004 also with JBIC funding. The project \nwas approved for the three States of Delhi, Haryana and Uttar Prades h at an estimated \ncost of Rs. 624 crore. The major items of work to be implemented under YAP Phase -II \ninclude rehabilitation of trunk sewers and works of sewage treatment plants in Delhi, \nconstruction of sewage treatment plants and rehabilitation of sewer lines/rising mains in \nU.P., interception and diversion of sewer lines and improvement in the efficiency of \nexisting sewage treatment plants in Haryana. \nIn addition to the above Action Plan, the State Governments of Delhi, Haryana and \nUttar Pradesh have als o taken up non- sewerage, sewerage and sewage treatment works \nout of their own plan funds. \nSea-worthiness of ships and boats \n*255. SHRI S.M. LALJAN BASHA: Will the Minister of SHIPPING ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government expect an increase in the number of ships and ocean plying \nvessels using Indian ports over the next ten years; \n(b) whether Director -General of Shipping is the sole maritime authority to \ndetermine \"sea-worthiness\" of all vessels flying the Indian flag or using Indian ports as a \nbase; \n\n34     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred Questions \n(c) the manner in which the Director -General, Shipping determines whether a large \nship or fishing boat is \"sea-worthy\"; \n(d) whether it is a fact that there are complaints on the un -predictable pattern of \nbehaviour of the personnel of Director -General, Shipping in making inspections and \nsuggestions; and \n( e) the steps proposed to assess the work of all surveyors on their pattern of work?  \nTHE MINISTER OF SHIPPING, ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWA YS (SHRI \nT.R. BAALU): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) Yes, Sir. However, the Government of India has also delegated under the \nMerchant Shipping Act, the work of survey, certification and inspection for ascertaining \nthe seaworthiness of vessel to classification societies. \n(c) The criteria for determining the sea -worthiness condition of a large ship or \nfishing boat is prescribed in Merchant Shipping Rules framed under Merchant Shipping \nAct, 1958. \n(d) and (e) Complaints received from time to time, against surveyors are \nimmediately investigated by the Director General of Shipping and appropriate  \ndisciplinary/vigilance action is taken. The assessment of work done by the  \nsurveyors is undertaken through superiors and controlling officers. In addition, the  \nfeedback from the industry is taken into account in assessing their performance. \nTelephone connections by 2010 \n *256. SHRI AMAR SINGH: \nSHRI UDAY PRATAP SINGH: \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have set a target of 250 million telephone connections by \n2007 and 500 million by 2010; \n(b) whether the pace of providing telephones is sufficient to meet these targets and \nwhether Government propose to introduce more operators for achieving it; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor; \n(e) whether Government propose to make it compulsory for all the operators to make \nuse of all technologies for achieving spectral efficiency and thereby requiring less \nspectrum which would help in introducing more competition; and \n(f) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATON TECHNOLOGY \n(SHRI A. RAJA): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) to (d) Against the target of 250 million telephone connections by December \n2007, we have reached about 233 million connections by 31st July 2007. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Starred Questions     35 \nLooking to the present additions per month, it is expected that the target of 250 million \nwould be achieved before the year end. \n(e) and (f) New Telecom Policy (NTP) 99 adopts a technology neutral policy.  \nRanchi and other cities under JNNURM \nt*257. SHRI AJAY MAROO: Will the Minister of URBAN DEVELOPMENT be \npleased to state: \n(a) the names of such parts of Ranchi, the capital of Jharkhand, and other cities of \nthe State which have been included for impro vement under the Jawaharlal Nehru \nNational Urban Renewal Mission; \n(b) the details of the amount allocated for Jharkhand under this scheme and by when \nit is likely to be implemented; \n(c) whether there is any difficulty in implementing this scheme in the State; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT (SHRI S. JAIPAL REDDY): (a) \nThree cities namely Ranchi, Dhanbad and Jamshedpur are covered under Urban \nInfrastructure and Governance (UIG) component of Jawaharlal Nehru National Urban \nRenewal Mission  (JNNURM). \n(b) to (d) The Planning Commission has made indicative allocation of a sum of Rs. \n641.20 crore and Rs. 70.53 crore for the sub -Mission on UIG and a sum of Rs. 114.52 \ncrore and Rs. 12.60 crore for UIDSSMT for the entire Mission period (2005- 12) and for \n2007-08 respectively in respect of State of Jharkhand. \nHowever, funds have not been released to any of cities in Jharkhand so far, as \nJharkhand has not yet signed Memorandum of Agreement (MoA) with the Central \nGovernment for implementation of mandatory/optional reforms as required under the \nguidelines of JNNURM for accessing funds. No Detailed Project Report (DPR) has been \nreceived from Jharkhand State so far. \nCorruption in Panchayati Raj \n*258. MS. MABEL REBELLO: Will the Minister of PANCHAYATI  RAJ be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government are aware that panchayati raj in most of the States is a \nground, for breeding corruption; and \n(b) by when panchayati raj would be really successful in people's projects in rural \nareas? \nTHE MINISTER OF PANCHAYATI  RAJ (SHRI MANI SHANKAR AIYAR): (a) \nWhile there are reports of corruption in Panchayati Raj, the Ministry of Panchayati Raj \ndoes not share the view that Panchayati Raj Institutions, generally speaking, are \nbreeding grounds for corruption. With respect to individual cases of \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n36     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Starred Questions \ncorruption, in terms of the Constitutional order, as set out in Article 243G, it is the State \nLegislatures and State executive who have been charged with endowing \"the Panchayats \nwith such powers and authority as may be necessary to enable them to function as \ninstitutions of self -government\". Accordingly, it is primarily the responsibility of State \nGovernments to monitor the implementation of works and the delivery of services by the \nPanchayati Raj Institutions. In so far as the schemes of the Ministry of Rural \nDevelopment are concerned, District Vigilance and Monitoring Committees have been \nestablished under the chairpersonship of Members of Parliament.  \n(b) Panchayati Raj will be truly successful only when Panchayati Raj Institutions are \neffectively endowed with functions, finances and functionaries as set out in the National \nCommon Minimum Programme. The Union Government is working along with States \nto achieve this goal. \nCoastal Zone Management Authority in Goa \n*259. SHRI SHANTARAM LAXMAN NAIK: Will the PRIME MINISTER be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Goa Coastal Zone Management Authority has started ground level \nsurvey as wel l as house verification of dwelling units and all other constructions in the \nCoastal Regulation Zone-Ill area, in order to identify the structures that were constructed \nafter February 19, 1991; \n(b) whether the survey has been completed; \n(c) whether the authority has submitted its report; \n(d) whether any action has been taken in that regard; and \n(e) if so, the details, thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a) Yes, Sir. In compliance to the \ndirections of the Hon'ble High Court of Bombay at Panjim-Goa in Writ Petitions No.422 \nof 1998 and No.99 of 1999, the Goa Coastal Zone Management Authority (GCZMA) \nhas initiated house to house field validation to ascertain the violations in the Coastal \nRegulation Zone II and III areas of Goa. \n(b) and (c) No, Sir. \n(d) and (e) Does not arise. \nIncreasing pollution in various rivers \n†*260. SHRIMATI MAYA SINGH: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that pollution has been increasing in various rivers in cluding \nthe Ganga despite spending crores of rupees; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions     37 \n(b) the methods which have been employed to make the three rivers of Europe viz. \nThames, Rhines and Danube, which were previously highly polluted ones, but now \npollution free; \n(c) whether any high level enquiry has been conducted into the matter of making \nthese holy rivers in India pollution free; \n(d) whether the agencies responsible for the same have been punished; and \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND FORESTS \n(SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a) River conservation work has been taken up for a number \nof rivers in the country, starting with the Ganga Action Plan (GAP) Phase I in 1985, for \npolluted stretches in the Ganga river, extending to the major tributaries of the Ganga in \n1993, and further expanding to cover 34 major rivers in 20 States under the National River \nConservation Plan (NRCP) from 1995 onwards. The expenditure on GAP-I was Rs.433.30 \ncrore and the approved cost of NRCP is Rs,4783 crore. \nWith the completion of the pollution abatement works, the water quality of the river \nGanga has shown an improvement at major locations over its pre-GAP quality but this is not \nso in the case of the Yamuna, primarily due to the large gap between the demand and the \navailability of sewage treatment capacity, particularly in Delhi, and lack of availability of \nfresh water for ensuring minimum flow in the river. The wor ks in respect of other rivers \nare at different stages of completion. \n(b) The river clean up projects in rivers like the Thames, Rhine and Danube span a \nlong period of time, between 20— 40 years, with much higher investments and costs when \ncompared with Ganga Action Plan and the NRCP. The strategies involved were primarily, \nidentification of pollution hotspots and treating of domestic and industrial effluents \nthrough construction of wastewater treatment plants. For Indian rivers, domestic sewage is \nthe major source of pollution and interception and diversion of raw sewage and treatment \nthrough sewage treatment plants form the main components of pollution abatement works \nundertaken by the Government. The works are implemented through agencies nominated by the \nState Government. As per an independent evaluation study conducted by University of Bath \nand Metroeconomica, United Kingdom and the Institute of Economic Growth, New Delhi \n(2000), the Ganga Action Plan compared favourably, both in terms of cost and time taken, \nand results achieved with those of the major rivers of the world like Thames, Rhine and \nDanube. \n(c) to (e) No high level enquiry has been conducted. However, 314 defaulting industries \nhave been closed as on 31.12.2006. \nWRITTEN ANSWERS TO THE UNSTARRED QUESTIONS \nDevelopment of technique for preservation of fruits \n1829. SHRIMATI VIPLOVE THAKUR: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Bhabha Atomic Research Centre (BARC) has developed a \ntechnique of long lasting preservation of fruits; \n\n38     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(b) if so, whether this technique has been adopted for preservation of various type of \nfruits and vegetables in Himachal Pradesh; \n(c) if so, the results thereof, and \n(d) if not, the reason therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE PRIME MINISTER'S OFFICE (SHRI \nPRITHVIRAJ CHAVAN): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) Experimental tests on apples from Himachal Pradesh have been conducted and \nthe results are encouraging. However, commercial application of this technology has not \nbeen taken up in Himachal Pradesh. \n(c) and (d) Mango was the only fruit among the various food items approved for \nradiation preservation, under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act Rules 1955. \nHowever, recently a notification has been issued approving food i rradiation on generic \nclass basis. With this, commercial radiation processing of other fruits is now possible.  \nShortage of Coal Washeries \n†1830. SHRI KALRAJ MISHRA: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether there is shortage of coal washeries in the country; \n(b) whether Government propose to enhance the number of coal washeries keeping \nin view the introduction of clean coal technology; \n(c) if so, the details thereof; \n(d) whether Government propose to make it mandatory for captive coal mines to \nbuild washeries; and \n(e) if so, by when the directions would be issued in this regard? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL (DR. DASARI \nNARAYANA RAO): (a) Demand of washed coal is derived out of consumers' need of \nimproved and consistent quality arising out of techno commercial requirement. Demand \nfor coal is assessed in terms of raw coal whereas specific demand of consumers of \nwashed coal is met from the washery capacity available with the coal companies or in \nthe private sector. \n(b) and (c) It has recently been decided that in order to supply improved \nquality and sized coal to the consumers, CIL would set up washeries under Build \nand Operate Scheme for supply of washed coal to all consumers other than pithead  \nconsumers. It has also been decided that all new open cast projects' with capacity  \nof more  than 2.50 MTY  and not linked to pithead consumers  will  have an \nintegrated washery. \n1 (d) and (e) At present there is no proposal to make it mandatory for captive  \ncoal mines to build washeries. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions     39 \nFormation of a Regulatory Commission \ntl831. SHRI LAXMINARAYAN SHARMA: Will the PRIME MINISTER be \npleased to state: \n\"by when the action regarding formation of a R egulatory Commission would be \ntaken for proper control over the-rates, supply of coal and its quality\"? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL (DR. DASARI \nNARAYANA RAO): The Government has appointed the Administrative Staff College \nof India (ASCI), Hyderabad as Consultant to examine various issues including the need \nfor a regulator, in consultation with all the stakeholders and then to suggest a suitable \nregulatory frame work. The appointment of coal regulator would require legislative \napproval. Hence, it is difficult to suggest a time frame for formation of a regulatory \nauthority. \nLosses incurred by CIL \ntl832. SHRI AJAY MAROO: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of coal mines of Coal India Ltd. (CIL) which are running in losses at  \npresent; \n(b) the amount of loss incurred to the company every year as a result of mines \nrunning in losses; and \n(c) the action being taken to improve the condition of these mines? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL (DR. DASARI \nNARAYANA RAO): (a) and (b)  The number of coal mines which are running in losses \nand the amount of loss incurred by CIL and its subsidiaries during 2006- 2007 are given \nas under:  \nCompany No. of mines Amount of Loss \n in losses (Rs. in crore) \nEastern Coalfields Ltd. (ECL) 103 1164.44 \nBharat Coking Coal Ltd. (BCCL) 87 969.53 \nCentral Coalfields Ltd. (CCL) 37 487.50 \nNorthern Coalfields Ltd. (NCL) - - \nWestern Coalfields Ltd. (WCL) 44 334.49 \nSouth Eastern Coalfields Ltd. (SECL) 46 345.10 \nMahanadi Coalfields Ltd. (MCL) 7 74.46 \nNorth Eastern Coalfields/ 3 73.49 \nCoal India Ltd. (NEC/CIL)   \nTOTAL:     327 3449.01 \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n40     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(c) Following actions are being taken by the coal companies to improve the conditions \nof these mines:- \n        (1)    Mechanization of underground mines with suitable intermediate technology \ni.e. Side Discharge Loader (SDL), Load Haul Dumper (LHD) alongwith transport \nsystem to improve production and productivity of the mines. \n(2) To deploy low cost continuous miner for viable economics, wherever \npossible. \n(3) To replace Surveyed off equipments with new fleet of Heavy Earth Moving \nMachine (HEMM) for improving availability and utilization. \n(4) Identification of isolated and fire effected patches for working through hired \nHEMMs. \n(5) Introduction of Mechanized loading in place of manual loading. \n(6) Rationalization of manpower. \nAllotment of coal fields \n  † l833. DR. MURLI MANOHAR JOSHI: \nSHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is fact that 745 applications have been received for the allotment of 15 \ncoal fields in the country; \n(b) if not, the facts in this regard; \n(c) whether petitioners include those people also to whom coal fields have been allotted for \nmining earlier; \n(d) if so, the names of those people and names of the people out of them who have not \nmade any production as per rule in the allotted coal fields so far; and \n(e) whether Government would blacklist such petitioners of these coal fields? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL (DR. DASARI NARAYANA \nRAO): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. 745 applications were received for 15 coal blocks earmarked for \nallocation to power sector under the captive use dispensation. \n(c) and (e) Yes, Sir. There are 36 appl icant companies which have applied for power \nblocks and were already allocated coal blocks for different end uses. A list indicating names of \n36 companies and status of production from the block allocated to them is given in the \nStatement {See below). The list also indicates names of such allocatees which have not \nstarted production. \nThe allocatees of coal blocks, who have not started production so far, are in various \nstages of obtaining statutory clearances and mining lease, preparing \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions    41 \nmining plan, acquiring land, procuring machinery and equipment etc. for both the mining as \nwell as end-use project. The progress achieved by them against certain given milestones are \nreviewed by a Committee headed by Additional Secretary (Coal) periodically. \nDevelopment of coal blocks involves a long gestation of about 3 to 5 years to reach \nproduction stage and about another two. years to reach its optimal production capacity. \nSpecific mile stones are laid down for development of coal blocks. This is monitored \nand reviewed periodically. In case of willful delays, notices are issued, where warranted, de-\nallocation of coal blocks could be considered. \nStatement \nA list indicating names of 36 companies and status of \nproduction from the block allocated to them  \nSI. \nNo. \nName of the party No. of block \nallocated \nNo. of block in \nproduction (MT) \n1 2 3 4 \n1 M/s Jindal Steel and Power Ltd 4 1 \n2 M/s Monnet Ispat Ltd. 2 1 \n3 M/s Jindal Power Ltd. 2 Started since            \nJune, 2007 \n4 M/s Raipur Alloys and Steel Ltd. 1 Expected to start in            \n07-08 \n5 M/s Prakash Industries Ltd.