losed down totally or partially in month of \nApril; \n(c) if so, the steps taken or being taken to overcome the danger arisen out of \nwaterlogging; and \n(d) The details of safety measures taken in this mine for future? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF C OAL (DR. DASARI \nNARAYANA RAO): (a) Yes, Sir. The mine management is aware of possible water -\nlogging at Tandsi 3 and 4 under ground coal mine; in Chhindwada district of Madhya \nPradesh. \n(b) on 16-4-2007 one section of Tandsi 1 and 2 mine, i.e. 14X depillaring se ction \nwas stopped due to waterlogging in adjacent Tandsi 3 and 4 mine. \n(c) waterlogged workings were dewatered successfully to overcome the danger. \n(d) water level is being kept at safe level as suggested by Directorate General of \nMines Safety (DGMS) and is being monitored regularly as safety measure in future. \nStand-by pumping arrangement has been made. \nSecurity threat for coal companies \n1835. SHRI C. PERUMAL: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that there is security threat for c oal companies in our country; \nand \n(b ) if so, the actions taken by Government to provide proper security systems for \nthose companies? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL (DR. DASARI \nNARAYANA RAO): \n(a) and (b) There are no special security threats to Coal Companies in the Country. \nGenerally, the following security measures have been adopted to deal with security \nthreat to the coal companies:- \n1. Intelligence gathering; \n2. ensuring all round protection to the establishments through CISF and other \nsecurity personnel; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n44     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n3. access control; \n4. multiple communication system for quick response; \n5. effective  security  measures  for the  safeguarding  of explosive magazines, \ncash etc., \n6. intensive patrolling in and around the coalfields round the clock and \noverlapping of surveillance among patrols; \n7. introduction of techno-gadgetory at sensitive installations; \n8. use of trained guarding dog squads at stores, workshops and outer periphery \nof the mine areas; \n9. regular liaison with police and State authorities; \n10. guarding of vital installations with armed guards; \n11. arrangement of early warning system at high threat perception zones. \nSetting up of coal washeries   \n1836. SHRI B.J. PANDA: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Centre has directed the CIL and its subsidiaries to push up setting up \nof coal washeries or expanding the existing ones to boost supply of coal; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) to what extent this would help to meet the needs of the consumers? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL (DR. DASARI NARAYANA \nRAO): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. Coal India Limited (CIL) has been encouraging Power Sector \nand other coal consumers and private entreprene urs to set up coal washeries on Build -\nOwn-Operate basis for supply of washed coal to the linked consumers. To encourage \nsetting up coal washeries by consumers/ private entrepreneurs, CIL has been providing \nland and other infrastructural facilities on lease/chargeable basis subject to its availability. \nCIL has also decided in principle to set up washeries with the State -of-the-art \ntechnologies on \"Build and Operate\" (BO) basis where Coal India Limited and its \nsubsidiary companies will provide the capital fun ding and other infrastructure facilities. \nPrivate entrepreneurs having core competence in the field will construct and operate the \nwasheries. Further, it has been decided that all new opencast projects of more than 2.50 \nmillion tonnes capacity, which are not linked to pit-head power stations should be designed \nwith an integrated washery. \n(c) The need of the consumers for improved and consistent quality of coal will be \nsatisfied to a large extent through supply of washed coal. Existing non coking coal \nwashing capacity is 103 metric ton per year and it is expected that additional 149 metric \ntone per year additional washing capacity would be added in XI Plan. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions    45 \nReview of private sector ownership of coal mines \n1837. SHRI RAJKUMAR DHOOT: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that all coal mines are owned by public sector, private sector \nand joint sector; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State-wise; \n(c) whether Government are considering review of private sector ownership of coal \nmines; \n(d) if so. the details thereof; arid \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COAL (DR. DASARI NARAYANA \nRAO): (a) and (b) The coal mines are owned by either a public sector company, private sector \ncompany or joint venture company. Besides the Coal India Limited, a public sector undertaking \nand the Singareni Collieries Company, a joint venture company of the Government of India \nand the Government of Andhra Pradesh, there are 165 coal  blocks allocated to other \npublic/private sector companies. The table below indicates the number of mines/coal blocks \nowned by Public Sector and Private Sector Companies. \nNumber of mines/blocks  \nState CIL SCCL PSUs other than \nCIL and SCCL \nPrivate \nCompanies \nWest Bengal 98  13 2 \nJharkhand 159  26 19 \nUttar Pradesh 4  - - \nMadhya Pradesh 73  11 8 \nMaharashtra 52  9 13 \nChhattisgarh 54  14 16 \nOrissa 23  17 12 \nAssam 5  - - \nAndhra Pradesh  55 4 - \nArunachal Pradesh   1 - \nTOTAL: 468 55 95* 70 \n* The blocks a llocated jointly to public sector companies and private sector, \ncompanies have been indicated in public sector as public sector companies account for \nmajor share of reserves. \n\n46     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(c) to (e) Blocks are being allocated to private sector companies as per the provisions \nof the Coal Mines (Nationalisation) Act, 1973 for specified end uses. A Bill, namely, the \nCoal Mines (Nationalisation) Amendment Bill, 2000, was introduced in the Rajya Sabha \nto amend the Coal Mines (Nationalisation) Act, 1973. The amendment Bill seeks to \nallow an Indian company to undertake coal mining without the present restriction of \ncaptive end use. \nIntimidation of subscribers by telecom operators \n1838. SHRI B.K. HARIPRASAD: Will the M inister of COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether on the lines of banks and credit card companies engaging recovery  \nagents, the telecom operators have also employed recovery agents, who intimidate  \nsubscribers for paying up their dues; \n(b) whether many such telecom operators have engaged junior lawyers to threaten \nsubscribers with non-bailable warrants, if they don't pay up the dues; \n(c) whether the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI) has asked for details \nfrom telecom operators of methods used by them for recovery of dues; and \n(d) whether the call data records of subscribers are being shared with the recovery \nagents? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) Te lecom Service \nProviders, in certain cases, unable to recover the outstanding dues outsource the work of \nrecovery of dues to recovery agents. \n(b) Telecom operators hire/engage lawyers to initiate legal proceedings for  \nrecovery of disputed outstanding dues. \n(c) Yes, Sir. \n(d) As per reports received from the service providers, the call data records are not \nshared by mobile companies with the recovery agents. \nLosses incurred by MTNL \n1839. SHRI ABU ASIM AZMI: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND  \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are aware that Mahanagar Telephone Nigam Ltd. (MTNL) \nDelhi is running into losses; \n(b) if so, the reasons therefor alongwith the time since when it has been running in \nlosses; \n(c) the amount of loss incurred during eac h year of the last loss making \nfinancial year; and \n(d) the steps taken/proposed to be taken to improve the situation? \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions    47 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) No, Sir. MTNL as a \nwhole has earned profit of Rs.681.74 crores in 2006- 07. However, MTNL Delhi Unit \nhas incurred a loss of Rs.4.59 crores in 2006-07. \n(b) The main reason for the loss in Delhi Unit is the stiff competition and re duction \nin landline. A large number of connections in MTNL Delhi are from legacy system \nrequiring fixed costs to maintain, which are difficult to reduce (being committed \npayments). This is the 1st year in which Delhi Unit has exhibited operational loss. \n(c) Loss incurred is Rs.4.59 crores in Delhi Unit for the year 2006- 07, for the first \ntime. \n(d) The following steps have been taken to improve the situation. \n \n♦ Introduction of various schemes to attract new landline subscribers and \nsustaining existing landline base. \n♦ More emphasis on-adding GSM and Broadband connections. \n♦ Bringing Delhi and Mumbai units under 95 dialing facility (local call). \n♦ Introduction of flexible tariff policies. \n♦ Rationalization of expenditure to reduce Administrative and Operative cost. \n♦ Implementing Voluntary Retirement Scheme (VRS) to reduce staff cost. \n♦ Overseas operation as an operator. \nNorms for timely delivery of mails \n1840. SHRI MOTIUR RAHMAN: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS \nAND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Department  of Posts (DoP) has formulated any norms in the matter of \ndelivery of mails; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether only 55 per cent of mail is being delivered within the prescribed norms \nof delivery; and \n(d) if so, the steps taken by DoP to enhance its efficienc y in terms of timely delivery \nof mail to improve customer satisfaction? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) Department of Posts has fixed norms for delivery of mail acros s the country, and \nhas widely circulated it for information of the public. A copy of norms for delivery is \ngiven in the Statement-I (See below). \n\n48     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(c) No, Sir. The Department has a mechanism for ch ecking of delivery efficiency  \nacross the. country through live mail survey. Results of live mail survey for the last  \nthree years are given in the Statement-II and III (Urban and Rural Areas). \n(d) Following measures have been taken to improve delivery effic iency and  \nquality of service: \n(i) Regular monitoring of mail routing and delivery by posting Test Letters and \nTrial Cards. \n(ii) Progressive mechanization of delivery by providing mopeds to postmen for \ndelivery work in outlying areas of big cities. \n(iii) Ra tionalization/restructuring of delivery with a view to deploy adequate \nmanpower in the expanding urban conglomerations. \n(iv) Surprise checks on the delivery of mails by the supervisory staff and officers. \n(v) Live Mail Survey at regular intervals both in r ural and urban areas to identify \nweak links and streamline the mail transmission and delivery system. \n(vi) Regular Co -ordination meetings with Airlines, Railway and State Road \nTransport Authorities to sort out problems relating to transmission of mails.  \n(vii) Encouraging pre -sorting of mails by the bulk mailers by providing discount \nin postage as an incentive and new pricing policies to attract more \ncustomers. \n(viii) Educating the customers for installation of mailboxes on the ground floor \nof multistory buildings. \n(ix) Preparation of triplicate registered list in the destination mail office for bulk \nmailers to speed up delivery of such mail. \n(x) To cope up with the seasonal mails, separate centers with adequate-\nmanpower are opened to give expeditious handling of such mail. \n(xi) Innovative use of Pin- Codes, which have been in use in Department since \n1971 for facilitating sorting and delivery. \nStatement-I \nNorms for transmission and delivery of mail  \nCategory of mail Norms for Delivery \n1 2. \nWithin the District  \n \n \n \nWithin the State \nWithin 48 hours after the day \nof posting. [D+2 ] \n \nWithin 48 to 72 hours after the \nday of posting. [D + 2 TO \nD+3] \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions     49  \n1 2 \nMails for other States 3 to 5 days depending on \n the distance involved and \n transport  links.   [D+3 TO \n D+5] \n Mails from/to Branch Post \n offices take a day more. \nFirst Class Mails between Metro Within 48 to 72 hours after \nCities-[METRO CHANNEL] the day of posting. [ D + 2 \n(Delhi, Mumbai, Calcutta, TO D+3] \nChennai, Hyderabad, Bangalore)  \nFirst Class mails between State Within 48 to 72 hours after: \nCapitals -* the day of posting. [ D + 2 \n TO D+3] \negistered Mail and money order 1   to   2   days   more   than \n ordinary mail depending on \n the distance and points of \n handling. \n♦ The above norms apply to normal areas. Mails relating to hilly, tribal and \nremote areas will take longer, as per specific local conditions. \n♦ These broad norms are subject to mail carrying trains, planes, buses and \nother public transport maintaining their 'schedule. \n[The above norms were issued vide DO No. 13 -5/94-D dated 5 -3-97 of DDG \n[MM&TS] and addressed to all Circles for notifying/displaying to the public. Orders \nhave also been since issued in 1998 to including non -Pin Ce ded mail for metro and \nRajdhani Channels] \n* (This refers to mails from Delhi to State Capital and vice versa -RAJDHANI \nCHANNEL) \nStatement-II \n Comparative mail efficiency for last three years  \nCircle name % of mail delivered \nwithin norms \n% of mail delivered \nwithin norms \n