sam 79.1 90.9 91.1 78.7 77.1 79.6 74.8 80.5 86.5 \nBihar 91.2 85.4 78.7 88.4 86.6 83.8 68.9 76.5 72.3 \nChhattishgath 90.3 89.1 89.2 90.2 88.8 87.4 75.8 64.4 64.4 \nDelhi 91.2 100 96.3 81.1 98.9 100 100 100 100 \nGujarat 98.8 99 97.3 96.3 97.4 96.3 86.7 82.7 95.7 \nHaryana 96.7 99 99.1 98.1 98.9 98.5 82.9 50 48.4 \nHimachal 94.7 95.9 93.9 93.2 89.8 95.4 98.4 89.9 67 \nJ&K 83.3 89.8 86.6 92.9 78.1 68 80.9 73.3 75.6 \nJharkhand 93.8 96.8 92.2 88 84.4 81.1 79.2 96.3 80.8 \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions     51  \n1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 8 10 \nKarnataka .96.9 95.2 97.5 97.6 97.8 97.9 94 98.9 98.2 \nKerala 95.4 94.8 85.1 98 97.5 92.4 88.5 89.4 75.5 \nMadhya Pradesh 99.6 97.1 98.4 99 99.7 99.1 100 100 90.3 \nMaharashtra 91.1 92 88.8 91.1 94.4 87.9 79 86.8 59.8 \nNorth-East 90.4 90.4 90.4 85.6 85.6 85.6 92.9 92.9 92.9 \nOrissa 90.3 98 95.9 75 99.7 68 73.9 97 54.8 \nPunjab 92 93.6 94.4 90.7 95.2 94.8 77.8 94.2 84.9 \nRajasthan 94.8 96.7 97.2 92.2 92.1 98.9 95.1 96.4 82.6 \nTamil Nadu 100 100 100 100 100 100 99.7 99.6 100 \nUttar Pradesh 90.9 96.2 93.7. 93.1 94.8 93.2 83.6 87.4 89.2 \nUttaranchal 83.4 88.3 88.6 94.3 75.7 83.2 80.5 58.2 72.5 \nWest-Bengal 81.9 87.4 73.2 79.2 79.5 71.1 75.8 79.6 72.8 \nAverage 92 94.2 92.05 91 91.4 89.05 85.4 86 79.8\n \nReduction of lower level posts in BSNL \n†1841. SHRI LALIT KISHORE CHATURVEDI: Will the Minister of \nCOMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether a large number of posts are vacant in Bharat Sanchar Nigam  \nLtd.(BSNL) and no recruitment has been made since long; \n \n(b) whether it is also a fact that the number of posts at lower level in the BSNL is being \nreduced; \n(c) the details thereof with regard to the whole country, circle-wise; \n(d) the details thereof with regard to telecommunications in Rajasthan, district-wise; and \n(e) the comparative details of the posts lying vacant and increase in telephone subscribers \nduring the last three years till date? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) to (c) No, Sir. Creation \nand filling up of the posts are continuous processes. Due to declining trend to fixed lines, \ntotal as well as category -wise vacant posts in all the categories are liable to change as \nmajority of the posts relate to fixed line scenario. Keeping in view the fast changing business \nas well as technological scenario, strictly need based promotion as well as outside \nrecruitment had been done in the past and will be continued in future as well. \n(d) Detail is given in Statement-I (See below). \n(e) Detail is given in the Statement-II. \n †Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n \n \n \n \n \n\nStatement-I \nStatus of group-wise sanction, working and vacancy position in the elecome districts \nof Rajasthan telecom circle as on 31.3.2007  \nSI. \nNo. \nName of \nTelecom \nDistrict \n Group-A \n 31-3-07 \n  Group-B \n31-3-07 \n  Group-C  \n31-3-07 \n  Group-D \n 31-3-07 \n \n  \n \nSan. Wkg. Vac. San. Wkg. Vac. San. Wkg. Vac. San. Wkg. Vac. \n1 Ajmer 22 13 9 126 87 39 697 592 105 138 115 23 \n2 Alwar 13 5 8 126 55 71 475 373 102 84 84 0 \n3 Banswara 7 2 5 75 40 35 147 94 53 .65 65 0 \n4 Banner 7 3 4 55 35 20 348 222 126 88 34 54 \n5 Bharatpur 9 7 2 72 46 26 376 342 34 101 46 55 \n6 Bhilwara 10 9 1 91 64 27 373 262 111 28 28 0 \n7 Bikaner 11 9 2 82 57 25 395 312 83 61 61 0 \n8 Bundi 4 4 0 36 24 12 142 66 76 22 11 11 \n9 Chhittorgarh 6 6 0 71 33 38 206 143 63 59 23 36 \n10 Churu 7 5 2 67 38 29 232 188 44 20 19 1 \n11 Jaipur 64 54 10 448 262 186 2619 2252 367 464 418 46 \n12 Jaisaimer 1 1 0 18 14 4 102 93 9 47 9 38 \n13 Jhalawar 4 3 1 45 27 18 102 72 30 15 12 3 \n14 Jhunjhunu .    9 9 0 87 42 45 262 93 169 34 33 1 \n \n\n1. Creation and filling up of the posts are continuous processes. Due to declining trend of fixed lines,  \ntotal as well as category -wise vacant posts in all the categories are liable to change as majority of the \nposts relate to fixed line scenario. \n2. There  is   ball   on  direct  recruitment against Group   'D'   posts.  The  available  posts  are  to  be  \nused  for regularization  of eligible  Casual  Labourers,  compassionate  ground appointments and re-\ndeployment of Industrial Staff. \n3. Conseouent   to  restructuring  of cadres   in  Group   'C   and   'D',  the  posts  occupied  by   pre -\nrestructured  wasting  cadre officials  are  to  be abolished  on  being  vacated  by pre- restructured cadre \nofficials due to promotions, retirements etc. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n15 Jodhpur 20 17 3 168 94 74 888 741 147 189 133 56 \n16 Kota 17 15 2 140 114 26 643 521 122 113 111 2 \n17 Nagaur 11 7 4 63 37 26 298 209 89 37 35 2   . \n18 Pali 13 6 7 93 45 48 501 391 110 45 42 3 \n19 SWM 6 6 0 73 38 35 294 227 67 34 34 0 \n20 Sikar 9 8 1 88 42 46 297 194 103 51 41 10 \n21 Sirohi 9 7 2 78 43 35 330 187  , 143 54 41 13 \n22 SGR 19 8 11 160 87 73 763 616 147 63 61 2 \n23 Tonk 4 4 0 52 30 22 121 90 31 17 17 0 \n24 Udaipur 17 15 2 153 92 61 754 624 130 195 193. 2 \n25 Circle Office 39 39 0 183 183 0 139 138 1 117 117 0 \n.26 CMTS 26 28 0 114 72 42 31 30 1 10 10 0 \n27 CE (Civil) 16 16 0 147 95 52 86 80 6 81 39 42 \n28 Pr.CE (Elect) 10 10 0 104 67 37 27 24 3 26 6 20 \n TOTAL: 392 316 76\" 3015 1863 1152 11648 9176 2472 2258 1838 420 \n**Note: \n\n \n \n54     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nStatement-II \nDetails of subscriber's base during the last 3 years till date  \nSI. As on Wired         WLL       Cellular Grand Vacant Working \nNo               DELs    Connections Total Posts Staff \n1 Jul-2007 32656215    3676493    28974819 65307527 39,472 3147266 \n2. Mar-2007 33738604    3556263    27428658 64723525 33,687 320506 \n3. Mar-2006 35422889   2572525    17163761 55159175 36,339 326948 \n4 Mar-2005 35859482    1628111      9447357 46934950 38,888 339853 \n1. Creation and filling up of the posts are continuous processes. Due to declinin g trend of \nfixed lines, total as well as category -wise vacant posts in all the categories are liable to \nchange as majority of the posts relate to fixed line scenario. \n2. There is ban on direct recruitment against Group 'D' posts. The available posts are to be \nused for regularization of eligible Casual Labourers, compassionate ground appointments \nand re-deployment of Industrial Staff. \n3. Consyquent to restructuring of cadres in Group 'C and 'D' the posts occupied by pre -\nrestructured wasting cadre officials are to be abolished on being vacated by pre -\nrestructured cadre officials due to promotions, retirements etc. \nAppointment on compassionate ground \n1842. SHRI TARIQ ANWAR: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) the total number of applications received from various Departments seeking \nappointment on compassionate ground during each of the last three years; \n(b) the number of persons appointed, applications rejected and pending cases out of the \ntotal applications received; and \n(c) the reasons for the rejection and pendency of these cases? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) and (b) Details of the \ncases relating to various Departments of Ministry of Communications  and Information \nTechnology are given in the Statement (See below). \n(c) There are duly empowered Committees which examine applications received for \nappointment on compassionate grounds keeping in view the guidelines issued by the \nGovernment. The Committee considers received/pending cases periodically depending on \navailability of vacancies and only most indigent cases are recommended for appointment \non compassionate grounds. This is an ongoing exercise. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions     55 \nStatement-I \nDetails of compassionate appointment cases in respect of \nvarious departments of Ministry of Communications and \nInformation Technology during each of the last three years  \nName of the \nDepartment \nYear Total \nNo. of \nApplications \nReceived \nTotal \nNo. of \nPersons \nAppointed \nTotal \nNo. of \nApplications \nRejected \nTotal \nNo. of \nApplications \nPending \nPosts 2004 1226 156 1036 119 \n 2005 1097 246 847 169 \n 2006 1790 252 1191 410 \nInformation 2004 1 NIL NIL 1 \nTechnology 2005 3 1 1 1 \n 2006 3 2 1 1 \nTelecom 2004 \n2005 \n2 \n3 \n   \n 2006 3 4* 2* 2* \n*Includes total number of persons appointed, applications rejected and pending cases out of the total \napplications received during each of last three years. \nOver-allotment of spectrum \n1843. SHRI V. HANUMANTHA RAO: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS \nAND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that GSM telecom companies are trying to get extra 2G \nspectrum free of charge; \n(b) the basis of their demand; \n(c) whether their association is using and 'oppressive and unfair' contract to penalize \ncustomers; \n(d) whether it is also a fact that already extra and over -allotments of spectrum has \nbeen done;and \n(e) the steps proposed to enhance competition for benefit of Indian public? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMU NICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): \n(a) to (d) No, Sir. The initial spectrum is allotted to the GSM operators in \naccordance with the relevant provisions of their respective Service Licence Agreements \nsubject to availability of spectrum. \nFurther, to meet the growth of mobile telecom services, additional spectrum is \nallotted as per the criteria which prescribe \"Active Subscribers\" and \"Peak traffic\" \naveraged over a month in the Visitor Locator Register (VLR) and Network \nManagement System  (NMS) respectively. This allotment of additional spectrum \nensures optimal and efficient use of allotted spectrum. \n\n56     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(e) TRAI has indicated in its recommendations dated 13th May, 2005 on spectrum \nrelated issues that from the analysis of level of competition it is evident that with 4 to 7 \nmobile operators in different service areas, there is adequate competition in almost all \nthe service areas. \nLow market share of BSNL/MTNL phone services \n1844. DR. K . MALAISAMY: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) the market share of BSNL/MTNL in mobile phone services comparing private \nservices; \n(b) the reasons for its low share despite its infrastructure, technical back up etc; \n \n(c) in terms of service and facilities, how private services stand head and shoulder \nabove BSNL/MTNL; and \n(d) the major measures to improve the performance, facilities, services, etc. to equate \nwith the best private services and the time-frame before which it could be done? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) As on 31.07.2007, \nthe market share of BSNL and MTNL in Global System for Mobile Communications \n(GSM) compared to other private oper ators is 20.44% and 16.4% respectively in their \narea of operation. \n(b) In all, there are 9 private GSM. mobile telephone operators providing mobile \ntelephone services besides BSNL and MTNL. Out of these, there are four major \noperators viz. BSNL, Bharti, Vodaph one, Essar and Idea. The lower market of BSNL \nshare in mobile has happened mainly due to capacity constraints of mobile equipment in \nBSNL. The present average utilization of mobile capacity of BSNL is 100%. The mobile \ntender of BSNL has been finalized and Advanced Purchase Order is issued to the \nsuccessful bidder which is yet to be accepted by vendor. MTNL has been facing a very \ntough competition in Delhi and Mumbai from private telcos like Tata, Reliance, Bharti \netc. Nevertheless, MTNL is giving a tough fi ght to the competition by taking lot of \nmeasures to improve perfonnance, facilities and services, expand the capacity etc.  \n(c) GSM based cellular mobile service being provided by BSNL and MTNL are at \npar with other private operators. The said service of BSNL a nd MTNL is working \nsatisfactorily and is, in general, meeting the Quality of Service (QoS) parameters \nprescribed by Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI). The cellular mobile \nnetwork of BSNL/MTNL is continuously optimized to ensure better perfonnance. \n(d) The major measures taken by BSNL and MTNL to improve the performance, \nfacilities, services, etc. to equate with other private operators during this financial year \nare given in Statment-I and II respectively. \nStatement-I \nMajor measures taken by BSNL \n Global  System  for Mobile Communications  (GSM) based Cellular Mobile \nTelephone Service being provided by Bharat Sanchar Nigam \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions     57 \nLimited (BSNL) is comparable to that provided by  private mobile service \nlicensees in India. \n  The said service of BSNL is working satisfactorily and is, in general, \nmeeting the quality of Service (QoS) parameters prescribed by Telecom \nRegulatory Authority of India (TRAI). The cellular mobile network of \nBSNL is being continuously optimized for its performance and monitoring \nof the network has been strengthened by BSNL to ensure performance as \nper the QoS parameters prescribed by TRAI. \n BSNL is, inter alia, provided General Pocket Radio Service (GPRS) and \nMulti Media Messaging Service (MMS) in all its licensed Service Areas \nexcept in some areas of Madhya Pradesh, Gujarat, Maharashtra and \nChhattisgarh. Further, Enhanced Data Rates for Global Evolution (EDGE) \nhas been made operational in major cities and districts headquarters. \nVarious contents based Value Added Services and personalized Ring Back \nTones are also provided to GSM based mobile subscribers. As such, \nBSNL is keeping pace with the advancement in technology and providing \nlatest services to its customers. \nStatement-II \nMajor measures taken by MTNL \n MTNL has planned to expand Cellular Mobile Network by one million each in \nDelhi and Mumbai in 2007- 08. Purchase Order has already been placed for \n750 K lines each for Delhi and Mumbai. Installation of the network has also \nbeen started. Nearly 300 BTS each in Delhi/Mumbai are being installed which \nwill further improve coverage and capacity. \n MTNL plans to commission Convergent Billing and CRM in second quarter of \n2007-08. This system provides one bill for all services to a subscriber.  The \nsystem will also address customer request of services, tariff, complaint \nresolution etc. MTNL is providing a lot of Value Added Services for both \nPSTN and Mobile subscribers like news, songs, astrology, e -ticketing, SMS, \nVoice SMS, Internet, Broadband, IPTV etc. in line with the emerging trends. \n New Tariff plans both in land line and cellular based PCOs are launched to \nretain PCO holders and attract new PCO franchisees. \n MTNL is also taking care of its customers by opening Sanchar Haat, QCSC, \nappointment of Dealers and Agent and special care of Corporate customers. \nMTNL is reviewing its tariff for various products and services so as to make \nthem customer friendly and to suit various segments of the society. \n MTNL has planned to launch 3G mobile services VOIP and Wi-MAX network \nin 2007-08. \n MTNL has introduced Broadband services w.e.f. 14.01.2005 which has been a \ngreat success in the country. 4.99 lakhs broadband connections are working as \non 30.06.2007. In addition, MTNL plans to provide 10 lakhs lines capa city of \nbroadband network in 2007-08. \n\n58     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n MTNL has launched IPTV services in both Delhi and Mumbai. It will help stop \nthe churn of landline subscribers. \n Improving the legacy PSTN network by change of cables, drop wire etc. \nBringing Delhi and Mumbai under 95 dialing facility (local call). \nPostal subsidy for newspapers and periodicals \n1845. SHRI PRAVEEN RASHTRAPAL: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS \nAND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) the p rocedure for subsidized postal charges to be paid by newspapers and \nperiodicals; \n(b) the reasons for requirement of all to approach the Central Authority at Delhi; \n(c) the reasons for not delegating these powers to Chief Post Master General of \nrespective State; and \n(d) whether it is a fact that postal facilities of hundreds of periodicals is  \ncancelled in the recent past? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) The procedure for \nspecial rate of post age to be paid by newspapers and periodicals is given in the \nStatement (See below). \n(b) As per the existing procedure, Divisional Superintendents of post offices and \nGazetted Postmasters can register publications as registered newspapers for the purpose \nof transmission by post to avail special rate of postage. There is no procedure to register \npublications falling under the category of periodicals. Periodicals can avail special rate \nof postage provided they fulfill the stipulate conditions. There is no requ irement to \napproach the Central Authority at Delhi. \n(d) In all, 395 registered newspapers were not considered to be extended the special \nrate of postage during the year 2006- 2007 and till date, since these publications did not \nfulfill the prescribed conditions for availing this facility. \nStatement \nProcedure for special rate of postage to be \npaid by Newspapers and Periodicals \n(I)      Newspapers \nSection 9 of the Indian Post Office Act, 1898 provides that (1) The Central \nGovernment may make rules providing fo r the registration of newspapers for \ntransmission by inland post as registered newspapers. \n(2)       For the purpose of such registration, every publication, consisting of wholly or in \ngreat part of political or other news, or of articles relating thereto or to other \ncurrent topics, with or without advertisements, shall be deemed a newspaper, \nsubject to the following conditions, namely:- \n(a) that it is published in numbers at intervals of not more than thirty-one days; and \n(b) that it has a bona fide list of subscribers. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions     59 \nThe Divisional Superintendents and independent Gazetted Post Masters have \nbeen delegated powers of granting registration of publications, which fulfill the \nprescribed conditions, as regis tered newspapers for transmission by post on special rate \nof postage. \nPROCEDURE: \nAn application for the first registration of a newspaper as registered newspaper \nfor the purpose of transmission by post shall, save in the case of a newspaper printed or \npublished under the orders of any Government in India or for official purposes, be made \nto Divisional Superintendent of Post Offices or Gazetted Postmasters in the form \nprescribed for the purpose by the Director General, and will be accompanied by two \ncopies of the latest issues of the newspaper sought to be registered, a list showing the \nnames and addresses of at least fifty bonafide subscribers and by a certificate from the \nDistrict, Presidency or Sub -Divisional Magistrate within whose local jurisdiction the \nnewspaper is printed or published or the printer or the publisher resides stating that the \ndeclaration or declarations required by Section 5 of the Press and Registration of Book \nAct, 1867 has or have been made or no such declaration is required under the said Act \nas the publication is not a newspaper according to the definition given in that Act.  \n(II)     PERIODICALS \nRule 17-B of the Indian Post Office Rules, 1933, framed under the powers given \nin the Indian Post Office Act, 1898, provides-that the special rates of postage mentioned \nin Rule 1 in respect of book packet containing periodicals shall be applicable only if the \nfollowing conditions are satisfied:- \n(a) That it is registered with the Registrar of Newspapers for India under the Press \nand Registration of Books Act, 1867 (25 of 1867). \n(b) That the periodical bears in print in any convenient place either on the first or \nlast page thereof, the superscription \"Registered with the Registrar of \nNewspapers for India under No ................ \" \nNew law against private phone-tapping \n1846. SHRI SHAHID SIDDIQUI: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that due to emerging advanced technologies mobile phone \ntapping and keeping surveillance on them is becoming very easy; \n(b) whether it would harm the security and integrity of our country; \n(c) the steps Government are taking to keep a watch on such problems; and \n(d) whether Government are planning to make some new law to stop this private \ntapping of phones? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MIN ISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) to (d) Lawful  \ninterception is carried under section 5(2) of the Indian Telegraph Act, 1885 in the  \ninterests of \" ...... the sovereignty and integrity of India, the security of the State, \n\n60     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nfriendly relations with foreign states or public order or for preventing incitement to the  \ncommission of an offence, for reason to be recorded in writing ....... \" The Rule 419A of \nthe Indian Telegraph R ules, 1951 framed under Indian Telegraph Act, 1885 lays down \nelaborate mechanism for lawful interception. As per the conditions of the telecom \nservice licences, the Licensee shall ensure protection of privacy of communication and \nensure that unauthorized interception of messages does not take place. \nIntroduction of VRS in BSNL \n1847. SHRI TAPAN KUMAR SEN: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS  \nAND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether there is any move to introduce Voluntary Retirement Scheme (VRS ) in \nBSNL; \n(b) the total number of employees targeted to be reduced through VRS; \n(c) whether the employees and their recognized union has been consulted on the \nsame; \n(d) if so, the outcome of the consultation; and \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN  THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) Sir, BSNL \nManagement is considering a Voluntary Retirement Scheme for its non -executive \nemployees. However, no VRS scheme has been approved so far. \n(b) No specific numbers have  been targeted for VRS. However, for assessing \nfinancial implications of the proposed VRS scheme, a tentative number of 20,000 has \nbeen taken. \n(c) and (d) Representatives of employees Union have been consulted a number of \ntimes on VRS in BSNL. However, employe es' union representatives are opposed to \nVRS in BSNL. \n(e) Does not arise in view of (c) and (d) above. \nContract and casual workers in BSNL \n1848. SHRI TAPAN KUMAR SEN: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS  \nAND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) the total number of contract and casual workers engaged in BSNL; \n(b) the numbers of those who are deployed in permanent and perennial jobs; \n(c) whether these workers are covered by PF, ESI and Minimum Wages Act; and  \n(d) the steps being taken to regularize the contract work ers engaged in \npermanent/perennial nature of jobs according to the rules of Government? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) Sir, 12830 contract \nand casual labourers are engaged in BSNL. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions    61 \n(b) No contract labourer is being engaged in jobs of permanent or perennial  \nnature. However, 718 casual labourers are being engaged in jobs of perennial nature.  \n(c) Yes, Sir. \n(d) Does not arise in view of (b) above. \nMeeting QoS parameters by service providers \n1849. SHRIMATI VIPLOVE THAKUR: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS \nAND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that no service provider has achieved all the benchmarks \nprescribed by TRAI for Quality of Service parameters for various services including \nWireline (Fixed Line) services in the country particularly in Himachal Pradesh;  \n(b) if so, the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether TRAI has recommended any action against the operators for n ot \ncomplying with the QoS parameters, so far; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; and \n(e) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) As per the \nPerformance Monitoring Report  of the Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI) \nfor the quarter ending 31.03.07, some of the service providers are meeting the \nbenchmarks for all the Quality of Service parameters in some of the service areas \nincluding Himachal Pradesh. \n(b) According to t he service providers, shortage of spectrum and number of Points \nof Interconnection (POI) are the key factor for degradation to performance and quality \nof service for wireless access. \n(c) to (e) As per Section 29 (penalty for contravention of direction of TRAI)  of \nTRAI Act, 1997, if a person violates the direction of the TRAI, such person shall be \npunishable with fine as per provisions in TRAI Act, 1997. TRAI has issued a direction \non 7th June, 2005 to all service providers to provide Interconnection on the requ est of \nthe interconnection seeker within 90 days of the applicable payments made by the \ninterconnection seeker. BSNL had gone to TDSAT against this direction. The matter is \npending with TDSAT and sub-judice. \nDownsizing of employees in DoP \n   †850. SHRI SATYAVRAT CHATURVEDI: \nSHRI MOTILAL VORA: \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) the decrease/increase in number of employees of the Department of Posts during \nthe last three years; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n62     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(b) the affects on efficiency due to decrease in number of employees in the \nDepartment; \n(c) whether according to Report of CAG business of Postal Department has halved \nin the last five years; \n(d) the loss occurred to Postal Department in 2005-06 and the reasons therefor; and \n(e) the steps being taken by Government to coverup the losses and probable amount \nof profit/loss? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) The decrease in the \nnumber of employees in the Department of Posts during the last three years is as under:   \nYear (as on 31st Actual Strength Decrease in number \nMarch) of Employees of Employees \n2003 262752 - \n2004 250714 12038 \n2005 246678 4036 \n2006 234187 12491 \n(b) Wherever the shortage of staff is faced, the work is managed by  \nredistribution and combination of duties. Also computerization and rationalization,  \nof procedure has helped in smoothing functioning of  the Department. In view of it  \ndecrease in number of employees has not affected the efficiency of the Department.  \n(c) No, Sir. \n(d) The deficit of the Department for the year 2005 -06 is Rs. 1209.88 crore. The said \ndeficit is due to the fact that nearly 89 percent o f the expenditure is incurred on \nmandatory items like, salary, pension of staff etc. Apart from it, rates of most of the \npostal services have not kept pace with the cost of operation. The Department is also \nproviding a host of services as part of Universal  Service Obligations as per the policy of \nthe Government. \n(e) The Department has been taking steps to increase the revenue through  \nintroduction of the new products, market development activities and adoption of  \ncustomer oriented strategies. Speed Post, Bus iness Post, Express Post, Retail Post  \netc. as premium services have been consistently growing. The Department of Posts  \nis also using its vast network for retailing of financial products and services  \nagency functions thereby adding to the Department's reven ue. Moreover,  \nDepartment has also included value added money transfer services to its portfolio  \nlike instant Money Order Service and International Money Transfer. The amount of  \ndeficit during the year 2006- 07 is Rs. 1249.52 crore and the probable amount of  \ndeficit during the year 2007-08 is estimated to be Rs. 1473.62 crore. \nLicence for broadband wireless services \n 1851. SHRI UDAY PRATAP SINGH: \nSHRI AMAR SINGH: \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions     63 \n(a) whether it is a fact that the Telecom Regulator had submitted its  \nrecommendations on Broadband Wireless Services during 2006; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the reasons for not taking any action on those r ecommendations and by when \nthese are likely to be accepted; and \n(d) the number of operators who are going to get the licence for the Broadband \nWireless Services and by when? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR . SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) to (d) Yes, Sir. \nTelecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI) has given its recommendations on \nspectrum for Broadband Wireless Services. The guidelines for allotment of Broadband \nWireless Access (BWA) spectrum are to be formulated tak ing into account all relevant \naspects, including these TRAI recommendations. \nSubmarine cable landing station \n1852. SHRI TAPAN KUMAR SEN: \nSHRI MOHAMMED AMIN: \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to refer to the answer to  Unstarred Question 3055 given in Rajya Sabha on \n3rd May, 2007 and state: \n(a) the name of the consultant appointed for the project; \n(b) whether the consultant has given its report; (c)if \nso, the details thereof; \n \n(d) if not, by when the report would be given; and \n(e) the tentative time schedule to start the project? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) M/s Datawave \nLimited of UK was appointed as phase -I consultant to examine feasibility/viability of \nthe project and Return on Investment. M/s Axiom of France has been appointed as \nphase-II consultant for providing assistance till the completion of the project. \n(b) to (d) M/s Datawave Limited of UK have submitted the report. M/s Axiom have \nalso submitted a d etailed analysis of Indian landing stations along with its \nrecommendations. The Desktop study of the proposed cable route is being done in \nparallel by M/s Axiom. \n(e) The tentative time schedule to start the project is January-February, 2008. \nGlobal auction of 3G spectrum \n1853. SHRI DHARAM PAL SABHARWAL: \nSHRI GIREESH KUMAR SANGHI: \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n\n64     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(a) whether Government are considering to opt for global auction of third generation \ntechnology popularly known as 3G spectrum; \n(b) if so, the details in this regard; \n(c) whether Government propose to hold any discussion with telecom operators on \n2G and 3G licences; and \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) to (d) The \nguidelines for allotment and use of 3G spectrum are to be formulated taking into account \nall relevant aspects. \nCustomer growth rate of BSNL \n1854.   SHRIMATI N.P. DURGA: \nSHRI TAPAN KUMAR SEN: \nSHRI MOHAMMED AMIN: \nWill the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that in the last four months of this year i.e. between March-\nJuly, 2007, BSNL c ould extend its customer base only by I million whereas other \nprivate players have been able to increase their base 4 or 5 times than BSNL which lead \nto slipping of BSNL to 4th Place from the 2nd; \n(b) if so, the reasons for not able to attract the customers by BSNL; \n(c) the percentage of customer growth rate of BSNL during the last three years vis -\na-vis private players; quarter-wise; and \n(d) the number of circles in which BSNL is in operation, State-wise? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) Sir, during the four \nmonth period (March -June, 2007) growth of customer base of BSNL and private \noperators was as follows: \nItem BSNL Private Operators \nAddition of wireline and 2.9 million 18.91 million \nwireless subscribers \nBSNL was at second position in terms of total number of wireless subscribers and \nretained first position in terms of wire line subscriber in the country as on 30.6.07.  \n(b) Reasons for lower increase in customer base of BSNL are as follows: \n(i)     Capacity constraints in GSM mobile equipment. \n(ii)    Poor supply of Fixed Wireless Terminals (FWT) by the vendor. \n(iii)   Surrender/disconnection of landline telephones by customers. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions     65 \n(c) As per the data reported by BSNL, MTNL and other private wireless operators, \nthe quarterly comparison between the growth rate of BSNL and private wireless \noperators are given in Statement -I and Wireline Operators are given in Statement -II \n(See below). \n(d) BSNL is pr oviding telephone services in 26 telecom circles in India. Details of \nstate-wise coverage with name of BSNL circle is given in Statement-Ill. \nStatement-I \nThe quarterly comparison between the growth \nrate of BSNL and private .wireless oper