\n77 Pali City Pali 4 \n78 Char Bhuja Road Rajsamand 4 \n79 Rajsamand Rajsamand 4 \n80 Sawai Madhopur Town Sawaimadhopur 4 \n81 Khandel Sikar 4 \n82 Kanwata Sikar 4 \n83 Ramgarh Shekhwati Sikar 4 \n84 Ringus Sikar 4 \n\nWritten Answers to [3D AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions     81  \n1 2 3 4 \n85 Swroopganj Sirohi 4 \n86 Sribijay Sriganganagar 4 \n87 Anup Garh Sriganganagar 4 \n88 Sadulsahar Sriganganagar 4 \n89 Sriganganagar Sriganganagar 4 \n90 C.T. Tonk Tonk 4 \n91 Vanasthali Vidyha Peeth Tonk 4 \n92 Tod Rai Singh Tonk 4 \n93 Bhinder Udaipur 4 \n94 Fateh Nagar Udaipur 4 \n95 Kanore Udaipur 4 \n96 Udaipur Surajpole Udaipur 4 \nList of offices in Rajasthan Circle computerised during 2006-07  \nSI. \nNo. \n1 \nName of Computerised \nPost Office \n2 \nName of District 3 \nNo. of Computers \nsupplied \n4 \n1 Govindgarh Alwar 3 \n2 Alawar RS Alwar 3 \n3 Air Port Sanganer Jaipur 3 \n4 M.I. Road Jaipur 3 \n5 SDM Hospital Jaipur 3 \n6 Krishna Mandir Jaipur 3 \n7 Raja Park Jaipur 3 \n8 Tilak Nagar Jaipur 3 \n9 Indira Bazar Jaipur 3 \n10 Kishan Pole Bazar Jaipur 3 \n11 Sector 11 Pratapnagar Jaipur 3 \n12 Ind. Estate Jaipur 3 \n13 Murlipura Jaipur 3 \n14 Amer Jaipur 3 \n15 Dwarikapuri Jaipur 3 \n16 Ashok Nagar Jaipur 3 \n17 S F S Mansarovar Jaipur 3 \n\n82     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions  \n1 2 3 4 \n18 PaliRS Pali 4 \n19 Chand Pole Bazar Jaipur 4 \n20 Rajasthan State Hotel Jaipur 4 \n21 Regional College Ajmer 4 \n22 Udaipur Nai Sarafa Udaipur 4 \n23 Hiran Magri Udaipur 4 \n24 Bhilwara City Bhilwara 4 \nCoin boxes of BSNL \n†l859. MISS ANUSUIYA UIKEY: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) the total number of coin boxes of Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL) and \nother private sector telephone companies which are working in the country; \n(b) the number of coins being collected approximately everyday in such boxes; \n(c) whether the public is not facing any problem for change due to collection of \ncoins in these boxes; and \n(d) the provisions made in order to stop collection of coins in telephone boxes? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) Sir, approximately \n9, 01, 600 coin box PCOs are working in BSNL as on 30.06.2007. Out of which 15,745 \nare BSNL owned and remaining 8,85,855 are owned by PCO franchisees. The \ninformation of PCOs in respect of private operators is not maintained by the \nGovernment. \n(b) On an average approximately 1,90,00,000 number of coins are estimated to be \ncollected everyday by BSNL in these Coin Boxes. \n(c) No, Sir. As the coins collected in Coin Boxes get circulated in the market. \n(d) Does not arise in view of (c) above. \nExpansion of mobile telephone services in Uttarakhand \n†1860. SHRI HARISH RAWAT: Will the Minister of COMMUNI CATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of towers to be established by Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited \n(BSNL) for the expansion of mobile telephone services in Almora, Pithoragarh and \nChampavat districts of Uttarakhand during the year 2007-08; \n(b) the percentage of the population of the above districts to be liable to get benefit \nof mobile telephone services after the establishment of these towers; and  \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions     83 \n(c) by when hundred per cent population of the above districts would be covered by \nmobile telephone services? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) Sir, Bharat Sanchar \nNigam Limited (BSNL) has planned for installation of mobile base stations in Almora, \nPithoragarh and Champawat districts of Uttarakhand during the year 2007- 08. Details \nare as follows:- \n1. Almora (16 towers) : Almora (3 towers), Amyariband, Bhanoti, Daula, \nDeghat, Dewal, Dol, Kausani, Mallimarai, Nail, Naugaon, Ranikhet (2 \ntowers), Tarikhet. \n2     Champawat (4 towers): Barkot, Champawat, Lohaghat, Ritha. \n3. Pithoragarh (10 towers): Baram, Berinag, Dedihat, Munsyari, Pithoragarh (4 \ntowers), Tallimirthi, Toli. \n(b) Presently approx 15.77% population of Almora district, 5.37% population of \nChampawat district and 16.76% population of Pithoragarh district is utilizing mobile \ntelephone services. After installation of towers mentioned in para (a) above, approx \n18.12% population of Almora district, 6.97% population of Champawat district and \n17.96% population of Pithoragarh district will be in a position to utilize the mobile \ntelephone services in the Uttarakhand State. \n(c) Having provided reach of its mobile ne twork up to significant number of Tehsil \nand Block Headquarters, BSNL has now planned to expand its mobile network across \nthe country including Uttarakhand progressively to cover villages having population \ngreater than 1000 during the next three phases dep ending upon techno -commercial \nfeasibility. \nRural direct exchange lines \n†l861. SHRIMATI SUSHMA SWARAJ: Will the Minister of \nCOMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether any agreement has been signed between telephone operators for Rural \nDirect Exchange Lines (RDELs); \n(b) if so, the names of such telephone operators; \n(c) whether any provision has made regarding giving subsidy to telephone operators \nfor this purpose; and \n(d) if so, the basis upon which this subsidy would be given? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) and (b) Universal \nService Obligation Fund (USOF) entered into an agreement with Bharat Sanchar Nigam \nLimited (BSNL), Reliance Infocomm Limited (RIL), Tata Teleserv ices Limited (TTL) \nand Tata Teleservices Limited (Maharashtra) (TTL-MH) for provision of \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n84     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nindividual Rural Household Direct Exchange lines ( RDELs) in 1685 commercially \nunviable Short Distance Charging Areas (SDCAs) in the country during the period \n01.04.2005 and 31.03.2007. \n(c) Out of a total of 2,647 SDCAs in the country, 1,685 SDCAs were identified for \nproviding subsidy support, where the cost of providing RDELs was more than the \nrevenue earned. The mode of subsidy was based on a front loaded payment and equated \nannual subsidy installments. \nWaiting list for cellular connections \n1862. SHRI SITARAM YECHURY: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS  \nAND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that there is long waiting list before BSNL for cellular \nconnections; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, circle-wise, till date; \n(c) whether it is also a fact that this long waiting list is because o f lack of handsets \nwith the BSNL; and \n(d) the steps being taken by Government to resolve this and provide  \nconnections to the subscribers? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) and (b) Si r, BSNL \nhas a waiting list of 10,283 Cellular mobile connections as on 31st July, 2007 in North \nEast-II Telecom Circle comprising of Manipur, Nagaland and Arunachal Pradesh States.  \n(c) No, Sir. The waiting list in North East -II Telecom Circle is due to non -\navailability of additional equipment capacity. \n(d) BSNL has ordered GSM equipment for capacity addition of 80,000 lines in \nNorth East-II Circle to wipe out the long waiting list and to provide new connections. \nAn Advance Purchase Order (APO) has already been issu ed to one of the successful \nbidder on 18.07.07. Unconditional acceptance of the APO will establish the price to \nenable issue of APO to other successful bidder. This will enable BSNL to provide more \nnew connections to the subscribers. \nIncreasing subscriber base of BSNL \n1863. SHRI SITARAM YECHURY: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS  \nAND INFORMATION TEGHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of cellular subscriber base of BSNL vis -a-vis the major private \noperators between June 2004-July 2007; \n(b) whether it is a fact that the BSNL subscriber base is dwindling over the period; \nand \n(c) if so, the reasons therefor and the corrective measures taken by  \nGovernment? \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions     85 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CO MMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) Sir, details of cellular \nGSM subscriber base of BSNL vis -a-vis major private operators between June, 2004 to \nJuly, 2007 are given in the Statement (See below). \n(b) and (c) Bharat Sanchar Niga m Limited (BSNL) has generally been registering a \ncomparable growth for mobile subscribers as that of private operators in the past. \nHowever, there has been decline in growth at present due to GSM equipment capacity \nconstraints and impediment in the procur ement efforts for almost six months, as one of \nthe bidder had approached the Hon'ble High Court against the decision of BSNL \nrejecting his bid. BSNL has already placed an Advance Purchase Order (APO) to one of \nthe successful bidder. It is expected that APO  will be accepted soon and enable BSNL \nto be competitive in the market and regain its market share. \nStatement \nOperator-wise GSM connections from June 2004 to July 2007  \nSI. Operator  As on  \nNo. June 2004 June 2005 June 2006 June 2007 \n1. Bharti 7375335 11646068 23072579 44763391 \n2 Hutchison Essar 3846572 5594665 10021846 32437084 \n3. Idea 2955528 5552347 8535443 17004327 \n4. BPL Mobile 2148393 2629251 3267385 1094569 \n5. Aircel 1171814 1862044 2713889 7162100 \n6. Reliance 881710 1362932 2316771 4347593 \n7. Fascel 1022195 1314041 2689159 0 \n8. Spice 1302997 1473350 2054627 3290395 \n9. Aircel Digital Link 901826 1532081 2863247 0 \n10. Hexacom 296788 610216 0 0 \n11. Escotel 1089915 0 0 0 \n12. Dishnet Wireless 0 0 484928 0 \n13. Escorts Telecom 0 0 1632 0 \n14. BSNL 5793800 10230029 18297081 28974819 \n15. MTNL 416873 1112980 2169661 2668973 \nTOTAL: 29203746      44920004              78488248      141743251 \n \n\n86     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nClosure/shifting of post offices \n1864. SHRI S.S. AHLUWALIA: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that a number of post offices, particularly in districts and \nsemi-urban areas have been reduced by closing and/or shifting out from  their age old \naddresses to distant places or merger with major post offices and so on; \n(b) if so, the details thereof for the last five years, year-wise; \n(c) the reasons therefor; and \n. \n(d) the steps being taken to ensure that citizens especially elder/infirm, wh o had  \nbeen dependent upon the post offices in close proximity for communication/small  \nsaving and other services were not put to much inconvenience due to such  \nclosure/shifting etc.? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) Yes, Sir. The number \nof post offices has slightly reduced due to closure and merger of some of the post \noffices. \n(b) The details of post offices closed, relocated/shifted and merged from 2002 -03 to \n2006-07 are given in the Statement (See below). \n(c) Closure of post offices is resorted to in very rare and unavoidable circumstances \ne.g. completion of construction work of a dam, dilapidated accommodation, court orders \nfor vacation of buildings and availability of post offices in close  proximity. Further, \nsome of the post offices, which were no longer justified in their original locations, were \nrelocated to other needy areas for catering to the postal requirements of those areas \nwhere there was demand and justification for postal facili ties. Merger of some of the \npost offices has taken place for providing better postal facilities through bigger post \noffices. The Department is committed to fulfill its Universal Service Obligations. While \nfulfilling these obligations, relocation/merger of some of the post offices had become a \nnecessity for serving some under -served areas without affecting access to postal \nfacilities. \n(d) Before relocating/merging any post office, care has been taken to ensure that \nother post offices are available in the vicinit y and that level of access to postal network \nis not affected so that the citizens, especially elder/infirm are not put to much \ninconvenience. The communication/small savings facilities are provided through nearby \npost offices so that inconvenience is not c aused to the citizens. The interests of the \ncustomers have been kept in mind before any such closure, relocation/shifting and \nmerger of post offices. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions     87 \nStatement \nNumber of post offices closed, relocated/shifted and \nmerged in districts/semi-urban areas  \nSI. Year  No. of Post Offices Closed  \nNo.   In urban In rural          In semi-urban Total \n  areas areas             areas  \n1 2002-03 2 2 Nil 4 \n2 2003-04 7 3 Nil 10 \n3 2004-05 15 5 Nil 20 \n4 2005-06 14 2 Nil 16 \n5 200647 6 2 1 9 \n TOTAL: 44 14 1 59 \n  No. of Post Offices Relocated   \n1 2002-03 3 1 Nil 4 \n2 2003-04 21 6 1 28 \n3 200445 58 10 1 69 \n4 2005-06 49 13 2 64 \n5 200647 59 16 Nil 75 \n Total: 190 46 4 240 \n  No. of Post Offices Merged   \n1 2002-03 14 Nil Nil 14 \n2 2003-04 71 9 1 81 \n3 2004-05 144 10 1 155 \n4 2005-06 82 3 1 86 \n5 200647 111 13 2 132 \n TOTAL: 428 35 5 468 \nPost and telegraph offices in rented buildings in Punjab \n1865.    SHRI    VARINDER   SINGH   BAJWA:    Will    the    Mi nister   of \nCOMMUNICATIONS AND INFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of post and telegraph offices other than non -departmental ones in the \nState of Punjab at present which are housed in rented buildings; \n\n88     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(b) whether there is any proposal under consideration of the department to own  \ntheir own buildings; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof and the budget allocation for the purpose for the  \nyear 2007-08? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN TH E MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) At present 589 post \noffices and 13 Central Telegraph Offices/Telegraph Offices/Telecommunications \nCentres are housed in rented buildings in Punjab. However, BSNL have planned to shift \none Telegraph Office functioning at Sangrur from rented building to its own building. \n(b) Yes, Sir. There is a scheme under Eleventh Five Year Plan to provide funds  \nfor construction of post office buildings in the Department of Posts. \nAs regards t elegraph offices there is no such proposal under consideration in the \nDepartment of Telecommunications. \n(c) No funds for new projects have been allocated for the year 2007- 08 in the  \nDepartment of Posts, as the proposal for new projects for Eleventh Five Year Plan  \nhas not been approved as yet. However, an amount of Rs. 1393.40 lakhs has been  \nallotted all over the country for 2007- 08 to complete construction of ongoing post  \noffice projects of Tenth Five Year Plan, out of which Rs.85.50 lakhs has been  \nallotted to Punjab. \nAs regards telegraph offices, the question does not arise in view of (b) above. \nProcurement of switching equipment \n1866. SHRI NANDI YELLAIAH: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether delays in procurement of switching equipment to augment capacity of \nCell One's GSM (Global Standard for Mobile Communication) has led to the Public \nSector Undertaking BSNL slipping to the No.3 position in the cellular industry as Airtel \ncontinued to be No.l and Vodafone moving to No.2 position; \n(b) whether 45.5 million BSNL Cell One connections could not be given, due to \ndelay in procuring switching equipment; and \n(c) if so, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) to (c) Sir, equipment \nprocuremtmt efforts for BSNL's new mobile network expansion project received a \nsetback with one of the bidders approaching the Hon'ble High Court against the decision \nof BSNL rejecting his bid. Due to dela y in procurement efforts and capacity constraints, \nBSNL growth has been comparatively lesser resulting in BSNL slipping to 3rd position \namong GSM operators. However, BSNL has now placed an Advanced Purchase Order \n(APO) on the successful bidder. It is expected that APO will be accepted soon and \nenable BSNL to be competitive in the market and regain its market share. \n\nWritten Answers, to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions     89 \nIssue of identity cards \n1867. SHRI NANDI YELLAIAH: Will the Minister of COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government would instruct the BSNL authorities in empowering \nDistrict Telecommunication Authorities in India to issue identity cards to the non -\nofficial Telecommunication Advisory Committee's M embers, mentioning the period for \nwhich they are nominated in view of Members facing humiliating situations before \nBSNL employees, when they visit various BSNL offices; \n(b) if not, the reasons therefor; \n(c) whether Government would raise the present allowance of R s. 200/- to at least \nRs. 500/ - which is the minimum taxi/car fare required for each sitting of every local \nnon-official member of Telephone Advisory Committee; and \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF COMMUNICATIONS AND \nINFORMATION TECHNOLOGY (DR. SHAKEEL AHMAD): (a) and (b) No,  Sir. In \nthe existing guidelines, there is no provision of issue of Identity cards to TAC members.  \n(c) and (d) No, Sir. The local non -official members are required to make local \njourney only to reach TAC meeting venue and existing ceiling appears to be quite \nenough to meet the expenses of local journey. \nEstimation of tiger population by NBWL \n1868. SHRI B.K. HARIPRASAD: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the on -going estimation of tig er population in the country by the \nNational Board of Wildlife (NBWL) has indicated that only 1300 -1500 tigers are left in \nthe country; \n(b) whether a study by the Wildlife Institute of India has established that much of \nthe loss in tiger population is in forests outside the national parks and sanctuaries; \n(c) whether the conservationists allege that the Forest Rights Act in its present form \nis not conducive to conservation of wildlife; and \n(d) if so, Government's comments thereon? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI S. REGUPATHY): (a) and (b) The All India estimation of tigers and \nother wild animals, including habitat status evaluation, is ongoing as per the revised, \nenhanced methodology standardized by the National Tiger Conservation Authority \n(erstwhile Project Tiger) and the Wildlife Institute of India, as endorsed by the Tiger \nTask Force from November, 2005. So far, the tiger estimation figures relating to only \nfour States (Rajasthan, Chhattisgarh, Madhya Pradesh and Maharashtra) ha ve been \nfinalized, which indicate a decline in tiger population in areas outside tiger reserves and \nprotected areas. \n\n90     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(c) and (d) The Ministry of Environment and Forests has taken steps for \nsafeguarding various concerns of wildlife in the rules being framed under \"The \nScheduled Tribes and other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) \nAct, 2006\" \nTargets for afforestation \n1869. SHRI M.V. MYSURA REDDY: Will the PRIME MINISTER be ple ased to \nstate: \n(a) whether Government have fixed any target for afforestation in the country; and \n(b) if so, the details of progress and achievements made in this respect? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI S. REGUPATHY): (a) an d (b) The targets for tree planting were \nbeing, fixed under two mutually exclusive parameters, namely Point 16 A (Tree \nplantation on private lands -number of trees planted) and Point 16 B (Area covered-\npublic and forest land, Hectares) of the Twenty Point P rogramme-1986. The target and \nachievement during 2006-07 were as under:-  \nYear Point 16 A (Tree plantation on \nprivate lands) \nNumber of Trees planted in \nlakhs \nPoint 16 B (Area covered under \nplantation-public and forest \nlands) Area in hectare, \n Target                  Achievement  \n \n15010                  13218.49 \nTarget                  Achievement \n2006-07 18,70,000            1,16,55,727 \nThe targets for tree planting under revised Twenty Point Programme -2006 for the \nyear 2007-08 areas given below:- \nPoint 52 (a) : Area covered under plantation public and forest land- 18,60,000 \nhectare. \nPoint 52 (b) : Number  of seedlings  planted  on  public  and  forest  land (Number): - \n12,090 Lakh \nNational environment mission \n1870. SHRI M. V. MYSURA REDDY: Will the PRIME  MINISTER be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether Government have launched any National Environment Mission in the \ncountry; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) if not, the time-frame within which the mission would be launched? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENV IRONMENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a) No, Sir. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions    91 \n(b) Does not arise. \n(c) There is no proposal at present to launch national environment mission. \nHowever, a nation -wide campaign called the Nati onal Environment Awareness \nCampaign (NEAC) is conducted every year with the objective of creating \nenvironmental awareness at the national level. Eco -clubs in schools are also supported \nunder the National Green Corps programme regularly. \n'Vangrams' in M.P. \ntl871. SHRI LAXMINARAYAN SHARMA: Will the PRIME MINISTER be \npleased to state: \n(a) the number of 'Vangrams' situated in Madhya Pradesh alongwith the action \nbeing taken in respect of transferring these 'Vangrams' into revenue villages of the State \nof Madhya Pradesh; and \n(b) by when these 'Vangrams' would be notified as revenue villages? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI S. REGUPATHY) (a) and (b) There are 893 'Vangrams' (forest \nvillages) situated in the State of Madhya Pradesh. Go vernment of India, has accorded \nin-principle approval for the conversion of 310 'Vangrams' into Revenue villages. \nHowever, further action in this regard could not be taken up due to the Hon'ble Supreme \nCourts orders dated 13.11.2000 banning de -reservation of forests. In view of the orders \nof the Hon'ble Supreme Court dated 13.11.2000, the State Government of Madhya \nPradesh has been advised that the proposals for conversion of forest villages into \nrevenue villages shall be processed by the Central Government  only after the State \nGovernment of Madhya Pradesh obtains the permission of the Hon'ble Supreme Court, \nor after a final judgment is delivered in the matter by the Hon'ble Court. \nControlling pollution \n1872. SHRIMATI HEMA MALINI: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether Government are aware that species one after another are being wiped \nout, forest cover is dwindling and pollution is becoming a major health hazard;  \n(b) if so, whether Government are considering any firm step to conserve on war \nfooting, rebuild rural forestry and empowering pollution control board to proactively \nregulate pollution in order to provide clean water, air to all living being including all \ncitizens; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI S. REGUPATHY): (a) Pollution is becoming health hazard but forest \ncover is not dwindling in India. There have been reports that over the past fifty years, \nthere has been a loss in the biodiversity throughout the World. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n92     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(b) and (c) The Government has taken following measures to conserve flora and \nfauna of the country and to regulate the pollution:- \n• Survey and inventor isation of floral and fauna resources are carried out by \nBotanical Survey of India (BSI) and Zoological Survey of India (ZSI). The \nForest Survey of India assesses the forest cover to develop an accurate \ndatabase for planning and monitoring purposes. \n• A Prot ected Area Network of 96 National Parks, and 509 Wildlife \nSanctuaries and three Conservation Reserves, covering more than 4.74% of \nthe .geographical area of the country has been created for in -situ \nconservation of habitats and ecosystems. \n• Fourteen biodiver sity rich areas have been designated as Biosphere \nReserves for conservation of representative ecosystems. \n• Species oriented programmes, namely, Project Tiger, Project -Elephant and \nProject Rhino focussing on single species as well as for conserving their \nhabitats and a variety of other species in those habitats are being \nimplemented. \n• To complement in -situ conservation efforts, ex- situ conservation has been \nundertaken through botanic gardens, zoos, gene banks etc. \n• Biological Diversity Act 2002 has been enacted  and Biological Diversity \nRules 2004 have been notified, which aim at conservation of biological \nresources of the country and regulation of access to these resources to \nensure equitable sharing of benefits arising out of their use. \n• Notification to general and industry specific emission and effluent \nstandard. \n• Industries to obtain \"Consent for Establishment\" as well as \"Consent to \nOperate\" under the provisions of the Water (Prevention and Control of \nPollution) Act, 1974 and the Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act, \n1981 from the concerned State Pollution Control Boards (SPCBs) prior to \ncarrying out operations. \n• Environmental Impact Assessment of developmental projects and \npreparation of Environmental Management Plan as per the provisions of the \nEnvironmental Impact Assessment notification of September, 2006. \n• Adoption of cleaner technologies and use of improved fuel quality. \n• Regular monitoring of industrial units for environmental compliance. \n• Issuance of directions in cases of industries not complying wi th \nenvironmental norms. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions     93 \nMercury pollution \n1873. SHRI VEDPRAKASH P. GOYAL: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Government are planning to set up 50 Mercury Dis posal \nCentres across the country to stop mercury pollution; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, State-wise; and \n(c) if not, the step being taken to stop mercury pollution? \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND FORESTS \n(SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a) to (c ) For evolving policy for safe management \nof mercury from Compact Fluorescent Lamps (CFLs) including safety in manufacture, \nusage and disposal and creation of public awareness a Task Force has been constituted \nby the Ministry of Environment and Forests on 14.8.2007. A Technical Committee has \nalso been constituted by the Central Pollution Control Board on 27th August, 2007 to \nrecommend measures, standards and guidelines for safe usage and disposal of CFLs.  \nPamba action plan \n1874. PROF. P.J. KURIEN: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) the year in which Pamba Action Plan in the State of Kerala was approved by \nUnion Government; \n(b) the details of the Action Plan; \n(c) the details of implementation till  date; and \n(d) the reasons for the inordinate delay? \nTHE MINISTER  OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a) to (d) The project for abatement of \npollution of River Pamba at Sabrimala and Pamba in Kerala was approved for an \namount of Rs. 18.45 crore as Pamba Action Plan under Nation al River Conservation \nPlan (NRCP) in May, 2003. The works included under the Plan are; construction of \nsewage treatment plants, bathing ghats, community toilets and solid waste management. \nThe Central and State Government's share in the project cost is Rs.  12.92 crore and \nRs.5.53 crore. Against the Central share, funds amounting to Rs.1.75 crore have been \nreleased to Government of Kerala for execution of approved works. The State \nGovernment has reported an expenditure of about Rs.85.0 lakhs only towards \nimplementation of the plan. The execution of sanctioned work is slow on account of \nnon-availability of forests land. According to State Government, the process of \nacquisition of land is in progress. \nContamination of Yamuna \n1875. SHRI RAJKUMAR DHOOT: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the water of Yamuna river is contaminated to such an \nextent that it is harmful even to sericulture and vegetables irrigated by its water;  \n\n94     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the steps being taken to ensure that only treated water from sewage is allowed to \nreach, Yamuna in Delhi; and \n(d) if so, the target set to complete this work? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND \nFORESTS (SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a) and (b) As per the Central Pollution \nControl Board, the entire Yamuna river from its origin i.e. Yamnotri to its confluence \nwith river Ganga at Allahabad is not entirely contaminated. However, a few stretches of \nriver Yamuna are contaminated due to disposal of waste water from industries and \ndomestic sewage. The water quality is deteriorated at polluted stretches from Wazirabad \nto Etawah due to higher bio -chemical oxygen demand value. As per primary water \nquality criteria, the  river water at polluted stretches is considered to be fit for the \npurpose of irrigation. \n(c) and (d) According to the information made available by the Delhi Jal Board to \nensure discharge of treated effluent into the river Yamuna, it is envisaged to imple ment \nthe works namely, laying of interceptor sewers along major drains, augmentation of \nsewage treatment plants, interception of small drains, rehabilitation of trunk sewers and \nconstruction of additional sewage treatment plants in a time bound manner. \nIn addition Yamuna Action Plans (YAP) have been taken up by Government of \nIndia with the assistance of the Japan Bank for International Cooperation in a phased \nmanner for undertaking pollution abatement works in the river Yamuna. Under the \nphase-I of YAP laun ched in April, 1993, 753.25 million litres per day (mld) of sewage \ntreatment capacity was created, of which 30 mld in Delhi. YAP -I was declared closed in \nFebruary, 2003. \nUnder the YAP-II, which commenced in December, 2004 at an approved cost of Rs. \n624 crore, major portion of the outlay i.e. Rs. 387.17 crores, has been allocated to Delhi. \nA sewage treatment capacity of 135 mld is proposed to be created under YAP -II in \nDelhi. In addition, projects for the rehabilitation of 324 mld treatment capacity and \nrehabilitation/laying of trunk sewers for a length of 30.82 kms. are also envisaged under \nthe YAP-II. \nGiving degraded forest land to industries \n† l876. SHRI RUDRA NARAYAN PANY: \nSHRI SHREEGOPAL VYAS: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that less fertile or degraded forest land is proposed to be \ngiven to the industries on contract basis; \n(b) if so, the terms thereof; \n(c) whether industries would safeguard bio-diversity; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions    95 \n(d) whether this policy would also be applicable on rural land; and \n(e) whether Ministry of Rural Development and Employment has also been consulted \nin this regard? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY ENVIRONMENT AND FORESTS (SHRI S. \nREGHUPATHY): (a) The Ministry of Environment and Forests has mooted a Multi -\nStakeholder Partnership (MSP) framework for forestation on degraded lands in the country. \nThe proposed framework envisages a tripartite arrangement between the land owing agency, \nthe local village community and the sponsor who may be a company, firm, user group, trust, \nsociety or organization in public or private sector. \n(b) and (c) Under the above mooted MSP framework, the terms of such Memorandum \nof Understanding (MoU) for each case are to be decided by the stakeholders together while \nsafeguarding the ecology of the area and economic and socio- cultural interests of the \nlocal communities, traditionally dependent on such lands. \n(d) and (e) The above mooted MSP framework has been drafted in consultation with \nvarious stakeholders and the draft has been circulated to concerned Central Government \nMinistries, including the Ministry of Rural Development. \nRSPM in metro cities  \n   † l877. SHRI MOTILAL VORA: \n SHRI SATYAVRAT CHATURVEDI: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER pleased to refer to the answer to Unstarred Question \n2796 given in the Rajya Sabha on 27th April, 2007 and state: \n(a) the quantity of Respirable Suspended Particulate Matter (RSPM) in excess of the \nprescribed norms in Delhi, Mumbai and Kolkata metro cities; \n(b) the efforts made by Government so far to reduce RSPM; \n(c) whether it is also a fact that strict excretory norms are not being implemented for \ncontrolled pollution certificate system and diesel generator set in many States; and \n(d) the quantity of Respirable Suspended Particulate Matter (RSPM) likely to be reduced \nas a result of Government's efforts? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT AND FORESTS \n(SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a) to (d) The Respirable Suspended Particulate Matter \n(RSPM) levels have been observed to exceed the prescribed Ambient Air Quality \nStandards of 60 microgramme per cubic metre in residential areas of Delhi, Mumbai and \nKolkata. \n2. The Government has taken various steps to reduce and abate air p ollution \nwhich, inter-alia, include enforcement of strict auto exhaust standards  for new  \nvehicles  at the manufacturing  stage;  use of \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n96     Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nimproved fuel quality for vehicles, Pollution Under Control (PUC) \ncertificate system for in-use vehicles stringent diesel generator set emission \nnorms,. use of natural gas as fuel for public transport and thermal power \nplants in Delhi and Mumbai. In additio n, source apportionment studies \nhave been initiated in 6 cities including Delhi and Mumbai for ascertaining \ncontribution to air pollution from various sources. \n3. The vehicle inspection programme called PUC Certificate system is \nenforced under the Central Mot or Vehicle Rules, 1991 and the Regional \nTransport Authorities in various States are responsible for conducting \nregular emission tests for in-use vehicles. \n4. The Ministry of Road Transport and Highways has notified the revised in -\nuse emission norms on 10th Fe bruary, 2004 for their implementation from \n1st October, 2004. Four' gases in exhaust emission of in- use vehicles are \nanalysed for compliance of revised standards. Nine metro cities including \nDelhi, Mumbai and Kolkata have implemented the improved PUC syste m \nto check emission from in-use vehicles. \nSewage treatment plants \n1878.   SHRI BALAVANT ALIAS BAL APTE: \nSHRI VINAY KATIYAR: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the setting up of Sewage Treatment Plants under the Ganga and \nYamuna Action Plans is obligatory; \n(b) if so, the total number of STPs set up under the G.A.P. and Y.A.P., State-wise; \n(c) the costs involved in setting up of such plants and sources of funds mobilised \ntherefor; \n(d) whether the Union Government provides funds for such plants under any  \nscheme; \n(e) if so, the details thereof, State/UT-wise; \n(f) whether these plants are fully complying with the prescribed operational norms; \n(g) if so, the details thereof, and \n(h) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF ENVIRONMENT A ND \nFORESTS (SHRI NAMO NARAIN MEENA): (a; to (e) Sewage Treatment Plants \n(STPs) have been set up in different identified towns as one of the core pollution \nabatement schemes under the Ganga Action Plan (GAP) and Yamuna Action Plan \n(YAP). These plants are meant for treating the intercepted and diverted sewage to the \ndesired standards before its disposal into the receiving body. The State-wise \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions     97 \nstatus of STPs sanctioned and completed under GAP and YAP is at Statement-I {See below). \nThe Government of India has sanctioned pollution abatement schemes (including setting up of \nSTPs) under different River Action Plans at an estimated cost of Rs. 4260 crore on a cost sharing basis \nbetween Government of India  and the respective State Government. The State/UT -wise details of the \nsame are at Statement-II (See below). \n(f) to (h) As per a report of Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB 2006), there are 231 Sewage \nTreatment Plants (STPs) at present working in Class -I, Class -II cities and other smaller towns in the \ncountry. The notified effluent standards for STPs for discharge in surface water bodies are: Biochemical \nOxygen Demand - 30 miligrams per litre and Total Suspended Solids —100 miligrams per litre. Based \non the performance evaluation of 115 STPs carried out by CPCB, 70 STPs were reported to achieve the \nstapdards for discharge in surface water. The main reasons for non -compliance of STPs with the \nprescribed standards, is the poor operation and maintenance of t hese plants which is the responsibility \nof the respective State Government or the implementing agencies. \nStatement-I \nState-wise progress under GAP-l and under NRCP  \nSI. No.     Plan/State Sewagt  Treatment Plants \n Sanctioned Completed Ongoing \nGanga Action Plan Phase-I    \n1              Uttar Pradesh 13 13 0 \n2             Bihar 7 6 1 \n3              West Bengal 15 15 0 \nSUB TOTAL: 35 34 1 \nGanga Action Plan Phase-II    \n1             Bihar 0 0 0 \n2             Jharkhand 0 0 0 \n3             Uttar Pradesh 8 1 7 \n4             Uttranchal 10 2 8 \n5             West Bengal 29 10 19 \nSUB TOTAL: 47 13 34 \nYamuna Action Plan Phase-I and II    \n1              Delhi 8 7 1 \n2             Haryana 27 27 0 \n3             Uttar Pradesh 17 15 2 \nSUB TOTAL: 52 49 3 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\nStatement-II \nStatus of progress under GAP phase-l and national river conservation plan \n                                                                                               (As on 31-06-2007)                        (Rs. in lakh)  \n  Action \nPlan/ \nState \nEstimated \nCost \nNo. of         Cost of SCH.             \nSCR Santd.           Santd. 30-\n06-2007 \nNo. of \nSchemes \nCom. \n(31-03-2007) \nFunds \nReleased \nbyGOI \n(30-06-2007) \nFunds \nReleased \nduring \n2007-08 \nExpdt. \nIncurred \n(by State) \n(31-03-2007) \n  1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 \nA  Ganga Action Plan Phase-I        \n 1 Delhi 19995 106 19125.52 106 19012 0 19445.84 \n 2 Uttar Pradesh 5729 45 5401.19 45 5355 0 5272.81 \n 3 Haryana (OECF) 18805 110 18562.09 110 18560 0 18613.38 \n  SUB TOTAL: 44529 261 43088.8 261 42927 0 43332.03 \n  Establishment 1675  2243  2243   \n  TOTAL (GAP-I): •46204 261 45331.8 261 45170 0 43332.03 \nB  Ganga Action Plan Phase-II (Ganga river and tributaries)     \n(1)  Yamuna Action P