es; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF HOUSING AND URBAN \nPOVERTY ALLEVIATION (KUMARI SELJA): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) and (c) In the ru ral areas, Government has launched the National Rural \nEmployment Guarantee Scheme (NREGS) in February, 2006. A large number of \nDistricts have been covered under the Scheme. The objective of the Scheme is to \nenhance the livelihood security of the people in rural areas by generating wage \nemployment through works that develop the infrastructure base of the area.  \nIndian work force in Middle East \n1911. DR. K. MALAISAMY: Will the Minister of OVERSEAS INDIAN AFFAIRS  \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that G overnment have signed an Agreement with the Middle -\nEast countries to protect the interest of our work force there; \n(b) if so, the countries with which such agreements have been entered into; \n(c) whether the terms of agreement are implemented in letter and spirit; and \n(d) the reason for this kind of agreement not being resorted to in Malaysia, \nSingapore and Sri Lanka where there are huge number of workers of Indian origin? \nTHE MINISTER OF OVERSEAS INDIAN AFFAIRS (SHRI VAYALAR RAVI): \n(a) and (b) Yes Sir, the Government h as signed MoU with United Arab Emirates and \nKuwait. The Ministry has finalized an Additional Protocol with Qatar to update the 1985 \nAgreement. \n(c) Yes, Sir. \n(d) MoU with Malaysia has been finalized. MoUs are also being pursued with \ncountries which have large popul ation of Indian emigrant workers. There is no proposal \nto sign MoU with Singapore and Sri Lanka due to small size of emigrant flows to these \ncountries. \nCases under Emigration ACT/IPC \n1912. DR. GYAN PRAKASH PILANIA: Will the Minister of OVERSEAS INDIAN  \nAFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) the cases registered under the Emigration Act/IPC for illegal trafficking during \nthe last five years and their disposal; \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions   133 \n(b) the outcome of cases registered against Rakesh Kumar and Daler Mehandi: \n(c) the role and performance of the Protector of Emigrants to check illegal export of \nmanpower from India; and \n(d) the steps Government propose to take to check racket of Kabootarbaji? \nTHE MINISTER OF OVERSEAS INDIAN AFFAIRS (SHRI VAYAL AR RAVI): \n(a) to (d) Information is being collected and will be laid on the table of the house. \nRegistration of NRI marriages \n1913. DR. GYAN PRAKASH PILANIA: Will the Minister of OVERSEAS INDIAN \nAFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Standing Committee o n External Affairs has recommended that \nregistration of NRI marriages be made compulsory and stringent action be taken against \ncorrupt Protectorate of Emigrants offices (PoE) and fraudulent recruiting agents;  \n(b) if so, the follow up action thereon; \n(c) the statistics pertaining to incidents of failed and fraudulent marriages of Indian \nwomen with NRIs; and \n(d) the number of Indian workers in Middle -East and South -East countries and \nscenario of their exploitation and harassment? \nTHE MINISTER OF OVERSEAS INDIAN AFFAIRS ( SHRI VAYALAR RAVI): \n(a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) On the issue of compulsory registration of marriages the Ministry of  \nOverseas Indian Affairs (MOIA) has drawn the attention of all the States/UTs to a  \nrecent judgement of the Hon'ble Supreme Court in the matter and req uested them  \nto make relevant laws for the same. \nMOIA has taken various steps to improve the emigration process and make it more \ntransparent which include upgradation of 3 posts of Protector of Emigrants (PoE) to \nDeputy Secretary level, conducting periodic vigilance audit of POE offices, \ncomputerization of offices of the Protector General of Emigrants (PGE) and PoEs, \ninitiation of disciplinary proceedings against PoE officials found involved in corrupt \npractices and rationalization of procedure for processin g of fresh applications and \nrenewal of licenses of recruiting agents. \n(c) The Ministry has received about 168 cases relating to failed and fraudulent \nmarriages with overseas Indian spouses. \n(d) The number of Indian Workers in the Middle -East and South -East countries is \nestimated at about five million. \nThe main problems faced by Indian emigrant workers in foreign countries relate to: \n(i)     non-payment/reduced/delayed payment of salaries, \n(ii)    non deployment on promised jobs, \n\n134   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(iii)   adverse working conditions, (iv)   denial \nof appropriate medical facilities, (v)    poor \nliving conditions, etc. \nWomen marrying NRIs \n1914. SHRI T.T.V. DHINAKARAN: Will the Minister of OVERSEAS INDIAN \nAFFAIRS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether there is any proposal to enact a law to protect women marrying Non -\nResident Indians; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF OVERSEAS INDIAN AFFAIRS (SHRI VAYALAR RAVI): \n(a) to (c) No Sir, there is no prop osal under consideration to enact a separate legislation \nto protect the women marrying NRIs. However, a proposal to enact a suitable legislation \nrelating to compulsory registration of marriages is under consideration of the Ministry of \nLaw and Justice (Legislative Department). \nCorruption in Panchayats \n1915. MS. MABEL REBELLO: Will the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be  \npleased to state: \n(a) whether the Government are aware that Sarpanchs and the Gram Sewaks/ \nPanchayat Secretary are indulging in wholesale corruption in implementation of various \nschemes of Government such as road construction, NREGA, and many other \ndevelopment works; \n(b) in what manner Government propose to motivate the Sarpanchs who work truly \nfor the development of the area; and \n(c) in what manner Government  would motivate the State Governments to hold \nPanchayat elections in States like Jharkhand where Panchayat elections have not been \nheld for the last couple of decades? \nTHE MINISTER OF PANCHAYATI RAJ (SHRI MANI SHANKAR AIYAR): (a) \nIn view of the provisions of Article 243G of the Constitution, the processes of endowing \nthe Panchayats and their Presidents with powers and authority has been entrusted to \nState Legislatures and the State executive. This would include monitoring and correcting \nthe work of the Village Panchayat President and officers working with the Village \nPanchayat. As far as NREGA and other schemes of the Ministry of Rural Development \nare concerned, District Vigilance and Monitoring Committees have been established \nunder the chairpersonship of Members of Parliament. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   135 \n(b) The responsibility for motivating Sarpanches primarily vests in State \nGovernments. The Union Government in the Ministry of Panchayati Raj assists the \nprocess by making avail able funds under its Capacity -building and Training Schemes. \nA proposal is under process for providing sufficient funds to the Panchayats \nEmpowerment and Accountability Incentivization Scheme to incentivize States to \neffectively endow the Panchayats with F unctions, Finances and Functionaries and to \nhold the Panchayats accountable to their respective Gram Sabhas for the efficient and \nhonest delivery of services and works. \n(c) As per Article 243E of the Constitution, all States and Union Territories covered \nunder Part IX of the Constitution are required to hold elections regularly within five \nyears. By and large, elections to Panchayats have been held regularly in all States/UTs \nexcept Jharkhand. There is litigation pending before the Hon'ble Supreme Court in \nregard the Panchayati Raj system in Jharkhand. The matter is being pursued for an early \nhearing. \nImplementation of schemes through Panchayats \n † l916. SHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA:       \nDR. MURLI MANOHAR JOSHI: \nWill the Minister of PANCHAYATI RAJ be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that Government have considered to implement the schemes \ndeclared by Central Government through Panchayats; \n(b) if so, the facts in this regard and its outline; \n(c) the States where Panchayats have duly been constituted; and \n(d) by whe n the target of constituting the same properly in the remaining States \nwould be met? \nTHE MINISTER OF PANCHAYATI RAJ (SHRI MANI SHANKAR AIYAR): (a) \nand (b) In conformity with the letter and spirit of Part IX of the Constitution, read with \nArticle 243 ZD and  the Eleventh Schedule, these Centrally Sponsored Schemes (CSS) \ncovering the 29 subjects listed in the Eleventh Schedule of the Constitution are to be \nplanned and implemented by Panchayati Raj Institutions functioning as \"Institutions of \nLocal Self-Government\". While the guidelines in respect of some CSS, provide for the \ncentrality of Panchayati Raj Institutions (PRIs), there are other CSS where the PRIs are \nyet to assign a central role. In order to clarify and ensure the role of the PRIs in all CSS, \nconsultations are being held with the Central Ministries concerned under the aegis of \nthe Cabinet Secretariat. \n(c) and (d) Panchayats in all States and Union Territories (UTs), covered under Part \nIX of the Constitution, have been duly constituted except in the S tate of Jharkhand \nwhere Panchayat elections have not been held owing to pending litigation before the \nHon'ble Supreme Court. \n† Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n136   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nCases involving Ministers \n1917. SHRI ABU ASIM AZMI: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of cases involving the Ex- Chief Ministers, Ministers of Union and \nState Governments pending investigation with the CBI at present and since when; and  \n(b) the specific reasons for delay in the completion of investigations of each case \nand when these are expected to be sent to trial courts? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PUBLIC \nGRIEVANCES AND PENSIONS AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nPARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS (SHRI SURESH PACHOURI): (a) 9 cases against Ex -\nChief Ministers, Ex -Union Ministers and Ex -Union Ministers of State were under \ninvestigation in the CBI as on 31.07.2007. Out of these 9 cases, 5 cases were under \ninvestigation for less than one year, 1 case for more than one year and 3 cases for more \nthan two years. \n(b) It is the endeavour of the CBI to ensure expeditious completion of investigation \nin all cases. However, in some cases it takes time because of the complicated nature of \ncases requ iring scrutiny of voluminous documents, examination of large number of \nwitnesses, conducting extensive enquiries and in obtaining opinion of experts to \nestablish the allegations. No definite time frame can be stipulated for completion of the \ninvestigation/inquiry. \nPending cases awaiting trial \n1918. SHRIMATI HEMA MALINI: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether Government are aware that whopping 2,276 long pending cases \ninvestigated by CBI are awaiting trial for over twenty years; \n(b) whether it is a  fact that around 2032 cases are gathering dust in different courts \nfor over ten years; and \n(c) whether Government are taking any time bound steps to clear the pending cases \nsince everyday there is addition to pending cases? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINIST RY OF PERSONNEL, PUBLIC \nGRIEVANCES AND PENSIONS AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nPARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS (SHRI SURESH PACHOURI): (a) 648 CBI cases were \npending trial for over twenty years as on 31.07.2007. \n(b) 2123 cases were pending trial for over ten y ears as on 31.07.2007, which include \n648 cases pending over 20 years. \n(c) The Government has set up 39 Courts of Special Judges in various States \nexclusively for the trial of CBI cases. \nThe CBI also endeavours through constant monitoring/supervision, to ensure  that \nthere is no delay in trial on its part. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   137 \nSanctioned posts of IAS Officers \n †l919. MISS ANUSUIYA UIKEY: \nSHRI RUDRA NARAYAN PANY: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) the total number of sanctioned posts of officers of Indian Administrative Service \nthroughout the country; \n(b) the details thereof, State-wise; \n(c) whether officers of Indian Administrative Service are deputed in Madhya \nPradesh as per the sanctioned strength; \n(d) whether the shorta ge of officers affects the functioning of State \nGovernments; and \n(e) the details of the scheme of Government to meet the shortage? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, PUBLIC \nGRIEVANCES AND PENSIONS AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nPARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS (SHRI SURESH PACHOURI): (a) and (b) The total \nnumber of sanctioned posts of officers of Indian Administrative Service (IAS) in the \ncountry is 5460. The details are given in the Statement {See below). \n(c) As on 1.1.2007, 210 direct recruit officers and 85 promotee officers are available \nagainst the authorized strength of 257 of direct recruit officers and 112 promotee \nofficers respectively. Further, five direct recruit officers are being allotted to Madhya \nPradesh Cadre on the basis of the C ivil Services Examination, 2006. Similarly, 9 \nvacancies have been determined for filling up in the year 2007 under promotion quota.  \n(d) and (e) Recruitment to the IAS is done through the Union Public Service \nCommission keeping in view the sanctioned strength t o avoid shortage of officers and \nits impact on the functioning of the Government. The Government has already decided \nto recruit more and more IAS officers in the coming years, such as 110 during 2008, 120 \nin 2009 and 130 in 2010. \nStatement \nDetails of sanctioned posts of officers of Indian \nAdministrative Service (IAS) in the country  \nSI. No. Name of the IAS Cadre Sanctioned Strength \n1 2 3 \n1. Andhra Pradesh .314 \n2      • AGMU .   245 \n3. Assam-Meghalaya 226 \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n138   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions  \n1 2 3 \n4. Bihar 264 \n5. Chhattisgarh 138 \n6. Gujarat 260 \n7. Haryana     212 \n8. Himachal Pradesh 129 \n9. Jammu and Kashmir 112 \n10. Jharkhand 143 \n11. Karnataka 259 \n12. Kerala 214 \n13. Madhya Pradesh 369 \n14. Maharashtra 350 \n15. Manipur-Tripura 198 \n16. Nagaland 70 \n17. Orissa 202 \n18. Punjab 193 \n19. Rajasthan 260 \n20. Sikkim 50 \n21. Tamil Nadu 325 \n22. Uttarakhand 94 \n23. Uttar Pradesh 537 \n24. West Bengal 296 \n TOTAL: 5460 \nGovernment correspondence \n1920. SHRI S.M. LALJAN BASHA: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether Government are aware that officials and other correspondence \nemanating from Central Government offices do not have phone number or the fixed \naddresses; \n(b). if so, whether not mentioning phone and fax numbers and addresses is being \ndeliberately done to avoid public contact; \n(c) whether Government would direct all Government offices including officers of \nMinistries to list latest phone numbers; and \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions   139 \n(d) the steps that would be taken by Government to make officials available to \nordinary citizens without requiring introductions and interventions? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PERSONNEL, P UBLIC \nGRIEVANCES AND PENSIONS AND MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF \nPARLIAMENTARY AFFAIRS (SHRI SURESH PACHOURI): (a) to (d) Guidelines \nhave been laid down in chapter VIII, Para 50, Appendix 9 and 69(12) of the Central \nSecretariat Manual of Office Proce dure which requires Government officers to indicate \ntheir name, designation, address and telephone numbers in all written communication. \nAs a citizen centric initiative the Central Government Ministries/ Departments have set \nup Information and Facilitation Counters where a senior officer is designated as Contact \nOfficer. Instructions have also been issued to designate a senior officer at the level of \nJoint Secretary as Director of Public Grievances and their names are to be displayed at \nthe Reception and th at every Wednesday to be observed as 'meeting -less day' where \nDirector of Public Grievances is available from 10.00 to 13.00 hrs. to hear and receive \ngrievance of citizen. Their name and address, designation, telephone/fax numbers and \nemail address are also published in the leading news papers. \nKey goals of NCMP \n1921.   SHRIMATI SHOBHANA BHARTIYA: \nSHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether in an attempt to meet one of the key goals of the National Common \nMinimum Programme, G overnment cleared two proposals to provide economic \nassistance to the common man and the Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs \napproved the implementation of the skill development initiative scheme to be carried \nout over a period of five years at a cost of Rs. 550 crore; \n(b) if so, the other schemes that have been approved; and \n(c) by what time they are likely to be completed? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING (SHRI M.V. \nRAJASEKHARAN): (a) The Cabinet Committee on Economic Affairs (CCEA) has \napproved the scheme 'Skill Development Initiative' at a cost of Rs. 550 crore to be \ncarried over a period of five years. \n(b) Other main schemes related to skill development approved by the CCEA are - (i) \nUpgradation of 100 ITIs into Centre of Excellence; and (ii) Voca tional Training \nImprovement Project -Externally Aided Project for Reforms and Improvement in \nVocational Training Services rendered by Central and State Governments. \n(c) Likely completion time is as follows:  \nName of the Scheme Likely completion time \n1 2 \nSkill Development Initiative Scheme 2011-12 \n\n140   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions  \n1 2 \nUpgradation of 100 ITIs into Centre of 2008-09 \nVocational Training Improvement Project-\nExternally Aided Project for Reforms and \nImprovement in Vocational Training Services \nrendered by Central and State Governments \n2012-13 \nCommon entrance test for universities \n1922.   SHRI PR. RAJAN: \n     SHRIMATI N.P DURGA: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that this Ministry has prepared a plan to reform and restructure \nhigher education sector in the country; \n(b) if so, the details of the plan; \n(c) whether it is a fact that the Ministry proposes a common entrance test to all the \nuniversities in the country; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; \n(e) whether this issue has been discussed with the stakeholders; and \n(f) if so, the outcome thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING (SHRI M.V. \nRAJASEKHARAN): (a)to (f) Yes, Sir. Government plans to reform and restructure \nhigher education sector. Action i s being initiated to workout details as part of the \nEleventh Plan formulation. \nReport of per day expenditure \n †l923. SHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA: \nSHRI RAM JETHMALANI  \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government's attention has been drawn towards recently published \nreport of National Commission for Enterprises in the unorganized sector; \n(b) if so, whether it is a fact that according to this report, 836 million people in the \ncountry are making both ends meet by spending Rs.20 per day; \n(c) if so, the reaction of Government in this regard; \n(d) whether it is a fact that it is not possible for the people of the above said class to \nmake both ends meet as a result of continuous rise in the prices of items of day -to-day \nuse in the country; and \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions   141 \n(e) if so, the reaction of Government thereto? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING (SHRI M. V. \nRAJASEKHARAN): (a). Yes sir. \n(b) The Report o n Conditions of Work and Promotion of Livelihoods in the \nUnorganised Sector by the National Commission for Enterprises in the Unorganised \nSector (August, 2007) on page 1 (para 1.3) states \"At the end of 2004- 05, about 836 \nmillion or 77 per cent of the popu lation were living below Rs.20 per day and constituted \nmost of India's informal economy\". \n(c) The Report is under examination by the Government. \n(d) Yes, for the people of the above said class it is difficult to make both ends meet \nas a result of continuous rise in the prices of items of day-to-day use in the country. \n(e) The Government is already implementing the schemes/programmes like National \nRural Employment Generation Programme for providing at least 100 days' work for the \npeople in rural areas and Swarna Jayanti  Shahari Rozgar Yojana for urban poor to \nenable them to earn their livelihood by development/upgradation of skills. As per the \nApproach Paper for the Eleventh Plan, 70 million job opportunities are proposed to be \ncreated during the Eleventh Plan period. \nCapital investment for agriculture \n† 1924.  SHRI RAM JETHMALANI: \n  DR. MURLI MANOHAR JOSHI: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have estimated the requirement of additional capital \ninvestment in order to achieve the target of 4 pe r cent annual growth rate in the \nagriculture sector; \n(b) if so, the estimate of this capital requirement; \n(c) whether any suggestions have been made to make agriculture sector more \nlucrative in order to encourage this capital investment in the agriculture sector; and  \n(d) if so, the details of these suggestions? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING (SHRI M.V. \nRAJASEKHARAN): (a) and (b) The Approach Paper for the Eleventh Five Year Plan \ntitled \"Towards Faster and More Inclusive Growth - An Approach to the El eventh Five \nYear Plan\" in paragraph 3.2.38 has stated that \"Overall agricultural investment would \nneed to be about 16% of agricultural GDP, with public investment contributing 4 to 5%, \nduring the Eleventh Plan\". As per the latest figures available, Gross C apital Formation \nfor the year 2005- 06 (Quick Estimate at 1999- 2000 prices) in Agriculture, Forestry and \nFishing sector was Rs. 64,131 crores, which was 12.5% of the GDP of Rs.5,12,147 \ncrores for the Agriculture, Forestry and Fishing sector. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi \n\n142   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(c) and (d) The suggestions to make agriculture sector more lucrative in order to \nencourage capital investment in agriculture sector include ensuring remune rative prices \nto the farmers, developing appropriate technology to increase yields and reduce cost of \ncultivation, promoting private -public-partnership, removing restrictions and regulations \non private sector participation in setting up marketing and post- harvest infrastructure, \nallowing alternative marketing channels such as contract farming etc. \nPending proposals of Karnataka \n1925. DR. VIJAY MALLYA: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) the various proposals of Karnataka pending with the Union Government; \n(b) the reasons for the proposals not getting Union Government's clearance; \n(c) since when the proposals are pending; and \n(d) by when Union Government plan to clear the pending projects? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING. (SHRI M.V. \nRAJASEKHARAN): (a) No, Sir. There are no proposals of Karnataka pending with the \nPlanning Commission. \n(b) to (d) Does not arise. \nCurrent poverty line \n1926. SHRI S.K. KHABIR UDDIN AHMED: Will the PRIME MINISTER be  \npleased to state: \n(a) the poverty line in terms of per cap ita income, which is currently being used by \nthe Planning Commission; and \n(b) the figures of poverty, State-wise? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING (SHRI M.V. \nRAJASEKHARAN): (a) The poverty line currently being used by the Planning \nCommission i s based on per capita consumption expenditure and not on per capita \nincome. The per capita consumption norm has been fixed at Rs.49.09 per month in the \nrural areas and Rs.56.64 per month in the urban areas at 1973 -74 prices at national level \ncorresponding to a basket of goods and services anchored on a norm of per capita daily \ncalorie requirement of 2400 kcal in the rural areas and 2100 kcal in the urban areas. The \nstate-specific poverty lines are derived from the national level poverty lines, using state -\nspecific price indices and inter -state price differentials. The national poverty line at \n2004-05 prices is Rs. 356.30 per capita per month in the rural areas and Rs. 538.60 per \ncapita per month in the urban areas. The State-wise poverty lines are given in Statement-\nI (See below). \n(b) The Planning Commission estimates state -wise poverty from the large sample \nsurvey on household consumer expenditure conducted by the National Sample Survey \nOrganization (NSSO) at an interval of approximately five years following  the \nmethodology contained in the Report of the Expert Group on \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] .   Unstarred Questions   143 \nEstimation of Proportion and Number of Poor (Lakadwala Committee). The latest \nestimate of poverty is available for the year 20 04-05, based on the large sample survey \ndata on household consumer expenditure conducted by the National Sample Survey \nOrganization (NSSO) in its 61st round. The State -wise number and percentage of \npeople living below the poverty line in the rural and urba n areas are given in Statement-\nII \nStatement-I\n \nState-Specific Poverty Lines in 2004-05 (Rs. per \ncapita per month)  \nSI. No. States/U.Ts. Rural Urban \n1 Andhra Pradesh 292.95 542.89 \n2 Assam 387.64 378.84 \n3 Bihar 354.36 435.00 \n4 Chhattisgarh 322.41 560.00 \n5 Delhi 410.38 612.91 \n6 Goa 362.25 665.90 \n7 Gujarat 353.93 541.