.68 \n16 Maharashtra 29.6 171.13 32.2 146.25 30.7 317.38 \n17 Manipur 22.3 3.76 3.3 0.20 17.3 3.95 \n18 Meghalaya 22.3 4.36 3.3 0.16 18.5 4.52 \n19 Mizoram 22.3 1.02 3.3 0.16 12.6 1.18 \n20 Nagaland 22.3 3.87 3.3 0.12 19.0 3.99 \n21 Orissa 46.8 151.75 44.3 26.74 46.4 178.49 \n22 Punjab 9.1 15.12 7.1 6.50 8.4 21.63 \n23 Rajasthan 18.7 87.38 32.9 47.51 22.1 134.89 \n24 Sikkim 22.3 1.12 3.3 0.02 20.1 1.14 \n25 Tamil Nadu 22.8 76.50 22.2 69.13 22.5 145.62 \n26 Tripura 22.3 6.18 3.3 0.20 18.9 6.38 \n27 Uttar Pradesh 33.4 473.00 30.6 117.03 32.8 590.03 \n28 Uttarakhand 40.8 27.11 36.5 8.85 39.6 35.96 \n29 West Bengal 28.6 173.22 14.8 35.14 24.7 208.36 \n30 A and N Islands 22.9 0.60 22.2 0.32 22.6 0.92 \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   145  \n1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 \n31 Chandigarh 7.1 0.08 7.1 0.67 7.1 0.74 \n32 Dadra & N.Haveli 39.8 0.68 19.1 0.15 33.2 0.84 \n33 Daman & Diu 5.4 0.07 21.2 0.14 10.5 0.21 \n34 Lakshadweep 13.3 0.06 20.2 0.06 16.0 0.11 \n35 Pondicherry 22.9 0.78 22.2 1.59 22.4 2.37 \n ALL-INDIA: 28.3 2209.24 25.7 807.96 27.5 3017.2\n URP consumption = Uniform Recall Period consumption in which the consumer \nexpenditure data for all the items are collected from 30-day recall period. \nNote. \n1. Poverty Ratio of Assam is used for Sikkim. Arunachal Pradesh, Meghalaya, \nMizoram, Manipur, Nagaland and Tripura \n2. Poverty Line of Maharashtra and expenditure distribution of Goa is used to \nestimate poverty ratio of Goa. \n3. Poverty Ratio of Tamil Nadu is used for Pondicherry and A & N Island. \n4. Urban Poverty Ratio of Punjab is used for both rural and urban poverty of \nChandigarh \n5. Poverty Line of Maharashtra and expenditure distribution of Dadra and Nagar \nHaveli is used to estimate poverty ratio of Dadra and Nagar Haveli \n6. Poverty Ratio of Goa is used for Daman and Diu. \n7. Poverty Ratio of Kerala is used for Lakshadweep \nCentral Plan allocation in NER \n1927. SHRI DWIJENDRA NATH SHARMAH: Will the PRIME MINISTER be \npleased to state: \n(a) the details of Central Plan allocation made by Government for North Eastern  \nRegion, particularly Assam during the Eleventh Five Year Plan, year -wise/project- \nwise; and \n(b) the necessary steps taken by Government to sanction and release the  \namount demanded by the concerned State Governments of NER? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING (SHRI M.V. \nRAJASEKHARAN): (a) The Eleventh Five Year Plan allocation are yet to be finalized.  \n(b) The question does not arise. \nRate of agricultural production \n†1928. SHRJ RAM JETHMALANI: \nSHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have fixed 4 per cent target of annual  increase rate for \nagricultural production in the Eleventh Five Year Plan; \n† Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n146   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(b) if so, the facts in this regard and the total investment of capital that will be \nrequired in the agricultural sector for achieving this target; and \n(c) the total capital investment made in the agricultural sector of the country by the \nend of March, 2007? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING (SHRI M.V. \nRAJASEKHARAN): (a) and (b) The Approach Paper for the Eleventh Five Year Plan \ntitled \"Towards Faster and More Inclusive Growth - An Approach to the Eleventh Five \nYear Plan\" has projected an average growth rate of around 4.1% for agriculture and \nallied sectors during Eleventh Five Year Plan. The Approach Paper has further stated in \nparagraph 3.2.38 that \"Overall agricultural investment would need to be about 16% of \nagricultural GDP, with public investment contributing 4 to 5%, during the Eleventh \nPlan\". \n(c) As per t he latest figures available, Gross Capital Formation for the year 2005 -06 \n(Quick Estimate at 1999 -2000 prices) in Agriculture, Forestry and Fishing sector was \nRs. 64,131 crores, which was 12.5% of the GDP of Rs.5,12,147 crores for the \nAgriculture, Forestry and Fishing sector. \nSchemes for Chhattisgarh and Jharkhand \n†l929. SHRI SHREEGOPAL VYAS: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to \nstate:   \n(a) whether any special schemes have been formulated on the lines of Bihar for  \nnewly created States of Chhattisgarh and Jharkhand; \n(b) if so, whether the achievement of targets of the year 2006 -07 and the schemes of \n2007-08 have been reviewed; \n(c) whether the States have been consulted in this regard; \n(d) whether special provisions have been made while formulating the scheme in \nview of the situation arising out of naxalist problem; and \n(e) if so, the details of works to be implemented and the amount provided therefor?  \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF PLANNING (SHRI M.V. \nRAJASEKHARAN): (a) No, Sir. \n(b) to (e) Does not arise. \nRoad accidents on NHs \n†l930. SHRI AJAY MAROO: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of people who died in the road accidents on National Highways in the \ncountry during the last three years, State-wise and year-wise; \n† Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   147 \n(b) the details of the provisions regarding compensation amount to be provided to \nthe kith and kins of the persons who died in these accidents; and \n(c) the steps being taken to prevent these accidents? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) As per the report by \nthe States/UTs., the details regarding number of persons killed  in the road accidents on \nNational Highways during the years 2003, 2004 and 2005 (the latest available data), \nState-wise details are given in the Statement (See below). \n(b) The compensation to the deceased/injured in a road accident is given on the \nbasis of St ructured Compensation Formula prescribed under II Schedule of the Motors \nVehicle Act, 1988 on no- fault principle basis. The amount is calculated on the basis of \nnumber of factors such as age of the victim, income, number of dependents etc. If the \nroad acci dent victim/legal heir is not satisfied with the amount of compensation, they \nmay approach Motor Accidents Claims Tribunal/ Civil Courts for higher compensation \non fault principle. \n(c) The safety of road users is primarily the responsibility of the concerned S tate \nGovernment. However, this department has taken several steps to improve road safety \nfor road users which are as under: \n(i) Road safety is the integral part of road design at the planning stage for \nNational Highways/Expressways. \n(ii) Various steps to e nhance road safety such as road furniture, road \nmarkings/road signs, introduction of Highway Traffic Management System \nusing Intelligent Transport System, enhancement of discipline among \ncontractors during construction, road safety audit on selected stretches, \nhave been undertaken by National Highways Authority of India (NHAI). \n(iii) Refresher training to Heavy Motor Vehicle drivers in the unorganized \nsector. \n(iv) Involvement of NGOs for road safety activities by Department of Road \nTransport and Highways and (NHAI). \n(v)    Setting up of Model Driving Training School in the country. \n(vi) Publicity campaign on road safety awareness through audio -visual-print \nmedia. \n(vii) Institution of National Award for voluntary organizations/individual for \noutstanding work in the field of road safety. \n(viii) Tightening of safety standards of vehicles. \n(ix) Provision of cranes and ambulances to various State Governments/ NGOs \nunder National Highway Accident Relief Service Scheme. National \nHighways Authority of India also prov ides ambulances at a distance of 50 \nKm. on each of its completed highways under its Operation and \nMaintenance contracts. \n\n148   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(x)     Widening and improvements of National Highways from 2 lanes to 4 la nes \nand 4 lanes to 6 lanes etc. \nStatement \nDetails of State-wise persons killed on national highways  \nStates/U.Ts. 2003 2004 2005 \n1 2 3 4 \nAndhra Pradesh 2948 3423 3284 \nArunachal Pradesh 45 67 7 \nAssam 719 870 1041 \nBihar 1165 592 749 \nChhattisgarh 643 631 814 \nGoa 99 142 112 \nGujarat 1479 1631 1474 \nHaryana 1298 1321 1309 \nHimachal Pradesh 263 280 296 \nJammu and Kashmir 294 350 306 \nJharkhand 809 856 847 \nKarnataka 2132 2425 2467 \nKerala 1149 1196 1216 \nMadhya Pradesh 1578 1475 1005 \nMaharashtra 3168 3135 3193 \nManipur 84 53 102 \nMeghalaya 122 87 73 \nMizoram 41 36 19 \nNagaland 23 36 18 \nOrissa 1155 1353 1051 \nPunjab 1155 1047 1217 \nRajasthan 2615 2672 2734 \nSikkim 8 16 16 \nTamil .Nadu 4426 4158 3659 \nTripura 69 77 103 \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   149  \n1 2 3 4 \nUttaranchal 266 425 492 \nUttar Pradesh 3240 3860 5040 \nWest Bengal 1732 2113 2365 \nA and N Islands NIL NIL NIL \nChandigarh 24 22 28 \nD and N Haveli NIL NIL - \nDaman and Diu NIL NIL    \nNIL \nDelhi 302 276 974 \nLakshadweep NIL NIL - \nPondicherry 102 98 128 \nTOTAL: 33153 34723 35439 \nCRF funds for Rajasthan \n1931.   SHRIMATI PREMA CARIAPPA: \nSHRI SANTOSH BAGRODIA: \nWill the Minister of SHIPPING ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether there is any shortfall in amount released under CRF to Rajasthan with \nrespect to the amount accrued in the years 2000-01, 2001-02 and 2003-04; \n(b) if so, the amount of shortfall, year-wise and cumulatively; \n(c) whether it is a fact that the shortfall of a particular year gets lapsed; and \n(d) if not, the reasons for shortfall as mentioned above not being accounted for in \nsuccessive years? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) to (d) Funds accrued to the State of Rajasthan under Central Road Fund (CRF) \nand its release from 2000-01 to 2006-07 were as under:  \nYear  Amount in Rs. crore \n                     Accrual Release             Utilization \n1 2 3                         4 \n2000-01 75.82 25.27                   0 \n\n150   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions  \n1 2 3 4 \n2001-02 75.84 50.54 58.65 \n2002-03 76.71 99.34 100.68 \n2003-04 71.02 47.23 49.71 \n2004-05 67.87 67.87 84.37 \n2005-06 121.16 121.16 109.13 \n2006-07 119.80 138.26 134.55 \nTOTAL: 608.22 549.67 537.09 \nFunds are released based on the progress of the sanctioned works and utilization \ncertificates received from the State Government. Total utilization of fund by the State \nfrom 2000- 01 to 2006- 07 was Rs.537.09 against which fund released was Rs. 549.67 \ncrore which is more than the utilization. CRF is a non lapsable fund. During 2002 -03 \nand 2006-07 release was more than the accrual. \nMaintenance of NHs in Rajasthan \n1932.   SHRI SANTOSH BAGRODIA: \nSHRI HARISH RAWAT: \nWill the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD TRAN SPORT AND HIGHWAYS be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that requirement of funds for Periodic Renewal and Ordinary \nMaintenance for National Highways in Rajasthan is more than the allocation made by \nGovernment; \n(b) if so, what action is being taken by Government to allocate balance funds; \n(c) whether it is a fact that renewal of NHs is done in three to four years; and \n(d) what is the status of renewals in Rajasthan and if there is back log by when it \nwould be cleared? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPP ING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) and (b) The \nallocation of funds for maintenance and repair of National Highways in the country is \nnearly 40% of the amount required as per norms. The shortfall in funds is reflected in \nallocation for maintenance and repair which also includes periodic renewal of National \nHighways to all the States including Rajasthan. \n(c) Periodicity is not only criteria for renewal. It also depends on condition of  \nroad. \n(d) Road condition can be improved by measur es such as strengthening,  \nwidening, improvement of riding quality and periodic renewal. These measures are  \ntaken up in phases depending upon condition of roads, inter -se priority of works  \nand availability of funds. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions   151 \nTwo-laning of NHs in Rajasthan \n1933. SHRI MAHMOOD A. MADANI: \nSHRI SANTOSH BAGRODIA: \nWill the Minister of SHIPPING ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that there are National Highways in Rajasthan havin g single \nlane width and intermediate lane width; \n(b) if so, the action being taken by Government to make them two -lane wide and by \nwhen; \n(c) whether it is a fact that the existing crust thickness of National Highways in \nRajasthan State is much less than the required thickness as per IRC standards; and \n(d) if so, what action is being taken by Government to make them as per IRC \nstandard and by when? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) Nearly 1060 km length of National Highways in Rajasthan which are entrusted \nto the State Government are having carriage way width less than 2 lanes. Widening to 2 \nlanes has been taken up in 217 km length under National Highway (Original) works and \nthese works are in progress. Development of National Highways in the form of \nwidening is a continuous process. During the current annual plan there is provision for \nwidening to 2 lanes in 193 km length. \n(c) and (d) Thickness or pavement in some stretches of National Highways , \nparticularly in National Highways declared since February, 2004 are deficient with \nrespect to the standards set in the relevant codes of IRC. Strengthening of pavements is \na continuous process and is taken up within available resources and as per inter -se \npriority of other development works. No time -frame can be given for completion of \nstrengthening of deficient pavements in the National Highways network in Rajasthan \ndue to limited resources. \nWork on National Highway 7 \n1934. DR. VIJAY MALLYA: Will the Minister of SHIPPING ROAD TRANSPORT \nAND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government propose to undertake development of the National \nHighway 7 Shiridi Ghat section from Shakaleshpur to Mangalore; \n(b) if so, the total cost of the project; \n(c) the time-frame fixed to undertake and complete the project; \n(d) whether there is any impediment in undertaking this project; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING. ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K. H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) to (e) Yes, Sir.  \n\n152   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nThree developmental works amounting to Rs. 32.00 crore have been sanctioned for the \nimprovement of NH -48 passing through Shiradi Ghat area (Shakaleshpur -Mangalore \nsection). Out of these, two work s have been awarded with twelve month completion \nperiod and are likely to be completed by June, 2008. For the third work, revised cost \nestimate based on tender is under consideration which will be completed after twelve \nmonths from the date of award. There has been some delay in the award and execution \nof sanctioned improvement works due to poor participation by contracting agencies.  \nEastern by-pass for Jaipur \n1935.   SHRI SANTOSH BAGRODIA: \nSHRI KALRAJ MISHRA: \nWill the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD TRANSPORT A ND HIGHWAYS be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Jaipur City needs an eastern by -pass to by -pass heavy vehicular traffic \nof National Highway; \n(b) if so, by when the sanctions for by -pass connecting NH-8 (Ajmer-Jaipur) to NH-\n12 (Jaipur-Tonk), NH-12 (Tonk-Jaipur) to NH-U (Jaipur-Agra) and NH-II (Agra-Jaipur) \nto NH-8 (Jaipur-Delhi) are to be accorded by Central Government; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) to (c) Ther e is \nalready a Jaipur by -pass on western side of NH -8 connecting NH-8 (Delhi-Jaipur), NH-\n11 (Jaipur -Bikaner) and NH -8 (Jaipur -Ajmer). The need for construction of Jaipur \nbypass on eastern side of NH-8 connecting NH-8 (Ajmer-Jaipur), NH-12 (Jaipur- Tonk), \nNH-11 (Jaipur -Agra) and NH -8 (Jaipur -Delhi) was under consideration for inclusion \nunder NHDP Phase VII. Due to inter -se priority of works, Jaipur City is not in the list of \ncities identified so far by the Ministry for construction of Ring Roads/By -passes under \nthe first phase of NHDP Phase VII (i.e. Phase VIIA). However, this phase is yet to be \napproved by the Government. \nFour-laning of Patna-Hajipur-Muzaffarpur road \n†l936. SHRI MANGANI LAL MANDAL: Will the Minister of SHIPPING ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether there is a plan to develop and construct Patna -Hajipur-Muzaffarpur road \nunder NHDP into a four-lane road; and \n(b) if so, the latest and complete details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) 4 -laning of Patna -\nHajipur-Muzaffarpur section of NH -19 and NH -77 has been included under NHDP \nPhase IIIA on BOT basis. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions  .153 \n(b) Patna-Hajipur section of NH -19 is already four lane. Bids were invited on BOT \n(Toll) mode for 4-laning of Hajipur-Muzaffarpur section of NH-77 , but no bid has been \nreceived till last date of submission of bid i.e. 20.08.2007. \nConstruction of roads under NHDP \n1937. SHRI MOTIUR RAHMAN: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the performance on National Highways Development Programme \n(NHDP) is poor and the pace of construction of roads is slow; \n(b) whether any irregularities/scam in the construction of roads have come to the \nnotice of Government and if so, the details thereof and the action taken thereon; \n(c) the quantum of money lost by Government due to slow progress in the \nconstruction of roads; and \n(d) the measures taken by Government to accelerate the pace of construction of \nquality roads? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) There has been \nsome delays in the completion of some cont racts under National Highways \nDevelopment Programme (NHDP), due to problems associated with acquisition of land, \nshifting of utilities, environment and forest clearances, poor performance of some \ncontractors and termination of some contracts etc. \n(b) The d etails of complaints received in Vigilance Division of National Highways \nAuthority of India (NHAI) during last five years are as under:-  \n Year Complaint received \n 2003 26 \n 2004 41 \n 2005 37 \n 2006 64 \n 2007 41 \nIrregularities noticed after completion of investigation are being attended/ rectified or \namount recovered from the Contractor/Consultant and also action against the staff is \ntaken up if they are found guilty. \n(c) In case the delay is attributable to NHAI, necessary, extension of time is accorded to \nthe contractor as per conditions of contract. However, in case the contractor does not \ncomplete the project in the extended period of time (if applicable), the necessary \nliquidated damages as per the contract provisions is levied on the contractor. \n(d) The step s taken by the Government to accelerate the pace of construction of \nNational Highways are given in the Statement. \n\n154   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nStatement \nThe steps taken to accelerate the pace of \nconstruction of National Highways \nThe following steps have been taken to ensure early completion of the projects:- \n(a) The Contracts are regularly monitored at various levels such as by \nSupervision Consultant, Project Directors, Senior officers of NHAI. \nProgress reviews are also held at th e level of Chairman, NHAI, Secretary, \nDepartment of Road Transport and Highways and Minister, Shipping, Road \nTransport and Highways. \n(b) State Governments have appointed Senior officers as nodal officers for \nresolving problems associated with implementation of  the NHDP such as \nland acquisition, removal of utilities, forest/pollution/environment \nclearances etc. These nodal officers hold periodic meetings  to review the \nprojects and take action to resolve the problems. \n(c) A Committee of Secretaries has been constitut ed under Cabinet Secretary \nto address inter-ministerial and Centre-State issues such as land acquisition, \nutility shifting, environment approvals, clearance of Road Over Bridges \n(ROBs). \n(d) The procedure of issue of Land Acquisition notifications has now been \nsimplified. \n(e) To expedite the construction of ROBs an officer of the Railways has been \nposted to NHAI to coordinate with Ministry of Railways. Memorandum of \nUnderstanding (MoU) has also been signed with M/s IRCON for \nconstruction of some of the ROBs. \n(f) Action has been taken against non -performing contractors and they are not \nallowed to bid for future projects unless they improve the performance in \nexisting contracts. \n(g) Steps have been taken to improve cash flow problems of contractors by \ngranting interest bearing discretionary advance at the request of contractor, \nrelease of retention money against bank guarantee of equal amount, \ndeferment of recovery of advances (on interest basis) and relaxation in \nminimum Interim Payments Certificate (IPC) amount. \nNS-EW corridor in MP \n†l938. SHRIMATI MAYA SINGH: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether North-South (NS)-East-West (EW) corridor plan has been finalized; \n(b) whether it is a fact that a mere 621 Kms. has been proposed under the National \nHighway for vast State like Madhya Pradesh; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions   155 \n(c) the reasons therefor and whether any proposal to increase this length further as \nper requirements of the State is under consideration; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) and (c) 652 km of National Highway in Madhya Prade sh is included in North \nSouth and East West (NS -EW) corridor. This length is as per the length of the corridor \ncrossing Madhya Pradesh. There is no proposal to increase this length. \n(d) Does not arise. \nMumbai port container terminal \n1939. SHRIMATI SUPRIYA SULE: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD  \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Mumbai Port's plan to set up a container terminal is under \nGovernment's scrutiny; \n(b) whether the port has been asked to provide details of container traffic reve nue \nand expenses to ascertain the viability of the project; \n(c) whether Rs.1,228 crore container terminal project is proposed to be developed \nby a consortium led by Gammon India through the build-operate-transfer basis; and \n(d) whether Government has justified the project in view of the increasing container \ntrade in the country? \nTHE MINISTER OF SHIPPING. ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI \nT.R. BAALU): (a) to (d) The Government has approved the proposal of the Mumbai \nPort Trust to award the contract to set up an Offs hore Container Terminal on Build, \nOperate and Transfer (BOT) basis at the Port to a Consortium led by Gammon India \nLtd. who has offered highest share of gross revenue of 35.064% from the planned \nfacility to the Mumbai Port Trust, on the basis of open compe titive bidding. Pre -\ninvestment studies on the project have established the viability of the project.  \nPedestrian over-bridge on NH-5 \n1940. SHRI V. HANUMANTHA RAO: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD  \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Govern ment have received a representation from the Road Safety \nForum objecting to non-use of pedestrian over-bridge at Chilakoluripeto on NH-5 as the \nreason to avoid building any other pedestrian over-bridges in Andhra Pradesh: \n(b) the objections that were raised by  the Road Safety Forum against Government's \ndecision to penalise Andhra Pradesh by not increasing road safety; \n\n156   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(c) whether Government have made any efforts to get world class architects to \ndesign user friendly over-bridges; and \n(d) the steps proposed to carefully consider the representation of Road Safety \nForum in this regard? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K. H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. \nOver bridges for exclusive use of pedestrians are provided in the exceptional locations \nwhere the pedestrian volume crossing a wide carriageway is extremely large and cannot \nbe regulated by other traffic control measures without causing undue delay both to the \npedestrian and vehicular traffic. There is no such situation on NH -5 at Gundugulanu and \ntherefore, pedestrian foot over bridge is not feasible. However, proposal for construction \nof foot over bridge at this location will be considered during preparation of Detailed \nProject Report ( DPR) for six laning of this stretch of NH-5 under NHDP Phase-V. \n(c) and (d) Yes, Sir. NHAI appoints International Level Consultants for project \npreparation for upgradation of National Highways. All cares from safety point of view \nand public needs are taken into consideration while designing the National Highways.  \nSix-Ianing on BOT basis \n1941. SHRI RAMDAS AGARWAL: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the National Highways Authority of India (NHAI) has now opened \nlarger tretches of over 200 Km. on a Build- Operate-Transfer (BOT) basis, under phase -\nV Programme, involving six-laning of selected projects; \n(b) if so, the details thereof, location -wise, cost per Kilometer -wise, feasibility \nreport-wise indicating whether any leading private infrastructure consultancy firm is \nalso involved therein indicating the number of bidders who have applied so far; and \n(c) if so, the estimated total cost of each road project and the time likely to be taken \nin co mpletion thereof particularly construction of 225 Km. long Gurgaon -Kotputli-\nJaipur stretch of NH-8? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) The Government \naccorded approval for undertaking s ix laning of 6,500 km. of National Highways \ncomprising 5,700 km of Golden Quadrilateral and balance 800 km on other sections, \nunder National Highways Development Project (NHDP) Phase -V. As per the approved \nimplementation mechanism for NHDP Phase -V, stretches of 100 km or more that have \nbeen completed at least two years ago and contractual actions on previous contracts \nhave been completed, are being included in the year -wise programme. Where the \ndistance between two cities is less than 100 km, such stretches  are also being included \nas exception cases. \n(b) List of proposed stretches of National Highways in different States under NHDP \nPhase-V is given in the Statement {.See below). Cost per km. has been \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   157 \nestimated as Rs. 6.34 crore. Feasibility report or seven stretches have been completed \nby the technical consultants and detailed given in the Statement- II (See below). Total \n203 firms have applied for pre-qualification for eight stretches under NHDP Phase-V. \n(c) NHDP Phase-V is scheduled for completion by December, 2012. Estimated civil \nconstruction cost for Gurgaon -Kotputli-Jaipur of 225 km. is Rs. 1517 crores. The likely \ntime period for completion is 30 months from the appointed date. \nStatement-I \nSix laning of National Highways  \nStretches States Length \n(in km.) \n(a)   Golden Quadrilateral Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, Gujarat, \nHaryana, Karnataka, Maharashtra, \nOrissa, Rajasthan, Tamil Nadu, Uttar \nPradesh, West Bengal and \nJharkhand.lucrative \n5700 \n(b)   Other Stretches   \n(i)    Panipat-Jalandhar Haryana and Punjab 300 \n(ii)   Delhi-Hapur-Moradabad Delhi and Uttar Pradesh 170 \n(iii) Samkhiali-Gandhidham Gujarat 56 \n(iv) Indore-Dewas Madhya Pradesh 55 \n(v)   Agra-Gwalior Uttar Pradesh and Madhya Pradesh 85 \n(vi) Chandikhol-Paradeep Orissa 77 \n(vii) Ludhiana-Chandigarh Punjab 82 \nStatement-II \nStatus of feasibility report for seven stretches under NHDP Phase-V  \nSI. Stretch NH No. Length Cost per km \nNo\n \n  (in km.) (in crore) \n1 Panipat-Jalandhar 1 291.100 7.55 \n2 Gurgaon-Kotputli-Jaipur 8 225.600 6.72 \n3 Surat-Dahisar 8 239.000 5.67 \n4 Chilkaluripet-Vijayawada 5 82.500 6.55 \n5 Chennai-Tada 5 43.400 7.71 \n6 Chandikhole-Jagatpur-Bhubaneswar 5 67.000 15.39 \n7 Delhi-Hapur 24 52.000 10.36 \n\n158   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nPhase IV of NHDP \n1942. MS. PRAMILA BOHIDAR: \nSHRI B.J. PANDA: \nWill the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is proposed to initiate phase IV of National Highways  \nDevelopment Programme (NHDP) all over the country; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the details of road stretches that would be two -laned in Orissa under the above \nproject indicating the time-frame within which these would be initiated and completed? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) to (c) National \nHighways Development Project (NHDP), Phase -IV is at initial stage of planning \nwherein two laning with paved shoulder of 20,000 km. of National Highways is \nenvisaged with an estimated cost of Rs. 27,800 crore. NHDP Phase -IV is targeted to be \ncompleted by December, 2015. It is too early to give the State -wise details for NHDP \nPhase-IV. \nBan on stereos while driving \n1943. SHRI MANOHAR JOSHI: Will the Minister of SHI PPING, ROAD  \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are considering putting a ban on use of stereos while \ndriving the vehicle as it may lead to distraction as in the case of use of cell phone; and  \n(b) if so, by when it is expected? \nTHE M INISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) No, Sir. \n(b) Does not arise in view of (a) above. \nWidening of road in RS Pura in J&K \n1944. SHRI T.S. BAJWA: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD TRANSPORT  \nAND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have received any proposal from the State Government for \nupgradation and widening of road from Tareva to Neokawali Sai in R.S. Pura sector last \nyear; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the progress in this regard? \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   159 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) No, Sir. \n(b) and (c) Do not arise. \nRoad from Satwari to RS Pura in J&K \n1945. SHRI T.S. BAJWA: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD TRANSPORT  \nAND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the upgradation and widening of road from Satwari to \nRS Pura (J&K) is in progress under CRF; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the present s tatus of the said road and the amount of funds that has been \nreleased; and \n(d) whether it is a fact that the work of the said road is not being done \nsatisfactory? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) and (c) Double laning of Satwari -RS Pura road was sanctioned for an amount of \nRs.1146 lakhs under Central Road Fund on 3rd August, 2005. The work is in progress. \nAs reported by State PWD, the physical progress is 60% and an expen diture of Rs. 910 \nlakhs has been incurred as on 17th July, 2007. Funds are released by this Ministry for \nCRF works in entire State. \n(d) No such report has been received from the State PWD in this regard. \nFour-laning of NHs in Uttarakhand \n1946. SHRI RAJ NAT H SINGH: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD  \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of kilometers of roads which have been constructed by NHAI in  \nUttarakhand since the present Government came to power; \n(b) by when Government propose to complete the four -laning of Delhi -Dehradun \nand Delhi-Nainital highways; \n(c) the reasons for the extremely slow progress in the completion of these \nhighways; and \n(d) the steps Government propose to speed up the work and the reasons for non-\ncompletion of road from Kotdwar to Pauri, Uttarakhand? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) and (b) So far, no \nNational Highway has been constructed by the National Highways Authority of India \n(NHAI) in the State of Uttarakhand. Current status of four laning in various stretches of \nDelhi-Dehradun sections of National Highway 58 and 72 and Delhi -Nainital sections of \nNH-24 and 87 are given in the Statement (See below). \n\n160   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n \n(c) Delay. In Hapur-Moradabad section of NH-24 and Merrut-Muzaffarnagar section \nof NH-58 where word is in progress, is due to delay in tree cutting and shifting of \nutilities, beside some encroachment. \n \n(d ) Regular coordination with the state Government to expedite tree cutting, shifiting \nof utilities and removal of encroachments is being carried out. Kotdwar to Pauri is not \nproposed for four-laning.  \n \nStatement \nFour-laning of NHs in Uttarakhand  \nSI.     Stretch Status \nNo.  \n1        Delhi-Dehradun' (a) The Stretch of National Highway from Delhi to \nsections of NH-58 Meerut is already four lane. \nand 72  \n b) The stretch from Meerut to Muzaffarnagar has \n been  awarded  for four laning  and  work is  in \n progress. This work is targeted to be completed by \n March, 2009. \n (c) Bids for four laning Muzaffarnagar to Haridwar \n stretch are to be re-invited by NHAI on Build \n Operate and Transfer (BOT) basis. Detailed Project \n Report and the bid documents are being updated. \n (d) The Haridwar to Dehradun stretch is passing \n through Rajaji National Park, and Lachhi wala Park. \n The project can only be taken up after getting NOC \n from concerned authorities. \n It is too early to indicate the date of completion \n of 4 -laning of Muzaffarnagar-Dehradun section. \n2        Delhi-Nainital The reach from Delhi to Hapur is already four lane. \nsections of NH-24 Four laning of Hapur to Moradabad section is in \nand 87 progress   and   targeted   to    be   completed   by \n March, 2008. Moradabad -Rampur section of NH-24 \n is also proposed to be four laned under National \n Highways Development Project (NHDP) Phase-IIIB, \n the DPR for which is in progress. \n Four laning of Rampur to Kathgodam section of NH- \n 87 has also been included under NHDP Phase III-B. \n Bids have been received for preparation of detailed \n project report (DPR). Kathgodam to Nainital stretch \n is not part of NHDP. \n It is premature to indicate the completion date of \n Moradabad-Rampur section of NH-24 and Rampur- \n Kathgodam section of NH-87  since DPR has not \n been completed/taken up. \n\n Written Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   161 \n  \nDredging at Gujarat ports \n1947. SHRI KANJIBHAI PATEL: Will the Minister of SHIPPING ROAD  \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Central Government are planning to dredge the channels' of Gujarat \nGovernment ports;  \n(b) the type of infrastructure proposed to be developed at Gujarat ports in comparison \nto major ports; and \n(c) in what way the Gujarat Ports can benefit with inception of rharitime policy? \nTHE MINISTER OF SHIPPING, ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI \nT.R. BAALU): (a) to (c) Responsibility for development of Norr-Major Ports vests in the \nrespective State Government, i.e., Government of Gujarat in respett of Non-Major Ports in the \nState of Gujarat. The comprehensive scope of the proposed Maritime Policy is expected to \nbenefit Non-Major Ports also. \nRail-road linkage projects to minor ports \n1948. SHRI KANJIBHAI PATEL: Will the Minister of SHIPPING ROAD  \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that a number of rail-road linkage projects pertaining to the minor \nports of Gujarat are pending and require preparation and execution of an early implementation \nplan by the Central Government;  \n(b) if so, the details of such projects; \n \n(c) the progress made in respect of these projects during the last three years,     \nyear-wise and project-wise; and \n(d) the steps propsed to be taken during the current year, alongwith the  \nproject-wise details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF SHIPPING, ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI \nT.R. BAALU): (a) to (d) Union Government is responsible for development and \nmaintenance of only National Highways (NHs) and providing connectivity to the \nMajor Ports, of the country. At present, there is only one Major Port in Gujarat, i.e., \nKandla Port which is well connected through National Highway NH-8A. However, some \nof the projects for augmentation of National Highways in Gujarat, that have been \nincluded in the National Highways Development Programme/(NHDP) Phase -Ill, would \nalso facilitate improved road linkage to some non-Major Ports of Gujarat. These projects are \nindicated below:--  \nProject Status \nKandla-Mundra Port \nSection of NH-8A \n \nSurat-Hazira Port \nSection of NH-6 \nThe work for preparation of DPR has been awarded to M/s \nRITES in the month of July, 2007 and is in progress. \n \nDPR is in progress. \n\n162   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nFor the connectivity of Broad Guage Rail Linkages to important non -Major Ports in \nGujarat, the Gujarat Maritime Board had initiated and completed Techno -Feasibility \nStudies through M/s Rail India Technical and Economic Services (RITES) Limited. \nDiscussions were held between senior officers of Government of Gujarat and Railway \nBoard on 20th July, 2006. Meanwhile, Railways are executing following projects for \nproviding linkage to non-Major Ports in Gujarat:  \nName of the Port Status \nDahej Port (Bharuch- The project has been sanctioned for execution by Rail \nSamni-Dahej-Gauge Vikas Nigam Limited (RVNL) through a Special Purpose \nConversion) Vehicle (SPV) viz.  Bharuch-Dahej Railway Company \n Limited   which   will   implement   the   project.   The \n shareholder's   agreement has been signed on 12.1.2007. \n The anticipated cost of the project is Rs. 165.77 crores. \n The Final Location Survey (FLS) has been completed \n and   the   report   submitted   to  Divisional  Railway \n Manager, Baroda on 29.6.2007. \nHazira Port (Surat- A Special Purpose Vehicle (SPV) for implementation of \nHazira-New Line) this  project  is  to  be  created  upon  finalization  of \n principles  for  revenue  allocation  and  Concession \n Agreement for this project FLS has been completed. \nDholera Port Rail Vikas Nigam Limited (RVNL) is undertaking the \n Preliminary Engineering-cum-Traffic Surey (PETS) for rail \n connectivity to this Port. \nBedi and New Bedi Railway Board is appraising the Study Report prepared \nPort Connectivity by RITES for this project. \nPedestrian over-bridges on NH-5 \n1949. SHRI S.M. LALJAN BASHA: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of pedestrian over-bridges on entire NH-5 presently; \n(b) the additional number of pedestrian over -bridges planned in the year  \n2007-08 on NH-5; \n(c) the break-up of such pedestrian over bridges, State-wise; \n(d) the reasons\" for ignoring vulnerable spots and accident -prone places -pointed-\nout by Road Safety Forum; and \n(e) the steps proposed to create a transparent and responsive NHAI in 2007- 08? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HI GHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) The number of \npedestrian over-bridges on entire NH-5 is four. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions   163 \n(b) Nil. \n(c) The State-wise break-up is as following:-  \nSI. No. State Number of Pedestrian over bridges \n1. Gujarat (on NH-8) 02 \n2. Karnataka (on NH-4) 05 \n3. Andhra Pradesh (on NH-5) 04 \n4. Bihar (on NH-2) 01 \n(d) and (e) The vulnerable spots and accident prone places are not ignored. The \nrequirements at such places are properly examined and accordingly action is taken. \nWidening of highways in hilly areas \n†l950. SHRI HARISH RAWAT: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether his Ministry is considering to widen highways to check road accidents \nin hilly areas and to construct parapets there; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the names of the highways situated in Uttarakhand \nState which have been included in this category; and \n(c) if not, by when the work is likely to be started in this regard? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE  MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) to (c) This Ministry \nis primarily responsible for development and maintenance of National Highways (NHs) \nin the country. The widening of NHs in the hilly areas, including construction of \nparapet wall etc., are taken up based on condition of roads, traffic density, inter -se \npriority and availability of funds, which also enhances the road safety. The details of \nsections of NHs in the State of Uttarakhand where widening works have be en included \nare given in the Statement. \nStatement \nDetails of sections of National Highways in the State of Uttarakhand where widening \nworks have been included  \nNHNo. Section Stretch Status \n1 2 3 4 \nNH-87 Jyolikot-Karna Prayag km. 36 to km. 46 Included in Annual-Plan \n2007-08. \nNH-87 Jyolikot-Karna Prayag km. 89 to km. 100 Included in Annual Plan \n2007-08. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi.. \n\n164   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions  \n1 2 3 4 \nNH-87 Jyolikot-Karna Prayag km. 108 to km. 120 Included in Annual Plan \n2007-08. \nNH-87 Jyolikot-Karna Prayag km. 200 to km. 210 Included in Annual Plan \n2007-08. \nNH-94 Dharasu Bend-Phool \nChatti \nkm. 176 to km. 183 Works Sanctioned \nNH-94 \n \nNH-119 \nDharasu Bend-Phool \nChatti \nkm. 122 to km. 128 Included in Annual Plan \n2007-08. \nMeerut-Kotdwar-\nSrinagar' \nkm. 193 to km. 202 Works Sanctioned \nNH-119 Meerut-Kotdwar-\nSrinagar \nkm. 202 to km. 212 Included in Annual Plan \n2007-08. \nNH-121 Ramnagar-Buakhal km. 38 to km. 50 Included in Annual Plan \n2007-08. \nNH-121 Ramnagar-Buakhal km. 189 to km. 205 Included in Annual Plan \n2007-08. \nNH-123' Harbatpuh-Barkot \nbend \nkm. 98 to km. 111 Included in Annual Plan \n2007-08. \nNH-58 Rishikesh-Joshimath -\nMana \nkm. 250-266, km. \n342-364, 388-399, \n446-468 and km. 48S \nto km 527 \nWorks Commenced \n \nNH-58 Rishikesh-Joshimath -\nMana \nkm. 240 to km. 250 Included in Annual Plan \n2007-08. \nNH-108 Dharasu-Gangotri km. 57 to km. 68 Works Commenced \nNH-108 Dharasu-Gangotri km. 0-13, km. 37 to \nkm. 57 \nIncluded in Annual Plan \n2007-08. \nNH-109 \n- \nRudraprayag-\nGaurikund \nkm. 1.5 to km. 8 Works Commenced \nNH-125 Tanakpur-Pithoragarh km. 1.88 to km. \n41.88 \nWorks Commenced \nNH-58 \nNH-72 \nUttar \nPradesh/Uttarakhand \nBorder to Dehradun \nsection \nkm. 152 to km. 219 \nkm. 144 to km. \n196.80 \nIncluded under National \nHighways Development \nProject NHDP-Phase-III. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions   165 \nForged driving licenses \n1951. SHRI KARNENDU BHATTACHARJEE: Will the Minister of SHIPPING,  \nROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the number of forged driving licenses has increased \ndramatically throughout the country; \n(b) the number of cases detected during the last three years in the country and the \nnumber of officers punished; and \n(c) the steps Government propose to take to solve the problem? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) and (b) The Motor \nVehicles Act, 1988 and Central Motor Vehicles Rules, 1989 prescribe detailed rules for \ngranting driving li cence. Under the provisions of Motor Vehicles Act, 1988, licensing \nauthorities are appointed by State Governments. It is the responsibility of ' concerned \nlicensing authorities to ensure that driving licences are issued only to an eligible person \nwho fulfills all the requisite conditions prescribed for issuance of driving licence. Further, it \nis the State Government which controls the licensing authorities and thus has the \nresponsibility to ensure that the functioning of the concerned department is free fro m \ncorrupt practices. \n(c) Does not arise. \nFour-laning of NH leading to Mundra Port \n1952. SHRI KESHUBHAI S. PATEL: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD  \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state:  \n(a) whether the Central Government propose to four -lane the Nation al Highway \nleading to the Mundra Port; \n(b) if so, whether any time bound programme has been framed for completion of the \nproject; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD  \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPP A): (a) to (c) Kandla to  \nMundra Port stretch of NH 8A (Extension) has been included in National Highway  \nDevelopment Project (NHDP) Phase III. The stretch of NH 8A from Kandla to  \nGandhidham is already 4 -lanned. The consultancy work for preparation of Detail ed \nProject Report (DPR) for 4 -lanning from Gandhidham to Mundra Port has been \nrecently awarded by National Highways Authority of India (NHAI) and feasibility  \nstudy is targeted to be completed by March, 2008. Bids for the civil, work shall be \ninvited after completion of DPR. The road portion from Mundra to Mundra Port is,  \nhowever, at present not a National Highway. It is too early to frame time bound  \nprogramme for completion of the project.  \n\n166   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nSpeed limit for vehicles \n1953. SHRI B.J. PANDA: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD TRANSPORT \nAND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is proposed to set up a panel to make roads safer; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether around 85,000 people die in road acci dents every year mainly due to \nover-speeding; \n(d) whether Government propose to introduce variable speed limits for motor \nvehicles of all types and uniform speed limits on all the National Highways; and  \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) and (b) A Committee \nunder the Chairmanship of Shri S. Sundar, former Secretary, Ministry of Surface \nTransport was set up to deliberate and make recommendations for creation of a \ndedicated body on Road Safety and Traffic Management. The committee submitted its \nreport on 20.02.2007 inter -alia recommending creation of a National Road Safety and \nTraffic Management Board through an Act of Parliament. A note for Committee of \nSecretaries in this regard has been prepared and has been circulated to concerned \nMinistries/Departments for their comments. \n(c) The accidents and fatalities on road are dependent on many factors such as \nnumber of vehicles on road, population, road space, speed, diffe rent traffic condition etc. \nThe data regarding number of road accidents and fatalities thereof due to over -speeding \nis not compiled by this Ministry. However, as per the report by the States/UTs, the \ndetails regarding number of persons killed in the road a ccidents on All Roads during the \nyears 2003, 2004 and 2005 (the latest available data) are as under:-  \n Year Persons killed in Road Accidents \n 2003 85998 \n 2004 92618 \n 2005 94968 \n(d) and (e) This Ministry has prescribed maximum speed limit for various c ategories \nof vehicles irrespective of category of roads. The details are given in the Statement. \nStatement \nTable, of maximum speed limit at a Glance' \nIn exercise of the powers conferred by sub -section (I) of Section 112 of the Motor \nVehicles Act, 1988 (59 of 1988), the Central Government hereby fixes the \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 20071 Unstarred Questions   167 \nspeed specified in column (2) of the Table below as the maximum speed in respect of \nthe class of motor vehicles specified in the corresponding entry in column (2) thereof. \nTABLE  \nClass of vehicles Maximum speed per hour in kilometres \n(1) (2) \n(1) If all the wheels of the vehicle are  \nfitted with pneumatic tyres and the  \nvehicle is not drawing a trailer-  \n(a) if the vehicle is a light motor  \nvehicle, other than transport vehicle; No limit \n(b) if the vehicle is a light motor  \nvehicle, and a transport vehicle; 65 \n(c) if the vehicle is a motor cycle; 50 \n(d) if the vehicle is a medium or  \nheavy passenger motor vehicle; 65 \n(e) if the vehicle is a medium or  \nheavy goods vehicle 65 \n(2) If the vehicle is an articulated  \nvehicle, all the wheels of which  \nare fitted with pneumatic tyres,  \nwhich is a heavy goods vehicle or  \nheavy passenger motor vehicle. 50 \n(3) If the vehicle is drawing not  \nmore than one trailer, or in case of  \nartillery equipment, not more than  \ntwo trailers and all the wheels of  \nthat vehicle and the trailer are fitted  \nwith pneumatic tyres-  \n(a) if the vehicle is a light motor  \nvehicle and the trailer being two-  \nwheeled has gross vehicle weight  \nnot exceeding 800 kilograms 60 \n(b) if the vehicle is a light motor  \nvehicle and the trailer has more  \nthan two wheels or a gross vehicle  \nweight exceeding 800 kilograms 50 \n(c) if the vehicle is a medium goods  \nvehicle or medium passenger motor  \nvehicle. 50 \n\n168   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions  \n(I)     (2)   \n(d) if the vehicle is a heavy goods \nvehicle or heavy passenger motor \nvehicle. \n  40 \n(e) if the vehicle is a heavy goods        \nvehicle, or heavy passenger motor vehicle \nused by the fire brigade. \n  50 \n(4) Any other case not covered by \nentry (1), (2) or (3) \n  30 \n2. This notification shall come into force on the first day of July, 1989. \nVide S.O. 425 (E), dated 9th June 1989. published in the Gazette of India, Extra. Pt. II. \nSec: 3 (ii), dated 9th June,  1989. \nMaintenance of Amritsar-Pathankot sector on NH-1 \n1954. SHRI VARINDER SINGH BAJWA: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the maintenance of Amritsar-Pathankot sector of the National \nHighway No.l is most unsatisfactory; \n(b) the expenditure incurred on its maintenance during the last three years ending \n31st March, 2007, year -wise and how it compares with the all India expenditur e on \nmaintenance of the highways per year and per kilometer; \n(c) whether there, is any proposal to widen some of its very narrow stretches like \nPathankot-Dinanagar; and  \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor, indicating the particular stretches to be widened, expenditure \nto be incurred and the likely date of completion? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD  \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) The National  \nHighway 15 (not NH -1) between Amritsat -Pathankot is maintained in traffic worthy  \ncondition with in the funds made available for maintenance of National Highways except \nat few locations which have been damaged due to heavy rains. \n  \n(b) The expenditure incurred on die maintenance of this road during the last  \nthree years is given aS, under.- \n \nPUNJAB   \nDescription 2004-05 2005-06 2006-07 \nMaintenance Rs. 143.96 lacs Rs.599.88 lacs Rs.90.63 lacs \nExpenditure    \nTotal length 100.82 km. 100.82 km. 100.82 km. \nCost per km. 1.431acs/per km. 5.95 lacs/per km. 0.90 lacs/per km. \nThe expenditure is comparable with the all India expenditure on maintenance of \nNational Highways.  \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   169 \n(c) and (d) Four laning of Amritsar -Pathankot has been approved under NHDPII1 \nwhich also includes widening of struct ures. As per DPR submitted by Consultant, \nprovision for construction of Dina Nagar bypass has been made. Therefore, there is no \nproposal at present to widen any structure in Dina Nagar. \nCondition of Highway Nos. 55, 31 and 31A \n†l955. SHRI SAMAN PATHAK: Wil l the Minister of SHIPPING , ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are aware of the deteriorating condition of Highway Nos. \n55, 31 and 31A; \n(b) the steps being taken by Government to improve these Highways; \n(c) whether Government are considering to provide a package to save these \nHighways which is the only lifeline of Darjeeling and North East; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI K.H. MUNIYAPPA): (a) and ( b) National \nHighways Nos. 55, 31 and 31A are kept in traffic worthy conditions for smooth running \nof the traffic under different development and maintenance programmes undertaken \nfrom time to time depending upon availability of fund and inter se priority. \n(c) and (d) Four/Six laning of National Highway No. 31 from Pumea in Bihar to \nSiliguri in West Bengal and Bijni in Assam to Jalukabari in Assam is in progress as part \nof East-West Corridor. From Siliguri to Bijni, an alternative alignment passing through \nMainaguri, Dhupguri, Falakata, Sonapur and joining the original alignment near \nSalsalabari oh NH -31C in West Bengal is identified as part of East -West Corridor and \nits DPR is in progress. Four laning of the stretch of NH -31 from Bakhtiarpur to Purnea \nin Bihar is also identified under NHDP Phase -Ill. Similarly, improvement of National \nHighway No. 31A is included in Phase -A of Special Accelerated Road Development \nProject for North-Eastern Region (SARDP-NE). \nAccidents of old supply vessels \n1956. DR. M.A.M. RAMA SWAMY: Will the Minister of SHIPPING, ROAD \nTRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS be pleased to state:     \n(a) whether there have been fatal accidents of supply vessels which are 25 years \nand above old; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether any enquiry has been ordered in such cases; \n(d) whether it is a  fact that old vessels are being used as supply vessels by ONGC \nwhich are highly vulnerable to storms; and \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n \n\n170   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(e) if so,  the steps proposed to be taken by Central Government regarding safety of \ncrew members and increasing crew insurance of such boats? \nTHE MINISTER OF SHIPPING, ROAD TRANSPORT AND HIGHWAYS (SHRI \nT.R. BAALU): (a) to (e) Although there have been no fatal accidents of supply vessels \nwhich are 25 years and above old, two shipping casualties have occurred viz.; \nSamudrika-10, flag India aged 21 years which sank, off Mumbai on 9th July 2007 and \nSamudra Suraksha, flag India aged 24 years which sank of Mumbai on 2nd August \n2005. In the case of Samudrika -10, nine crew members were rescued, six lost their lives \nand two are still missing. Regarding Samudra Suraksha casualty occurred when the \nvessel hit Mumbai High North production platform. This resulted in the total loss of the \nplatform. Out of the total of 383 personnel on the platform, 361 were rescued 10 died \nand 12 are missing. Out of a crew of 84 belonging to Samudra Suraksha, one person \ndied. In both the cases, the Directorate General of Shipping have ordered a preli minary \ninquiry by Principal Officer, Mercantile Department, Mumbai to investigate the \ncircumstances leading to these casualties. In case of \"Samudra Suraksha\", a formal \ninvestigation to be conducted Judicial Magistrate has also been ordered. As per records  \nmaintained by the Directorate General of Shipping on the registration of ships, the \naverage age of Indian registered offshore supply vessels operating in the offshore areas \nis about 22 years. They are issued with statutory certificates with respect to saf ety, \nconstruction of ships, machinery and equipments for undertaking safe voyages during all \nweather conditions that include stormy seas and adverse weather conditions. They \nbecome vulnerable to storms if not maintained by the ship owner during the currenc y of \nvalidity of statutory certificates. The crew are engaged under the Articles of agreement \nas per the provisions of the Indian Merchant Shipping Act, 1958. The welfare and \nsecurity measures including insurance are addressed in the collective Bargaining \nAgreement entered into by ship owners and Seafarers' trade unions. However, to \nimprove crew safety, structural examination and enhanced Flag State Inspection of all \nIndian offshore supply vessels of more than 15 years of age has been put in place.  \nSending Indian into Space \n1957.   SHRI VIJAY J. DARDA: \nSHRI GIREESH KUMAR SANGHI: \nWill the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to state: \n(a) whether ISRO proposes to launch an Indian into Space; \n(b) if so, whether ISRO is developing its indigenous technology for this historic \nevent, or already proven technology in this field available with advanced countries \nwould be utilized; \n(c) the likely expenditure on this project; and \n(d) the timeframe for hoisting an Indian into Space and the perspective planning of \nsuch other projects? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE PRIME MINISTER'S OFFICE (SHRI \nPRITHVIRAJ CHAVAN): (a) No, Sir. However, preliminary studies are being \nundertaken towards, technology development. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions   171 \n(b) Does not arise. \n(c) and (d) Not applicable. \nVillage Resource Centres \ntl958. SHRIMATI SUSHMA SWARAJ: Will the PRIME MINISTER be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that a plan has been formulated to set up Village Resource \nCentres (VRC) in the villages of each block of the country with the  help of Indian \nSpace Research Organisation (ISRO); \n(b) if so, by when this plan would be implemented; and \n(c) the details of the areas about which information would be provided by the \nVillage Resource Centres at the block level? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE PRIME  MINISTER'S OFFICE (SHRI \nPRITHVIRAJ CHAVAN): (a) and (b) No, Sir. However, Village Resource Centres are \nbeing set up in association with the partner agencies (such as NGOs, Trusts, \nGovernment agencies) in selected large villages of the Country depending on  the \ndevelopmental needs. So far 276 VRCs have been set up by ISRO in 14 States.  \n(c) Village Resource Centres provide space based information and services in the \nareas of education, awareness creation, healthcare, vocational training and skill \ndevelopment, agriculture, land and water resources management, livestock \nmanagement, fisheries, weather and other Information and Communication Technology \n(1CT) enabled services. \nProjects under MPLADS \n1959. SHRI MATILAL SARKAR: Will the Minister of STATISTICS AND \nPROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION be pleased to state: \n(a) the number of ongoing projects under MPLAD schemes all over the country;  \n(b) how many of them relate to 'Tsunami' reconstruction works; \n(c) whether it is a fact that many important projects cannot be taken up on account \nof restriction in the guidelines; and \n(d) whether the guidelines are relaxable according to suitability of the States? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE OF THE MINISTRY OF STATISTICS AND \nPROGRAMME IMPLEMENTATION (SHRI G.K. VASAN): (a) Based on the Monthly \nProgress Reports r eceived from District Authorities till 20.8.2007, the number of \nongoing works under MPLAD Scheme is 100961. \n(b) There are 74 ongoing works relating to 'Tsunami' reconstruction works.  \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n172   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(c) The works under MPLAD Scheme are taken up as per the Guidelines on \nMPLADS. Annexure -II of the Guidelines contains the list of prohibited works under \nMPLADS. \n(d) MPLADS Guidelines are uniform for all States. \nCPWD Engineers joining private companies \n†1960. SHRI KALRAJ MISHRA: Will the MINISTER OF URBAN \nDEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that many engineers of CPWD are leaving their Government \njobs and joining private companies where they are getting more salaries; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the number of engineers who have taken VRS or \nresigned from CPWD in the last two years; \n(c) whether Government would take any steps to stop this trend; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MIN ISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI AJAY MAKEN): (a) to (d) The information is being collected and will be laid on \nthe Table of the Sabha. \nWorld Bank assistance for towns \n1961. SHRI TARIQ ANWAR: Will the MINISTER OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \nbe pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that World Bank is providing assistance for development  \nof towns in the country; \n(b) if so, the details of the projects launched thereunder in Bihar and Maharashtra; \nand \n(c) the amounts of funds allocated during the last three years in Bihar a nd \nMaharashtra? \nTHE MINISTER OF 