 underground and 5.7 km \nelevated). Estimated completion cost Rs. \n5068 cr. \napproval. \nRegularisation of unauthorised constructions \n1963. SHRI BASHISTHA NARAIN SINGH: Will the MINISTER OF UR BAN \nDEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government through a Public Notice dated 12th August, 2007 have \nallowed regularization of unauthorized construction under the Master Plan 2021 upto \nthe second floor for constructions in Delhi; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the Master Plan 2021, allows regularisation of the thirdfloor also; \n(d) if so, the provisions in the Master Plan in this regard; \n(e) the reasons for the third floor not being regularised; and \n(f) the steps Government propose to take to rectify th is anomaly at the earliest and \nbring relief of lakhs of third floor owners allover Delhi? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI AJAY MAKEN): (a) and (b) The Municipal Corporation of Delhi (MCD), \nthrough a public notice dated 12th August, 2007 has allowed regularisation of \n\nWritten Answers to 30 AUG. 2007] Unstarred Questions   111 \nindividual floors (upto second floor and including basement) as per the permissible \nnorms under the Master Plan for Delhi (MPD)-2021 on payment of prescribed fee. \n(c) to (f) MPD -2021, notified on 7.2.2007, inter alia, prescribes the Development \nControl Norms for buildings in residential plotted housing on different size of plots in \nrespect of maximum ground coverage, FAR and maximum height of the building  as 15 \nmetres. The permissible ground coverage, FAR, and maximum height restriction of 15 \nmetres, subject to fulfilment of other terms and conditions, determines the number of \nfloors which could be constructed on a given size of plot. \nWithin these norms pr escribed in MPD 2021, plot owners/allottees seeking \nregularization of construction in terms of additional coverage/additional height are \nrequired to pay a penalty, compounding charges and betterment levy or additional FAR \ncharges as well as special compoun ding charges at the rates notified by Government for \nthis purpose. However, this is subject to orders of the Supreme Court on the matter. The \nSupreme Court in W.P. No. 4677 of 1985 in the case of M.C. Mehta vs. Union of India \nand others vide its Order date d 7.5.2007 directed MCD not to permit construction of \nadditional floor. Subsequently, Supreme Court vide its order dated 23.7.2007, modified \nits earlier order dated 7.5.2001 clarifying that the prohibition vide orders dated 7.5.2007 \nrelated to sanctions th at were being granted and not intended to cover cases where \nconstructions were permissible on the basis of the 1990 (2001) Master plan as modified \nby the modifications in 1998. MPD 2021 is under consideration of Supreme Court. \nLand to schools on concessional rates \n1964. SHRI JAI PARKASH AGGARWAL: Will the MINISTER OF URBAN \nDEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of defaulter schools that had been allotted land by Delhi  \nDevelopment Authority on the condition of free education but have not fulfilled  \nthe same; \n(b) whether the Delhi Development Authority has taken any action against such \ndefaulter schools which have violated this condition and which were sanctioned land on \nconcessional rates on the basis of social purpose; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI AJAY MAKEN): (a) to (c) Delhi Development Authority (DDA) has reported \nthat the Government of National Capital Territory of Delhi (GNCTD) (Education \nDepartment) forwarded to DDA a list of 93 de faulter schools who did not comply with \nthe terms of allotment of providing freeship seats to the children belonging to \neconomically weaker sections. DDA issued show cause notices to all these defaulters.  \nA list of defaulter schools was again forwarded by GNCTD to the DDA for the \nacademic session 2005 -06. In the meanwhile, the matter was seized of by the High \nCourt of Delhi in a Civil Writ. Petition No. 3156/2002 and therefore no notices were \nissued by DDA to these schools. \nIn view, of the above Court case,  no list of defaulter schools was forwarded by \nGNCTD to DDA after 2005-2006 academic session. Delhi High Court in the above \n\n178   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nWrit Petition has been issuing directions to GNCTD from time to time regarding the \npolicy of freeship condition and its compliance. \nMinimum wages paid by CPWD \n1965., SHRI PRAVEEN RASHTRAPAL: Will the MINISTER OF URBAN \nDEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) the amount of minimum wage paid to unskilled workers in urban areas and  \nunskilled workers in rural areas, State-wise; \n(b) the amount of minimum wages fixed by CPWD on various projects  \nundertaken by Government; \n(c) the reasons for large variation in the amount of CPWD minimum wage and \nwages fixed by various State Governments; and \n(d) whether there is any move by the Central Government to rationalize wage \nstructure keeping in view the price structure of essential commodities? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI AJAY MAKEN): (a) to (d) Information is being coll ected and will be laid on the \nTable of the Sabha. \nRepair of balconies in Government flats in Delhi \ntl966. SHRI VINAY KATIYAR: Will the MINISTER OF URBAN \nDEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are aware that balconies of Government flats in Del hi are \nin shabby condition; \n(b) the number of such balconies especially in Netaji Nagar E -Block which require \nimmediate repair; and \n(c) the reasons therefor and the action being proposed in this regard? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI AJAY MAKEN): (a) The balconies of Government flats in Delhi can benefit \nfrom aesthetic improvement, repair and retrofitting. \n(b) and (c) 105 balconies in Netaji Nagar E -Block require repairs. The work relating \nto repair, retrofitting and aesthetic impr ovements in Government colonies is an ongoing \nprocess and is being done in a phased manner subject to availability of funds. \nFinancial assistance for ring roads in State Capitals \n1967. SHRI RAMACHANDRAIAH: Will the MINISTER OF URBAN \nDEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether his Ministry is providing financial assistance to any of the State \nGovernments to construct ring roads in their Capital cities; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   179 \n(b) if so, whether the proposal of the State Government of Andhra Pradesh seeking \n50 per cent cost for development of second ring road (outer ring road) project has been \napproved; \n(c) if so, the details of the sanctions accorded thereto; and \n(d) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI AJAY MAKEN): (a) to (d) No, Sir. No proposal for ring roads has been \nsubmitted by any of the State Governments including Government of Andhra Pradesh \nseeking Additional Ce ntral Assistance under Urban Infrastructure and Governance \n(UIG) component of Jawaharlal Nehru National Urban Renewal Mission (JNNURM).  \nHowever, a revised concept Note for the Outer Ring Road Project of Hyderabad, \ncosting Rs.2700 crore, with Japanese Bank for International Cooperation (JBIC) \nassistance was received from the Government of Andhra Pradesh and has been \nrecommended to Department of Economic Affairs on 6.3.2007. \nComplaints received by CPWD enquiry, Sarojini Nagar \n1968. DR. M.A.M. RAMASWAMY: Will the MINISTER OF URBAN \nDEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) the total number of complaints received by the \"GI\" Block, CPWD Enquiry, \nSarojini Nagar, New Delhi during the last three years, year-wise; \n(b) the number of complaints which remained unattended for more th an 15 days out \nof the above complaints; and \n(c) the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI AJAY MAKEN): (a) Total number of complaints received by \"GI\" Block, \nCPWD Enquiry, Sarojini Nagar during the last three years is 34657. The year -wise \nbreak up is as under:  \nPeriod No. of Complaints \n21.8.06 to 20.8.07. 11967 \n21.8.05 to 20.8.06 10223 \n21.8.04 to 20.8.05 12467 \n(b) and (c) Eight complaints remain unattended for more than 15 days out of the \nabove complaints on reas ons such as non availability of the allottee, non availability of \nthe glass sheet, and request from allottee etc. \n\n180   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nInadequate water supply in Delhi \n1969.   SHRI DHARAM PAL SABHARWAL: \nSHRI GIREESH KUMAR SANGHI: \nWill the MINISTER OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government are aware that there is inadequate water supply in NCT of \nDelhi; \n(b) whether Government have decided to identify the ways for additional water \nproduction, treatment, distribution, storage and pumping facilitation in the Capital; and \n(c) if so, the complete details in this regard? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI AJAY MAKEN): (a) to (c) Delhi Jal Board is constantly identifying the ways for \nadditional water production, treatment, distribution, storage and pumping facilitation in \nthe capital. A statement Showing details is enclosed. \nStatement \nStatus of water supply in Delhi \nSituation at Present (2007) \nAvailability - 775 MGD \nScenario for year 2011 \nAvailability - 940 MGD \nWays for addition production, treatment, distribution, storage and pumping \nfacilitation in the capital. \nAddition water production \n(A) Short Term Measures (by year 2011) \n1.  Savings from carrier lined channel : 80 MGD \n2  Recycling back wash water at Water Treatment : 45 MGD \nPlants \n3. Additional BBMB water for Nangloi WTP : 20 MGD \n4. Full utilization of Sonia Vihar WTP : 20 MGD \n165 MGD \n(B)     Long Term Measures (by year 2021) \nI. In the long term DJB has envisaged the following water storage  projects for \naugmentation of raw water: \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   181  \nProject                                     State Storage water for Delhi \nRenuka Dam                  Himachal Pradesh 275 MGD \nKishau Dam                        Uttaranchal 372 MGD \nLakhvar Vyasi Dam             Uttaranchal 135 MGD \n2.  Possibility of underground water extraction from \nMeerut (U.P.) Gangi Basin 100 MGD \n100 MGD \n3.  Additional share from Ravi-Beas water 140 MGD \nTreatment \nDelhi Jal Board is executing/has planned following water treatment plants for \naugmentation of treatment capacity:-  \nWTP 2011 2021 \nDwarka 50 40 (additional) \nOkhla 20  \nRecycling Plants 45  \nDistribution, Storage and Pumping Capacity \nDelhi Jal Board is aiming to achieve rational water distribution goal by the year 2011. \n1. 53 new service reservations (UGRs) are proposed to be constructed in  \naddition to 59 already available. \n2. Each UGR to serve a distinct command area for specific hours at designated  \nflow and pressures. \nDDA works in Dvarka \n1970. SHRI PYARELAL KHANDELWAL: Will the MINISTER OF URBAN \nDEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether DDA has received any complaint regarding resurfacing, repair and \nmaintenance work of street roads and cleaning of drains lying inside pocket premises \nparticularly in pocket-7, sector-12, Dwarka; \n(b) if so, the details of action taken so far; and \n(c) if not, the action plan to improve the condition of these two basic services? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF DEVELOPMENT (SHRI \nAJAY MAKEN): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) and (c) For repair work of road, tenders have been called by the DDA. For cleaning of \ndrains, DDA has provided funds to MCD. \n\n182   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nWater crisis in Vasant Vihar, CPWD colony \n1971. SHRI KAM AL AKHTAR: Will the MINISTER OF URBAN  \nDEVELOPMENT be pleased to refer to the answer to Unstarred Question 3016  \ngiven in the Rajya Sabha on 24 August, 2006 and state: \n(a) whether Government are aware that CPWD colony, Vasant Vihar is still facing a \nsevere water crisis; \n(b) whether Government are also aware that this Government colony has since \nmany years been getting meagre amount of water only in the morning whereas other \nadjacent Government colonies like R.K. Puram, Moti Bagh, Sarojini Nagar, etc. are \ngetting water twice a day; \n(c) if so, action taken by Government to increase the supply of water to this colony, \nat least providing water for one hour in morning and evening; and \n(d) the reasons for the supply of water to this colony not being increased despite the \ncommissioning of Sonia Vihar treatment plant? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT  \n(SHRI AJAY MAKEN): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir.  \n(c) Delhi Jal Board (DJB) has informed that to distribute supply from Sonia  \nVihar Treatment Plant in South Delhi areas as per plan scheme, a Master Balancing  \nReservoir (MBR) of 4.8 million gallons capacity and necessary infrastructure \ncomprising of Under Ground Reservoirs/Booster Pumping Stations and the  \nperipheral mains is under construction. DJB has also taken up the mat ter of raw  \nwater supply without any disruption with the State of Haryana. \nTo mitigate the immediate difficulty faced by the residents, an average of 125 to 250 \nKL of water per day is being supplied by CPWD through tankers. DJB authorities are \nalso being regularly contacted by CPWD to increase the water supply. \n(d) The supply position to this colony can improve when MBR is fully  \ncommissioned by March, 2008. \nDevelopment of land as park by DDA \n1972. SHRI KAMAL AKHTAR: Will the MINISTER OF URBAN  \nDEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) the 'land use' of the piece of DDA land surrounded by DDA MIG green flats, \nRajouri Garden and road of, WZ -I06, Rajouri Garden Extension which generally forms \npart of a household garbage dumping ground and creates a lot of health hazard to  nearby \nresidents; \n(b) whether Government have proposed to develop the said land as a park so that the \nresidents of WZ-106 and DDA flats could benefit from it; and \n(c) if so, the details thereof and if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINIST RY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT \n(SHRI AJAY MAKEN): (a) to (c) Delhi Development Authority (DDA) has informed  \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   183 \nthat the land use of the piece of land surrounded by DDA MIG green flats, Rajouri Garden and \nroad of WZ-I06, Rajouri Garden Extension is earmarked for Senior Secondary School to be \nallotted to Education Department and hence the land cannot be developed as a park.  \nRegularization of unauthorised constructions \n1973. SHRI V. NARAYANASAMY: will the MINI STER OF URBAN  \nDEVELOPMENT be. pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the State Government of Delhi are urging upon his  \nMinistry to decide on the issue pertaining to regularization of unauthorized  \ncolonies in Delhi; and \n(b) if so, the steps Delhi G overnment have taken in consultation with his  \nMinistry for regularizing the unauthorised colonies? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT (SHRI \nAJAY MAKEN): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) The Government of National Capital, Territory of Delhi (GNCTD ) had invited \napplications from Societies/Associations of Unauthorized colonies existing as on 31.3.2002 \nseeking regularization and received 1432 applications. Further, 107 applications have been \nreceived by GNCTD in response to a later advertisement. GNCTD has informed that \nprocessing of these applications has been taken up. \nFlats for slum dwellers in Delhi \n1974. SHRI V. NARAYANASAMY: will the MINISTER OF URBAN \nDEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that DDA has decided to construct high ris e buildings for \njhuggi dwellers for their rehabilitation and accommodation; and \n(b) if so, the total number of flats Government are planning to construct for the \naccommodation to the slum people in Delhi every year? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF URBAN DEVELOPMENT (SHRI \nAJAY MAKEN): (a) and (b) The Master Plan for Delhi-2021 provides for Multi-storeyed in-\nsitu up-gradation of the land pockets of slum and JJ clusters, which are not required for \npublic/priority use, as the first option for provision of affordable housing for rehabilitation \nof squatters. \nFinancial assistance to States for development of sports \n1975. SHRI K. CHANDRAN PILLAI: Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS  \nAND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether the Centre is providing financial assistance to the States for the development \nof sports; \n(b) if so, the details of the schemes with Central assistance which are being implemented \nin States; \n\n184   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(c) whether Government of Kerala has submitted a propo sal \"Operation Olympia\" \nfor the development of sports in the State; \n(d) if so, the details thereof; \n(e) the details of the funds so far sanctioned to the project; and \n(0 if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS (SHRI MANI SHANKAR \nAIYAR): (a) and (b) The Ministry is implementing various schemes for the \ndevelopment of sports and games in the States. Under the Rural Sports Programme \ncompetitions and tournaments are organized at the block, district and national levels and \nfinancial ass istance is provided to States/UTs for the purpose as per the following \npattern: \nBlock Level: Nil \nDistrict Level: Rs.30,000/-  per discipline per district for 10 sports disciplines \n(maximum Rs.3.00 lac per district) \nState Level: Rs.50,000/- per discipline upto 10 sports disciplines \nNational Level: Rs.2.50 lakh per discipline upto 10 sports disciplines. \nA Sports Festival for the North Eastern Region is also organized every year in one \nof the North Eastern States by rotation and participation is restricted only  to residents of \nNorth Eastern States. Assistance of Rs.50,000/ - per district for District Level \nCompetitions and Rs.75,000/- per discipline for State Level Competition is given. \nApart from this, Ministry is also implementing the Sports Scholarship Scheme with a \nview to assisting talented boys and girls for their outstanding performance. The Scheme \naims at assisting sports persons so that they can have nutritious diet, sports equipment \nsupport and be able to pursue competitive sports. The scholarship fall u nder the \nfollowing three categories: \n(i)       State Level Scholarship (Category-I) : @ Rs.550/- per month for 150 \nSportspersons per State and 100 sportspersons per Union Territory. \n(ii)      National Level Scholarship (Category -II) : @ Rs.700/- per month to all \neligible sportspersons. \n(iii)     University/College level Scholarship (Category-Ill):  @ Rs.850/-per \nmonth to all eligible Universities/College students. \nThe eligibility for the team and individual events is as under:- \nTeam events: Winners and run ners up (all members) and outstanding individual \nperformers, If any, of the teams that do not make it to the finals. \nIndividual events:     First, Second and Third position holders. \nWith an aim to promote sports amongst women under the scheme of National S ports \nChampionships for Women, financial assistance is provided to the State/UT \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   185 \nfor conducting block, district and State/UT level competitions and participation at \nnational level. \n(c) to (f) The Government of Kerala have submitted a memorandum to the Ministry \nseeking financial assistance for be development of sports in Kerala, including the \nrevival of \"Operation Olympia\", which the State government had launched on a pilot \nbasis in 199- 1 to identif y talented children in the age group of 10 -12 years and to give \nthem training for 4 years at the Panchayat Centre, 2 years at the District Centre and 4 \nyears intensive training at the State Centre. The Pilot Programme was implemented up \nto 1996. The Government of India does not fund State -specific schemes, but runs \ndifferent schemes for the promotion of excellence in sports. The Sports Authority of \nIndia (SAI) provides intensive training to talented sportspersons in the age group of 14-\n21 years under its fl agship schemes: SAI Training Centres and Special Area Games. \nApart from this, the National Sports Federations are given assistance for national -\ncoaching camps in accordance with their approved Long Term Development Plan. The \nMinistry also funds outstanding sportspersons directly for both training and competition \nexposures under the Talent Search and Training Programme and the National Sports \nDevelopment Fund. \nBuilding of sports complex by SAI \n1976. SHRI RAJEEV CHANDRASEKHAR Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS \nAND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government of Maharashtra have provided land to Sports Authority \nof India (SAI) at Kandivili to build a sports complex there; \n(b) whether it is a fact that the complex has not so far been completed  \naccording to  the stipulations and the State Government wants the SAI to return the  \nland to it; \n(c) if so, the reasons for tardy action by SAI in the matter during the last 17 years; \nand \n(d) the present status of the case and the action taken/proposed to be taken on State \nGovernment's request to return the land to them? \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS (SHRI MANI SHANKAR \nAIYAR): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) and (c) During the last 17 years, the Sports Authority of India (SAI) has \ndeveloped the area of the Centre at Kandivili and added a number of facilities such as a \nTable Tennis Hall, a Squash Court and a Sauna/Steam Bath and Flood -lit the Kabaddi \nGround, Basketball Cemented Court, a Handball Ground as well as provided a Fitness \nCentre, a Judo/Karate HallWork is progressing on the  Synthetic Hockey Surface and \nSynthetic Athletic Track, and is likely to be completed in next six months. \nThe State Government wish to resume the premises to develop their own divisional \nSports Complex and International Conventional Centre. \n(d) The decisio n of the State Government to take back the land was challenged by \nSAI in the Hon'ble High Court of Mumbai and the Hon'ble Court in its \n\n186   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \njudgement has prohibited closure of the SAI Training Centre (STC) and directed SAI to \ninvest in the sports infrastructure. \nRole of 'National sports confederations' \n†1977. SHRI AMIR ALAM KHAN: Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND \nSPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a) the role being played by various 'National Sports Confeder ations' to promote \nsports in the country; \n(b) the details of achievements made by these National Sports Confederations \nduring past years; \n(c) the manner by which these confederations are being controlled by Government; \nand \n(d) the amount of grant sanctioned to these confederations during last year? \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS (SHRI MANI SHANKAR \nAIYAR): (a) and (b) The National Sports Federations (NSFS) are responsible and \naccountable for the overall management, direction, control, regulation, promotion \ndevelopment and sponsorship of various sports disciplines, The recognized NSFs \nundertake various programmes for promotion of their respective sport such as spotting \nof talent, holding of national championships for Sub -Junior, Junior and Senior \nCategories, organization of international sports events in the country, selection of teams \nfor participation in international sports events, training/coaching of national teams, \nprocurement of equipment required for training etc. The NSFs in coordination with the \nSports Authority of India (SAI) and the Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports are \nundertaking various programmes as per agreed Long Term Development Plans (LTDPs) \nfor the promotion, of the sports disciplines concerned. As a result of these efforts, the \nperformance of Indian sportspersons has shown some improvement at the international \nlevel, in recent years. Details of the achievements of Indian sportspersons at major \nmulti-disciplinary sports events held in recent past are given below:  \nSI. Name of the Event Host \nCountry \n Medals Won  \nNo. Gold Silver Bronze Total \n1 2 3 4 5 6 7 \nI. Asian Games, 2002 Busan, Korea 11 12 13 36 \n2. Commonwealth \nGames, 2002 \nManchester, \nUK \n30 22 17 69 \n3. Afro-Asian Games, Hyderabad 19 32 29 80 \n 2003 (India)     \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   187  \n1         2 3 4 5            6          7 \n4. South Asian \nFederation, Games, \n2004 \nIslamabad, \nPakistan \n10 59 31 191 \n5. Olympic Games, \n2004, Athens \nAthens, \nGreece \n00 01 00 01 \n6. Commonwealth \nGames, 2006 \nMelbourne, \nAustralia \n22 17 11 50* \n7. SAF Games, 2006 Colombo, \nSri Lanka \n118 69 47 234 \n8. ASIAN GAMES \n2006 \nDoha 10 18 26 54 \n*Note: Judo and Wrestling were not included as an event and Weightlifting had only \none medal for each weight category thereby reducing the number of medals available as \ncompared to the Commonwealth Games 2002. \nApart from above the disciplines of Athletics, Archery, Rowing, Table Tennis, \nTennis, Yachting, Chess, Billiards & Snooker, Weight lifting, Badminton etc. have \nshown marked improvement in the performance at the international level and this has \nbeen possible as a consequence of the efforts of NSFs concerned backed by the requisite \nsupport of the Ministry and SAL \n(c) to ensure that the NSFs maintain certain basic standards and norms with  \nregard to their internal functioning, the Government have prescribed guidelines  \nregarding financial accountability, fair and transparent elections and the fair  \nselection of the national teams. \nIn order t o ensure transparency in selection processes, the Ministry has appointed \nGovernment Observers in each discipline to oversee the selection process and submit \nreports to the Government. Assistance is released only after the accounts and utilization \ncertificates of the assistance given, if any, for the previous event are submitted. As per \nthe provisions of the General Financial Rules, the Ministry also issues necessary \npermission to CAG to conduct the audit of the NSFs receiving financial assistance of \nmore th an Rupees one crore in a financial year. The guidelines under the scheme of \n\"Assistance to National Sports Federations\" also provide for action by way of \nsuspension/de-recognition in the event of irregularities such as unfair elections, \nmisutilization of funds, functioning against the interests of sports etc. \n(d) A statement showing financial assistance given to the NSFs during the last  \nyear is attached herewith. \nStatement \n Details of financial assistance given to the NSFs during the last year \n(Rs. in lakhs)  \nSI. No. Name of the Federation 2006-2007 \n1 2 3 \n1. All India Carrom Federation 12.69 \n\n188   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions  \n1 \n \n2 3 \n2 All India Chess Federation 112.46 \n3. All India Karate-Do-Federation 3.46 \n4. All India Sports Council of the Deaf 19.15 \n5. Amateur Baseball Federation of India 15.49 \n6. Amateur Handball Federation of India 38.64 \n7. Atya Patya Federation of India 15.50 \n8. Ball Badminton Federation of India 0.00 \n9. Basketball Federation of India 36.31 \n10. Cycle Polo Federation of lndia 30.01 \n11. Fencing Association of India 45.70 \n12. Gymnastics Federation of India 66.47 \n13. Indian Body Building Fedn. 0.00 \n14. Indian Kayaking & Canoeing Association 21.23 \n15. Indian Polo Association 1.55 \n16. Indian Power lifting Federation 8.50 \n17. Judo Federation of India 48.62 \n18. Kho-kho Federation of India 2.00 \n19. Korfball Federation of India 12.50 \n20. All India Tennis Association 90.07 \n21. National Rifle Association of India 373.19 \n22. Netball Federation of India 9.50 \n23. Roller Skating Federation of India 0.00 \n24. Rowing Federation of India 9.75 \n25. Sepak Takraw Federation of India 13.00 \n26. Shooting Ball Federation of India 0.00 \n27. Softball Federation of India 12.00 \n28. Squash Racket Federation of India 21.94 \n29. Swimming Federation of India 13.59 \n30. Table Tennis Federation of India 178.75 \n31 Taekwondo Federation of India 0.00 \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   189  \n1 2 3 \n32 Tenni-Koit Federation of India 13.00 \n33 Tennis Ball Cricket Federation of India 11.50 \n34 Tug of War Federation of India 20.75 \n35 Volleyball Federation of India 58.17 \n36 Yachting Association of India 78.40 \n37 Wushu Association of India 13.50 \n38 Throwball Federation of India 0.00 \n39 Para Olympic Committee of India 33.34 \n40 Archery Association of India 96.48 \n41 Billiards and Snooker Federation of india 16.13 \n42 Indian Amateur Boxing Federation 81.79 \n43 Indian Hockey Federation. 92.09 \n44 Indian Women Hockey Federation , 111.64 \n45 Indian Amateur Kabaddi Federation .  13.95 \n46 Indian Weightlifting Federation 328 \n47 Athletics Federation of India 86.83 \n48 Badminton Association of India 117.65 \n49 Equestrian Federation of India 29.06 \n50 Football 30.55 \n51 Indian Golf Union 23.65 \n52 Wrestling Federation of India 32.58 \n53 Winter Games Federation of India (WGFI) 1.65 \n54 Women's Cricket Federation of india 1.00 \n55 Cycling Federation of India 1.00 \n56 Special Olympic Bharat 4.50 \n57 Indian Olympic Association 538.10 \n58 Sports Authority of India 500.00 \n TOTAL: 3222.66 \nNote: Rs. 150.00 lakhs was released in 2006-2007 as a one time grant to Bengal Olympic \nAssociation for organizing Indo-Bangla games. \n\n190   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \nQuota for sports persons \n1978. SHRI AMIR ALAM K HAN: Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND \nSPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have assessed the reasons for our miserable failure to \nproduce a single Olympic gold medalist inspite of having enormous human resource;  \n(b) whether Government are consi dering to make sports mandatory in schools and \nall educational institutions and earmarking certain quota for sports persons; and  \n(c) the reasons for not allowing the sports persons, who have represented the \ncountry to sport bodies? \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIR S AND SPORTS (SHRI MANI SHANKAR \nAIYAR): (a) Yes, Sir. India is yet to get a gold medal in any of the individual sport \nevents at the Olympics. Although we have a very large population with around 77 \ncrores coming within the below 35 years age group, hardly 5 crores have access to \nproper sports facilities. With a view to addressing this huge gap in the availability of \nsports infrastructure, especially at the grassroots level the Ministry has obtained the in -\nprinciple approval of the Planning Commission to lau nch a nation wide programme \nentitled the 'Panchayat Yuva Krida Aur Khel Abhiyan', which aims to provide all the \nvillage and block panchayats with basic sports infrastructure in a phased manner over a \nperiod of 10 yeats. \n(b) The Ministry has prepared a draft Co mprehensive National Sports Policy which, \ninter alia, lays special emphasis on the integration of sports with formal education at \nboth school and college level. As regards the sports quota in educational intitutions, a \nlarge number of higher educational in stitutions already have such reservations. Since \neducation is a concurrent subject, there is no uniform policy in this regard. \n(c) There is no restriction on having sportspersons on sports bodies. Rather, the \nGovernment of India scheme entitled \"Guidelines for  Assistance to National Sports \nFederations\" provides for the inclusion of prominent sportspersons as members of \nSports Federations. It also recommends that a certain minimum percentage, say 25% of \nthe members representing the Federation, should be sportspersons. \nSetting up of sports centres by SAI \ntl979. DR. PRABHA THAKUR: Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND \nSPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the policy formulated by Government to promote sports in the \ncountry; and \n(b) the number of sports centres set up by the Sports Authority of India (SAI) in \nvarious States of the country during the last three years and details of achievements \nthereof? \nTOriginal notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions   191 \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS (SHRI MANI SHANKAR \nAIYAR): (a) The Ministry has formulated a draft comprehensive National Sports \nPolicy-2007, which has been put on the Website to receive the comments and \nsuggestions of the general, public. The  Ministry is also separately holding interactive \nsessions with major stakeholders. The proposed policy falls under the rubric of three \nmajor segments: \n\"Sports for AH\" \n\"Excellence in Sports\" \n\"Constitutional, Legal and Institutional measures\" \nThe Poiicy also delves into the specifies of : \n(i) the integration of sports development, youth development and national \ndevelopment \n(ii)      Universalisation, of access to sports infrastructure. \n(iii) the integration of sports and physical education with formal education.  \n(iv) Special themes on the promotion of indigenous games, sports among \nwomen; and sports among disabled. \n(v) the adoption of pyramid approach for the promotion of excellence in sports, \ntalent identification, intensive training and competition exposure. \n(vi) coordination and convergence between Union Government, State \nGovernments, National Sports Federations, Indian Olympic Association \nand the Sports Authority of India. \n(v)      Appropriate constitutional measures and legal framework. \n(b) The Sports Auth ority of India (SA1) runs 126 National Sports Talent Contest \n(NSTC) Centres, 10 Special Area Games (SAG) Centres, 57 SAI, Training Centres \n(STC), 18 Army Boys Sports Company Scheme (ABSC) Centre and 10 Centre of \nExcellence (COX) in various States of the co untry where sportspersons are given \ntraining in different sports disciplines. During the last three years following centres \nunder SAI Sports Promotional Schemes have been established and are functional: -  \nSI. No. Centre Date of opening \n1 2 3 \n1. Special Area Games Tinsukia (Assam) August, 2006 \n2 Special Area Games, Kokrajhart (Assam) October, 2006 \n3. Special Area Games Mayilduthuria (Tamil Nadu) August, 2006 \n4. Centre of Excellence, Bhopal January, 2006 \n\n192   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions  \n1 2 3 \n5. Centre of Excellence, Sonepat January, 2006 \n6. Centre of Excellence, Kollam July, 2007 \n7. Centre af Excellence, Hissar July, 2007 \n8. SAI Training Centre, Sonepat July, 2007 \n9. SAI Training Centre, Ludhiana July, 2007 \nARMY BOYS SPORTS COMPANY (ABSC)  \n1. Mechanized Infantry Regimental Centre, \nAhmadnagar, Maharashtra. \nDecember, 2004 \n2. Army Sports Institute, Pune (Maharashtra) December, 2004 \n3. Ladakh Scouts Centre, Leh (J&K) December, 2004 \n4. Dogra Regimental Centre, Faizabad. May, 2005 \n5. Gorkha Rifles Regimental Centre, Lucknow (U.P.) May, 2005 \n6. Rajputana Rifle Regimental Centre, \nFatehgarh (U.P.) \nMay, 2005 \n7. RVC Centre and College Meerut (U.P.) May, 2005 \nDetails of achievement of the Centres during last three year are as under:-  \nYear  Gold   Silve\nr \n Bronze \n National International Nationa\nl \nInternational National International \n2004 -  - -  - - - \n2005 24  05 03  02 04 06 \n2006 27  06 09  02 14 - \nTOTAL: 51  11 12  04 18 06 \nSites for Commonwealth Games \n1980. SHRI T.T.V. DHINAKARAN: Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND \nSPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether site for Commonwealth Games to be held at New Delhi in 2010 has \nbeen decided \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the progress of preparation for the games? \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS (SHRI MANI SHANKAR \nAIYAR): (a) and (b) Yes, Sir. The XIX Commonwealth Games are scheduled to be \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG., 2007] Unstarred Questions   193 \nheld from the 3rd to 14th October, 2010 at Delhi. About 8000 sportspersons  are likely \nto participate in these Games. The details of the 17 competition sports disciplines \nfinalized for inclusion in these Games and the venues thereof are given in the Statement \n{See below). \n(c) A number of institutional arrangements have been made for ensuring proper \nplanning and timely preparation for the Commonwealth Games, 2010. These include a \nCore Group of Minister (GOM) under the Chairmanship of the Minister for Human \nResource Development for coordinating the various works related to the condu ct of the \nGames; an Apex Committee headed by Minister for Youth Affairs and Sports for \noverseeing and coordinating various matters related to CWG 2010; the Organizing \nCommittee (OC) headed by the President, Indian Olympic Association for the conduct \nof the  Games; and a Committee of Secretaries headed by Cabinet Secretary which is \nresponsible for monitoring the implementation of the decisions of the GOM and taking \ndecisions on other related matters. In addition to these arrangements at the Central level, \nthere is a sub -committee under the Chief Minister of Delhi to take decisions in respect \nof all matters pertaining to infrastructure and other preparations for the Games within \nthe jurisdiction of the Government of NCT of Delhi. The Lt. Governor, Delhi has bee n \nentrusted with the overall responsibility with specific reference to security, law and \norders and matter coming under DDA. \nThe locations for the various competitions/training venues, and the major non -\ncompetition venue, viz. International Broadcasting Ce ntre (IBC)/Main Press Centre \n(MPC), have been agreed to among the stakeholders. All the stakeholders have \nindicated their specific time lines with reference to key milestones, viz., finalization of \ndetailed design and estimates, commencement of tendering p rocess, award of work, \ncommencement of work, completion of work and handing over to OC. It is expected \nthat work on all the major sports venue infrastructure projects will commence between \nAugust-December, 2007 and the venues will be handed over to the OC by the end of \n2009. An Empowered Committee has been set up under the Chairmanship of Chief \nSecretary, GNCTD with representatives from all the concerned agencies/authorities to \nfacilitate speedy clearances in respect of the Games related projects. Another \nCommittee has been set up by under the Secretary, Ministry of Urban Development to \nfacilitate clearances pertaining to Delhi Urban Arts Commission (DUAC). Keeping in \nview the need for developing adequate tourist accommodation in the context of the \nCommonwealth Games, an inter -Ministerial Task Force has been set up under the \nMinistry of Tourism (MoT) for monitoring and coordinating further action in this regard \nby all the agencies concerned. A Committee for expediting the Central Government \nclearance in respect of Hotels coming up in Uttar Pradesh has also been set under the \nChairmanship of Secretary, Ministry of Youth Affairs and Sports. \nTo improve the performance of the Indian contingent in Commonwealth Games, \n2010, extensive discussions have been held with all the stakeholders and a programme \nfor providing training, foreign exposure, scientific backup, equipment etc. with an \nestimated cost of Rs.661.00 crore has been drawn up for the elite sportspersons, who \nwill represent India in the Commonwealth Games, 2010. \nGovernment has approved project proposals of Sports Authority of India for an \namount of Rs.1000 crores (+   10%) for upgradation/creation  of sports  venue  \n\n194   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \ninfrastructure; upgradatiop of Tenni s venue by All India Tennis Association at a cost of \nRs.30 crores (+ 15%) and expenditure budget of Organizing Committee on a revenue \nneutral basis of Rs.767 crores (± 15%) for 'Conduct of the Games'. The Expenditure \nFinance Committee has recommended the p roposal of the Delhi Development Authority \nfor Rs.325.00 crores, (± 25%) for the International Zone etc. in the Games Village \n(which is being developed on Public Private Partnership Mode) and sports \ninfrastructure. \nPlanning Commission has approved a propos al of the Government of the NCT of \nDelhi for an amount of Rs.770.00 crores. Planning Commission has also approved an \namount Rs.110.00 crores to the Government of Maharashtra for sports infrastructure for \nCommonwealth Youth Games, 2008 to be held in Pune, w hich is a sub -event of the \nmain Commonwealth Games, 2010. \nStatement \nDetails of competition sports/venues for Commonwealth Games, 2010  \nSI. \nNo. \nDisciplines Venue Status Agency \n1 2 3 4 5 \n1. Athletics J.N. Stadium Existing SAI \n2. Lawn Bowls J.N. Sports Complex New Outdoor \nGreens \nSAI \n3. Weight \nLifting \nJ.N. Sports Complex New Indoor \nAuditorium \nSAI \n4. Hockey Maj. Dhyan Chand \nNational Stadium \nExisting SAI \n5. Cycling Velodrome, I.G. Sports \nComplex \nExisting SAI \n6. Gymnastics I.G. Indoor Stadium Existing SAI \n7. Wrestling I.G. Sports Complex New Indoor Hall SAI \n8. Shooting Dr. Kami Singh Shooting \nRange 1000 yards Range at \nManesar (about 60 Km \nfrom Games Village) \nExisting SAI \n9. Netball Thyagraj Sports Complex New Indoor Hall Government \nof   NCT   of \nDelhi \n10. Boxing Talkatora Indoor Stadium Existing NDMC \n11. Badminton Siri Fort Sports Complex New Indoor Hall DDA \n12. Squash Siri Fort Sports Complex New Indoor Hall DDA \n13. Table Tennis Yamuna Sports Complex New Indoor Hall DDA \n14. Rugby Delhi * University New Outdoor \nGreens \nDU \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   195  \n1 2 3 4 5 \n15. Aquatics S.P. Mukherjee \nSwimming Pool \nExisting SAI \n16. Archery Yamuna Sports Complex Existing DDA \n17. Tennis R.K. Khanna Tennis \nComplex \nExisting AITA \nSAI \nGNCT of Delhi \nNDMC \nDDA \nDU \n    AITA \n                Sports Authority of India \nGovernment of National Capital Territory of Delhi \nNew Delhi Municipal Committee \nDelhi Development  Authority \n                 Delhi  University \n                    All India Tennis Association. \nAllocation of funds for training \n1981. SHRI RAVULA CHANDRA SEKAR REDDY: Will the Minister of YOUTH \nAFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that a meagre Rs.62 crore have been allocated for training \nathletes for Commonwealth Games; \n(b) if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefor; and \n(c) the steps Government are taking for sending teams abroad for training and \nparticipation in international events? \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS (SHRI MANI SHANKAR AIYAR): \n(a) No, Sir. Rs.62 crores is provided for assistance to National Sports Federations for various \nactivities, including training of athletes. Before the Commonwealth Games, a separate \nscheme of training of athletes has been worked out. \n(b) Does not arise. \n(c) After extensive discussions with National Sports Federations, a programme for \nproviding training, foreign exposure, scientific backup, equipment etc. of high standard has \nbeen drawn up for the elite athletes, who will represent India in the Commonwealth \nGames, 2010. Current estimates indicate an expenditure of Rs.661.00 crore for this training \nover four years (2007-08 to 2010-11), out of which expenditure on foreign exposure would \nbe substantial so that the elite athletes could get the best in terms of training and for eign \ncompetitions. \nBudget provision for Commonwealth Games, 2010 \n†l982. SHRI AJAY SINGH CHAUTALA: Will the Minister of YOUTH AFFAIRS AND \nSPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of the plan made by Government for developing projects, hotels, grounds etc. \nin NCR in view of Commonwealth Games, 2010 being organized in Delhi; \n† Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n196   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(b) the amount of budget provision which has been made by Government for  the \nCommonwealth Games, 2010; and \n(c) the details of this proposed budget together with the game -wise expenditure \nlikely to be incurred thereon? \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS (SHRI MANI SHANKAR \nAIYAR): (a) The details of competition and training ve nues finalized for each discipline \nalong with the agencies responsible for establishing the required infrastructure for the \nCommonwealth Games, 2010 is placed at Statement -I and II respectively (See below). \nThe Games Village near Akshardham Temple is being developed by Delhi \nDevelopment Authority on the public -private-partnership mode for housing all \nIndian/foreign accredited athletes, coaches, etc. For augmentation of hotel \naccommodation, Delhi Development Authority, Government of Uttar Pradesh, \nGovernment of Haryana etc. are taking necessary steps which is being monitored by the \nMinistry of Tourism. \n(b) and (c) Government has approved project proposals of Sports Authority of India \nfor an amount of Rs.1000 crores (± 10%) for upgradation/creation of sports v enue \ninfrastructure; upgradation of Tenuis venue by All India Tennis Association at a cost of \nRs.30 crores (+ 15%) and expenditure budget of Organizing Committee on a revenue \nneutral basis of Rs.767 crores (+ 15%) for 'Conduct of the Games'. The Expenditur e \nFinance Committee has recommended the proposal of the Delhi Development \nAuthority, for Rs.325.00 crores (+ 25%) for the International Zone etc. in the Games \nVillage (which is being developed on Public Private Partnership Mode) and sports \ninfrastructure. \nPlanning Commission has approved a proposal of the Government of the NCT of \nDelhi for an amount of Rs.770.00 crores. Planning Commission has also approved an \namount of Rs. 110.00 crores to the Government of Maharashtra for sports infrastructure \nfor Commonwealth Youth Games, 2008 to be held in Pune, which is a sub- event of the \nmain Commonwealth Games, 2010. \nIt is not possible to indicate the game -wise expenditure to be incurred on holding of \nthe Games as the Stadia etc. being constructed would be used for mu ltiple sports and the \ntraining and competition venues of some sports would be spread over different stadia.  \nStatement-I \nCompetition sports/venues for Commonwealth Games, 2010  \nSl. \nNo. \nDisciplines Venue Status Agency \n1 2 3 4 5 \n1. Athletics J.N. Stadium Existing SAI \n2. Lawn Bowls J.N. Sports Complex New Outdoor \nGreens \nSAI \n3. Weightlifting J.N. Sports Complex New Indoor \nAuditorium \nSAI \n\nWritten Answers to [30 AUG, 2007] Unstarred Questions   197  \n1 2 3 4 5 \n4. Hockey Maj. Dhyan Chand \nNational Stadium \nExisting SAI \n5. Cycling Velodrome, I.G. Sports \nComplex \nExisting SAI \n6. Gymnastics I.G. Indoor Stadium Existing SAI \n7. Wrestling I.G. Sports Complex New Indoor Hall SAI \n8. Shooting Dr. Kami Singh Shooting \nRange \nExisting SAI \n9. Netball Thyagraj Sports Complex New Indoor Hall Government \nof   NCT   of \nDelhi \n10. Boxing Talkatora Indoor Stadium Existing NDMC \n11. Badminton Siri Fort Sports Complex New Indoor Hall DDA \n12. Squash Siri Fort Sports Complex New Indoor Hall DDA \n13. Table Tennis Yarnuna Sports Complex New Indoor Hall DDA \n14. Rugby Delhi University New Outdoor \nGreens \nDU \n15. Aquatics S.R Mukherjee Swimming \nPool \nExisting SAI \n16. Archery Yamuna Sports Complex Existing DDA \n17. Tennis R.K. Khanna Tennis \nComplex \nExisting AITA \nSAI  -                   Sports Authority of India \nGNCT of D