DA \n17. Wrestling Games Village DDA \n  Delhi University DU \n  Ludlow Castle Hall GNCT of Delhi \nSAl - Sports Authority of India  \nDDA - Delhi Development \n \n \nDU - Delhi  University  \nGNCT of Delhi Government of National Capital Territory of Delhi \nJMI University                   \n \njamia Millia Islamia \n \n \nAITA - All India Tennis Association  \nDLTA  Delhi  Lawn Tennis \n \n \nNational Sports Development Fund \n1983. SHRI DWIJENDRA NATH SHARMAH: Will the Minister of YOUTH \nAFFAIRS AND SPORTS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government have set up any National Sports Development Fund for \nachieving excellent performance in sports and providing specialized training and \ncoaching to Indian sportsmen; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) the steps which are being taken by Government in this respect? \nTHE MINISTER OF YOUTH AFFAIRS AND SPORTS (SH RI MANI SHANKAR \nAIYAR): (a) to (c) The National Sports Development Fund (NSDF) was instituted by \nthe Government with a view to mobilizing resources from the Government as well as \nnon-Government sources, including the private/corporate sector and Non -Residential \nIndians, for the promotion of sports and games in the country. The Fund was created in \n1998 for the development of sports in the country, with an initial amount of Rs. 2.00 \ncrore and with the following objectives: \n(i) to administer and apply the mone ys of the Fund for promotion of sports in \ngeneral and specific sports disciplines and individual sportspersons in \nparticular for achieving excellence at the National and International level; \n(ii)      to  impart  specialized training  and coaching  in  relevant  sports disciplines \nto sportspersons, coaches and sports specialists; \n\n200   Written Answers to [RAJYA SABHA] Unstarred Questions \n(iii) to construct and maintain infrastructure, as may be required for promotion of \nexcellence in sports; \n(iv) to supply  equipment to Government and Non- Governmental Organizations \nand individuals with a view to promoting excellence in sports; \n(v) to identify problems and take up research and development studies for \nproviding support to excellence in sports; \n(vi) to promote international cooperation in particular, exchange which may \npromote the development of indigenous sport and human resources as also \nthe activities which are within the amount of Exchange Programme \nentered into between India and other countries; and \n(vii) to provide low interest or interest free loans for projects and activities \nrelated to any of the aforesaid objects. \nContributions to the NSDF are entitled to 100% exemption from the payment of \nincome tax. Moreover, a donor is permitted to indicate a project , along with specifics of \nthe location and other aspects of funding and also an agency for the execution of the \nproject while making donations to the Fund, subject to general policy guidelines and \nrules, if any, in this behalf. \nMoneys made available under NSDF can be provided as assistance for various \npurposes for the promotion of the sports, including coaching/training of high performing \nathletes and medal prospects for major international events. Details of assistance \nprovided to sportspersons from NSDF till date are given in the Statement. \nStatement \nDetails of assistance provided to Sportsperson from NSDF till date  \nSI. Name of the sportsperson                Purpose for which  Amount \nNo. assisted from NSDF                           assistance provided   \n1 2 3  4 \n 2001-2002    \n1. Shri Abhinav Bindra, Shooter           Training abroad Rs. 10,00,000 \n 2002-2003    \n2. Shri Abhinav Bindra, Shooter          Training abroad Rs. 5,00,000 \n3. Shri Anil Kumar, Athlete -do - Rs. 5,00,000 \n4. Ms. Bobby Aloysius, Athlete - do- Rs. 7,50,000 \n 2003-2004    \n5. Ms. Anju Bobby George, Athlete - do - Rs. 14,91,505 \n6. Lt. Col. Rajyavardhan Rathore, \nShooter \n- do - Rs. 78,23,496 \n7. Shri Abhinav Bindra, Shooter - do- Rs. 